noi_dung:
{align:justify|When the cause-and-effect books were first published, I was deeply moved to receive sincere feedback and shared experiences from Buddhist readers everywhere; many, though living overseas, were so stirred they could not sleep, calling my assistant at midnight to pour out their joy and happiness… A beautiful, educated woman professor (now in America), reading "The Flooding of Jinshan Temple" in "Karmic Retribution in Present Life, Vol. 3," was moved to practice accordingly—and thanks to it her ailing eyes (nearly bereft of light) had their vision fully restored. Young delinquents, after reading the cause-and-effect books, woke up, reformed, and turned into… sages.}
{align:justify|So, dear readers wishing to share your experiences, please contact the translator's representative at: 0938.422.977}
{align:justify|My heartfelt thanks to Chánh Đạo, a Buddhist I have never met face to face, who warmly gave his labor, sparing time to fix my computer—without him these works could never exist.}
{align:justify|Thanks to Viên An, ever ready to help, eagerly providing technical disks and warm guidance. Thanks to her I can design covers, lay out pages, and publish books myself…}
{align:justify|Thanks to út Chiến: when my computer burned out I could only sit fretting, but he came to the rescue in time, helping me stabilize its use.}
{align:justify|Thanks to someone I have never met, who, hearing I was ill, vowed to shorten his own life, praying I would recover and live long to keep translating and publishing…}
{align:justify|Thanks to Hồng Minh Châu, who sacrificed so much to support me.}
{align:justify|Thanks to one who helped me enormously yet insists on remaining anonymous.}
{align:justify|Thanks to Thanh Hải, who warmly helped design and polish the printed edition.}
{align:justify|Thanks to Diệu An and cô Nguyên Tâm, who never stopped encouraging and advising me toward more perfect content.}
{align:justify|Thanks to Liễu Như and the silent volunteer book-givers, who strove to spread the books everywhere hoping all may understand cause and effect.}
{align:justify|So each time a work is completed, I silently give thanks and dedicate this field of blessings to you all…}
{align:justify|May this "Cause and Effect Explained Through Buddhism" continue bringing you great benefit. My thanks for the open and silent support of all benefactors. I dedicate this translation's blessed field to the soul of young Carol Chi Phan and all beings in the Dharma realm. May all ever dwell in peace and happiness, noble in character, acting openly and uprightly, together building a beautiful human world.}
{align:justify|Lastly, please forgive this translator's errors.}
{align:justify|Hạnh Đoan – February 29, 2015}
{align:justify|PART 1}
{align:justify|JOURNEY TO THE WEST AND THE INVESTITURE OF THE GODS}
{align:justify|Master, hearing your analysis of the White Snake tale, I find it most reasonable. I have further questions to ask: Journey to the West is a famed classic beloved by the masses, filmed and cheered by so many…}
{align:justify|But watching the films as a child, I found Tang Sanzang so cowardly and weak… why did Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva make Sun Wukong take him as master? What is more, She gave him the Tightening-Circlet spell to tame a "punish-evil, praise-good" champion like the Great Sage? Tang Sanzang is no more gifted—how could he teach his disciples? How should this story be explained from the Buddhist view?}
{align:justify|Master Diệu Pháp answered:}
{align:justify|"I think, understanding Buddhism, Journey to the West should be explained thus:}
{align:justify|As the books state, Tang Sanzang himself followed the Lesser Vehicle—so Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva manifested as an old monk, pointing the way, advising Tang Sanzang to journey West to audience with the Buddha and request the Great Vehicle sutras.}
{align:justify|The road West for sutras is in truth the path Venerable Xuanzang walked—self-liberating and liberating others. But the practicing road is never smooth—always full of perils and hardships.}
{align:justify|The three disciples following Tang Sanzang represent the three evil habits—greed, anger, delusion—in every being. Zhu Bajie stands for those sunk in craving: craving wealth, beauty, fame, gain, food, sleep… hence he must keep eight precepts—meaning only precepts quench craving; only precepts win wisdom. Hence he is named Wuneng (Awakened Power).}
{align:justify|Sun Wukong stands for the dutiful and gifted, yet greatly wrathful! Naming him Wukong (Awakened Emptiness) tells such people to grasp the truth: "All is impermanent; all dharmas are empty."}
{align:justify|Sha Wujing denotes the honest, good-hearted yet deluded, who, not understanding Buddhism, created much evil karma (meat-eating, killing)—he once lived in the Flowing Sands River devouring humans. Naming him Wujing (Awakened Purity) bids him practice pure conduct: to escape the three realms, one must be compassionate and never kill.}
{align:justify|Tang Sanzang as master sees the four elements all empty, untempted by wealth, beauty, fame, gain; though less magically gifted than his disciples, yet at moments on life-and-death's edge he still takes it lightly, declaring:}
{align:justify|"Better step West and die than step back and live"—when Wukong broke precepts killing, disobeying his master, he had no choice but the Tightening-Circlet spell… meaning: use precepts to rein him in, turning him from deviance to right.}
{align:justify|Tang Sanzang cannot ride clouds or transform—only single-mindedly recite the Buddha's name—meaning: "Practice aims not at miracles."}
{align:justify|As for Wukong, vastly magical, meeting demons too mighty—was he not helpless too? Yet in the end evil never beats right!…}
{align:justify|Life imperiled, Tang Sanzang single-mindedly recites the Buddha's name, and Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva surely aids him—turning peril to safety, perils to peace. Besides, most demons Wukong cannot handle are attendants of saints or beasts of their households running wild…}
{align:justify|Explain it thus: Bodhisattvas and immortals, to help Tang Sanzang soon attain sainthood, deliberately contrived obstacles…}
{align:justify|All are trials and tests—watching how he responds? Tang Sanzang, even at death's door, never once blamed Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) for sending him fetching sutras!}
{align:justify|The Monkey King can ride clouds a hundred and eight thousand miles, yet never leaves the Tathagata Buddha's palm. If Buddhism wished to hand the Great Vehicle scriptures to Tang Sanzang, how easy—need the Buddha but lift a hand? Or simply send Sun Wukong fetching sutras, sparing the trouble?…}
{align:justify|So this Journey to the West warns us: practice is most arduous; in a Buddhist practicing community there are always all kinds of seekers. The Buddhist assembly is a great forge "gathering essences and storing all"…}
{align:justify|The Buddha's teachings are supremely hard—nothing can break them; the Buddha's teachings are supremely soft—nothing can divide them…. If all ever keep right mindfulness in practice, together they can build the great work. So through a thousand years' perils and dangers, at last master and three disciples meet the Buddha. But entering the "Sutra Repository" for scriptures, they meet obstacles—hinting: "Till Buddhahood, ignorance remains"—also explaining: "Let go, and you shall receive."}
{align:justify|On the return road, all scriptures fell into water—at last only six words remained: "Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!" In the "Diamond Sutra" the Buddha also said: "To say the Tathagata preached any Dharma is to slander the Buddha"—likewise hinting: in the latter Dharma age, all written scriptures shall gradually vanish—first the "Shurangama Sutra"… till at last only six words "Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!" remain.}
{align:justify|Truly, my explanation merely strives to "straighten the bent"—forced to do so.}
{align:justify|In fact, "Journey to the West" and "Investiture of the Gods" both arose from Buddhist-Daoist rivalry—born of mutual attacks.}
{align:justify|Recently, Venerable Hư Vân—the Zen North Star—explained these two works' origins thus:}
{align:justify|"In those years in Beijing, Venerable Bach Van preached the 'Dao De Jing' at Bach Van Temple; many Daoists came listening, then joined Buddhism becoming monks—enraging the Daoists at Truong Xuan Temple, who sued the officials…}
{align:justify|The court, to dissolve both sides' conflict, ordered Truong Xuan Temple (Daoist) renamed Truong Xuan Pagoda, while Bach Van Pagoda (Buddhist) must become Bach Van Temple.}
{align:justify|The Daoists, unconvinced, wrote the novel Journey to the West to libel Buddhism—so Journey to the West readers need wisdom and bright eyes; understanding that history, they see the truth at once.}
{align:justify|Most cunning: the novel pictures Tang Sanzang fetching sutras, returning to the Heaven-Reaching River—where all scriptures fell in the river, their words all lost—at last only six words "Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!" remaining. This is them saying: Venerable Xuanzang's whole sutra-translation enterprise was all false.}
{align:justify|Pity that later generations mistook and fanatically believed Journey to the West—burying, distorting, effacing Venerable Xuanzang's true westward journey…}
{align:justify|Answering Journey to the West, the monks "returned the salute"—writing the "Investiture of the Gods" to libel Daoists. Seen thus, everywhere in the tale… Daoists are mocked!}
{align:justify|For instance: Daoist immortals surely wield power—yet one by one fall under the immortal-slaying blade.}
{align:justify|Reading these two novels, not understanding that Buddhist-Daoist rivalry's history, readers mistake false for true!}
{align:justify|So reading books demands bright wisdom—clearly seeing right from wrong, well distinguishing deviant from true…}
{align:justify|Note: please read Venerable Hư Vân's teachings and instructions. Herein are only Master Diệu Pháp's explanations. He further urges us to study the "Shurangama Sutra" closely, to gain wisdom and bright eyes distinguishing true from deviant, right from wrong… to read many works by virtuous high monks themselves, opening our own wisdom.}
{align:justify|LAYMAN CHÂN MINH}
{align:justify|Layman Chân Minh's small two-story house had just been completed amid crackling firecrackers; Chân Minh and his wife joyfully led their eighty-year-old parents into the new home. That afternoon they hosted a small party (tea, fruit, cakes) for close neighbors come to celebrate. Their two-story building, the village's first storied house, drew special attention—above all because three years earlier the family ranked poorest in the village, too poor for even a kitchen. Their kitchen was patched from two ragged mats on four propping poles. Rains soaked the fire-stove (three chipped bricks)…}
{align:justify|The household—aged parents above, four children below—its whole burden fell on the daughter-in-law (Chân Minh's wife). So where was Chân Minh? Where shirking duty?…}
{align:justify|This tells from the reforms' start: Chân Minh handed fields to family management, himself directing a Packaging Company. At first, toiling hard, he managed. Then craving more money, bigger business, he borrowed two hundred thousand from the bank. Seeing his enterprise promising, the bank agreed.}
{align:justify|Funded, Chân Minh sought partners—soon these so-called "bosom friends" betrayed him, "pocketing" the whole sum and fleeing. Then Chân Minh could hardly stand—bank demanding debts, court summons delivered home… At wit's end, only "flight" remained—he hurried to shelter with his close friend Đan Lương in District H.}
{align:justify|Then Đan Lương was about to visit City T. seeking me—knowing I studied under a high master there—naturally Đan Lương seized the blessing, bringing along his ruined-director friend (soul adrift, Chân Minh) for me to lead both to audience with Master Diệu Pháp.}
{align:justify|Affinity truly passes thought: betrayed by friends fleeing with his money, Chân Minh thereby became Master Diệu Pháp's disciple.}
{align:justify|Returning to Đan Lương's house, Chân Minh dared not stay—hearing the procuratorate was sending men sniffing after him. So he hid in a remote land, dwelling in a wild hut, guarding the district. Đan Lương visited daily with food. No passersby here—especially clear and quiet. Eight straight months Chân Minh dwelled here; besides sleep and sitting meditation, he memorized and solely recited the Shurangama Mantra—living exactly like a monastic in sealed retreat: vegetarianism, Buddha-recitation, Shurangama Mantra recitation (guided and watched by Master Diệu Pháp). Nights, no electric light—not even an oil lamp lit.}
{align:justify|Practicing cross-legged sitting, past forty, thick-legged, big-bellied, folding his legs proved most hard; failing, he fretted greatly.}
{align:justify|One night, struggling to sit cross-legged, suddenly he saw a fat monk sitting opposite, teaching him how to sit… saying:}
{align:justify|"You are too fat—sit like me!"}
{align:justify|So he imitated the fat monk—right thigh atop, left leg folded below. Both hands face down, relaxed naturally on both thighs, eyes lightly closed…}
{align:justify|"Oh! Night has turned blue sky—all around vast, silent, soundless… where am I? Why can I no longer see my body? Yet thoughts remain—could… I be dead?"}
{align:justify|A little tense, Chân Minh hastily opened his eyes—dawn already graying. He plainly saw himself still sitting—but the fat monk nowhere… "Was I dreaming?"… Chân Minh looked down, checking—his legs still crossed exactly as the fat monk taught.}
{align:justify|"I was not dreaming—yet dawn has come?… Truly night had barely fallen when I began sitting—then the fat monk came teaching me to sit—only a moment?… But now dawn? Could… I… have sat all night? Yet my spirit is most alert, most well?"…}
{align:justify|That morning Chân Minh pondered endlessly, wondering on—till Đan Lương brought rice; he hastily told the night's tale. Đan Lương said:}
{align:justify|"This region has no monk like you describe!"}
{align:justify|Next noon Đan Lương brought rice, plus Buddha images for Chân Minh to view. One glance, and Chân Minh recognized the foremost seated Buddha, crying:}
{align:justify|"Right—exactly this monk's looks—last night 'he' taught me sitting just so."}
{align:justify|Đan Lương told Chân Minh:}
{align:justify|"This is Maitreya Bodhisattva—the future Buddha, also called Maitreya Buddha. Your practice is no small thing, moving Maitreya Buddha to teach you so… In future you shall surely attain."}
{align:justify|Chân Minh recalled: last night Maitreya Buddha called him Hang Van, saying it was his Dharma name… He asked his friend:}
{align:justify|"What is a Dharma name?"}
{align:justify|Đan Lương explained:}
{align:justify|"A Dharma name is given to refuge-taking disciples—perhaps in a former life you were a monastic, for you sit most well. Sooner or later you shall understand all!"}
{align:justify|Months after, whenever Chân Minh sat, all states appeared—here unwritable in full (but he never clung nor grew proud—ever mindful the "Shurangama Sutra" teaches: "Claiming sainthood, one surely strays deviant"…}
{align:justify|Eight months done, Đan Lương reported: a transport company director needs one able to dwell long-term in Shanxi managing coal distribution. Monthly wage one thousand—so Chân Minh hastened to take the job.}
{align:justify|This thousand, to him, was truly "sleepy meeting the mat"—luck for the afflicted—for he could aid his family with it.}
{align:justify|Chân Minh was a most filial son—eight months never filial to his parents a single coin. Had Master Diệu Pháp not urged him "let go of all conditions," devoting himself to memorizing the Shurangama Mantra, his heart would still brim with cares.}
{align:justify|In a blink three years passed—three years Chân Minh worked steadily—every three or four days dispatching trucks, checking machines, hauling goods, collecting freight—good coal quality sufficing.}
{align:justify|Besides three daily meals, all free time he sat "Maitreya-style," ever fluently reciting the Shurangama Mantra. Master Diệu Pháp required him daily to recite forty, fifty rounds. Nights he sat—hours at a sitting…}
{align:justify|One day the owner told Chân Minh that between him and the plant a conflict arose—they would call his haulage no more; Chân Minh should go settle accounts at the plant—then their contract ends.}
{align:justify|Unexpectedly, meeting Chân Minh, the plant owner wished to work with him—inviting him to keep supplying the plant. Chân Minh hastily said he had no capital… but the owner said: guarantee coal quality, and monthly they would pay his wage. Chân Minh sincerely wished to cooperate—the plant needed a trusted partner, and trusted him. Though capital-less, Chân Minh could work most smoothly with people (not all in this trade meet such luck)—for Chân Minh is good-natured, honest—diligent, entrusting all to diligent practice and the Buddhas' protection.}
{align:justify|The Shurangama Sutra teaches: "The Tathagatas of the ten directions, according to this mantra's mind, skillfully abide in the ten directions, rescuing all miseries—so hells', hungry ghosts', animals' miseries, blind, deaf, mute miseries, hatred-meeting misery, love-parting misery, wish-unfulfilled misery, five-heaps' blazing misery, misfortunes great and small, hunger, thirst, poverty—through this mantra's recitation dissolve… The Tathagatas of the ten directions ever guard practitioners, granting them in the four deportments all provisions as wished"…}
{align:justify|Time passing, when Chân Minh held a two-hundred-thousand check, he went to the bank wishing to repay—bank staff moved, inviting him to tea, saying feelingly:}
{align:justify|"Your great loan—we assumed total loss—never dreaming you would come repaying—deepest thanks!"}
{align:justify|Chân Minh said: At present I can repay principal—interest later. I cannot let the nation lose—please grant me more time…}
{align:justify|Bank staff hastily said:}
{align:justify|"Oh! Granted! Granted!"}
{align:justify|Since taking refuge under Master Diệu Pháp, Chân Minh's wife, children, and parents all became vegetarian Buddha-reciters. Mother- and daughter-in-law relations, long poor, thereby greatly improved.}
{align:justify|Chân Minh's wife confided to me:}
{align:justify|"Since understanding Buddhism, when my mother-in-law angers me, I quarrel no more—but quietly cycle to the fields, gazing at wind combing rice waves… vexations dissolve with them… Each time Chân Minh calls from Shanxi, he ever reminds me to recite diligently, telling how earnestly he practices…"}
{align:justify|Chân Minh's wife went on:}
{align:justify|"Since practicing, whether cycling or transplanting… whatever I do, my heart ever recites: 'Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!' Off work, homeward, walking the road I still: 'Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!' None around, I recite louder—my heart truly peaceful and blissful… home to eat, no more carrying my bowl out gossiping with neighbors—for after eating I must still recite the Buddha's name, ever feeling too little practicing time—so idle talk ended…"}
{align:justify|Many, seeing my house daily prosper, crowd asking what we did, poor turned rich?… Puzzled, I say:}
{align:justify|"'Through Buddhism'!"}
{align:justify|Chân Minh's wife tells:}
{align:justify|"Now more and more imitate our recitation—for half a year ago a villager fell witless (a ghost possessing)—all day muttering nonsense, rampaging… I led my son to see; just entering the courtyard we heard him indoors shouting. Mother and son through the door—and the patient's manner instantly changed—he grew most gentle, honest—staring fixedly at mother and son.}
{align:justify|Someone asked him:}
{align:justify|"Why no more rampaging and racket?…"}
{align:justify|He said: These two just entered, bodies wholly covered in the Bodhisattvas' blessing golden light—so I dare not rampage.}
{align:justify|Chân Minh's son said:}
{align:justify|"Then ghost, please leave here?!"}
{align:justify|The possessing being said:}
{align:justify|"Untaught by you, how dare we leave!"}
{align:justify|So Chân Minh's son told him:}
{align:justify|"Then friend, recite 'Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!'…"}
{align:justify|The being, memorizing this one line, said:}
{align:justify|"Then I may go take rebirth!"}
{align:justify|Finished, the patient dropped unconscious as if sleeping; family carried him to bed. A sleep done, next morning the patient woke—remembering nothing before—spirit normal again.}
{align:justify|The matter spread through the whole village—people telling: "Ghosts fear vegetarian Buddha-reciters"—so they flocked seeking Chân Minh's wife, crowding to learn vegetarian Buddha-recitation.}
{align:justify|Idle farming seasons, from Chân Minh's house often ring many people's sincere sutra and Shurangama Mantra recitations.}
{align:justify|DO NOT SPREAD FOOLISH THEORIES}
{align:justify|Question: Half a month ago I invited an Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) statue home, paying twenty coins for "eye-opening"—then joyfully brought Her home. Unexpectedly, friends learning I carried the Buddha's statue home after eleven in the morning paled, scolding me:}
{align:justify|"Inviting Buddha statues must be done before eleven! Else, eye-opened or not, not only no blessing—but calamity befalls!…"}
{align:justify|The result: within days my bicycle was stolen. Evenings still I fear—not knowing what calamities henceforth may strike? So I come asking you—how to escape disaster?}
{align:justify|Answer: If "carrying Buddha statues home at wrong hours draws calamity upon families" were true—who would dare enshrine Buddhas? Laypeople, never trumpet such muddled theories misleading people! Spreading deluded, witless, ignorant claims makes evil teachers! Using such false theories guiding people defies cause and effect!}
{align:justify|Some even say the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" must not be recited at home—for reciting summons little ghosts… Others say only the topmost floor may enshrine Buddhas—worshipping where people walk above disrespects… (Then apartment dwellers may never worship?)—then the Shurangama Mantra must be recited at 3–5 in the morning, must be this, must be that, and so on…}
{align:justify|All are deviant theories obstructing sutra reciters, mantra holders, Buddha worshippers… Perhaps speakers mean well—but such trumpetings aid the demon king unawares.}
{align:justify|Understand: any hour may invite Buddhas home—and "eye-opening"? Mere form. Know: Buddhas need no eye-opening from us! Eye-opening means declaring: from today I begin bowing to, learning from Buddhas. Saying "un-eye-opened, no Buddha present" is utterly mistaken!}
{align:justify|Enshrining Buddhas at home helps us ever behold the Buddha's image—reminding that our hearts ever hold the Buddha's true wisdom light—so all we do must hold right understanding and right views. Most needed, best worship: oneself ending greed, anger, delusion, diligently practicing precepts, meditation, wisdom.}
{align:justify|So inviting Buddha statues home, enshrine them wherever pleases and suits you best. After arranging incense, flowers, offerings, offering incense and bowing—count it eye-opened! The heavenly-eyed shall see the statue radiate golden light. Even without heavenly eyes, hands may feel this energy (for instance, meters away gently pushing toward the statue, feeling an invisible resisting force). I only tell you: statues invited home, worshipped with reverent bowing, differ—the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas shall manifest divisions present there (nor cling to testing whether blessed energy is felt or golden light seen?)… Invited home, while bowing you may recite 3, 7, 21, 49 Great Compassion Mantras, or the Heart Sutra, or the Diamond Sutra, the Amitabha Sutra (or whatever sutras you wish)—all good (as for Ksitigarbha images, 1–3 rounds may be recited)… In short, whatever seems most solemn and reverent—enshrine so! The Buddha says "all is mind-made"—so after inviting statues home, strictly keeping the five precepts, practicing the ten goods, you become true Buddha disciples at once! Conversely, clinging to forms, visiting many temples for eye-openings, yet never minding the five precepts and ten goods—the Buddha shall never descend upon your home!}
{align:justify|"As for the lost bicycle, tell me: working once, did you take from your unit six meter-and-half long timbers and one dozen-meters long? Yes—old timbers the unit never used—so I took them home building a kitchen."}
{align:justify|"But your unit never permitted it—you took them home on your own! That is stealing! Seeds of evil sown then—now the bicycle lost—fruits ripened! Thanks to inviting and bowing to Buddhas' merit, karma-repayment, karma-dissolving begins. You still covet more unit property—hasten repentance, perhaps sins dissolve, or heavy retribution turns light… The lost bicycle and statue-inviting hours have nothing to do with one another! May all Buddha disciples never be so superstitious!"}
{align:justify|MY AUNT}
{align:justify|Thinking how rescuing animals and harming animals both bear retribution, I resolved to write this out for beginners' understanding.}
{align:justify|From my taking refuge to that time counted the second winter. Master Diệu Pháp was then staying at my house. One day my aunt called from Xi'an, telling: half a month earlier, snow-iced roads—walking to the plant, she carelessly fell breaking her right leg; friends carried her to hospital. Lying on the operating table she discovered fellow plant workers (herself included) totaled seven (all injured) hospitalized. Except one cracked hip, the rest all broke kneecaps.}
{align:justify|Medicines then were scarce; operated, doctors said her leg could hardly fully recover—future lameness. So she agonized.}
{align:justify|Three days later she left hospital home to heal. Past forty then, thinking "Lamed, truly unbearable!"… She knew nothing of my Buddhism, much less Master Diệu Pháp. Pure misery moved her to call and tell me. As we talked the master was present—so I deliberately spoke loud, repeating her plight—aiming for the master's ears. Then I told her how the master and I met. As I spoke she believed at once—begging the master's instruction. So I passed her the phone.}
{align:justify|The master asked her:}
{align:justify|"Four years ago did you rescue and bandage a white pigeon with a broken leg?"}
{align:justify|Hearing it she thrilled—I beside heard as follows:}
{align:justify|"Yes, Master! Four years ago visiting a coworker sister's house—just inside the courtyard I saw a dozen pigeons ringing a limping one—curious, I neared—seeing the pigeon leg-broken—I called my friend over—hastily lifting the bird—we observed together—understanding it shot (by slingshot)—so we lanced its leg draining pus, disinfecting… then with wine and ointment bandaged its leg—then splinted sticks as crutches for its walking. At last the bird's leg healed. Master! You are truly gifted—knowing my years-ago deed… Has it to do with my broken leg?"}
{align:justify|The master asked again:}
{align:justify|"Are you fond of roast chicken—especially chicken thighs, delighting gnawing legs and feet?"}
{align:justify|"Yes—I love them!"}
{align:justify|"Market-bought chickens, though already killed—still you yourself in the kitchen wield the knife gutting… chopping chicken legs—stewing, cooking, steaming… For this cause your thigh bone broke—evil repaid with evil. But having once healed the broken-legged pigeon, your wound shall leave no aftermath—persist crutch-practicing, gradually recovering…"}
{align:justify|My aunt joyfully asked:}
{align:justify|"Gutting and chopping… chicken legs earned my broken leg—then gutting its belly—must hospitals gut my belly hereafter?"}
{align:justify|The master smiled:}
{align:justify|"You wake swiftly—have Quả Khanh send you Buddhist books to read…"}
{align:justify|That is my aunt's true story—her leg later fully recovered.}
{align:justify|LEAVING LUSTFUL THOUGHTS}
{align:justify|A young couple came asking Master Diệu Pháp—telling how, refuge-taken Buddhists, vegetarianism easy, wine and drugs all quit—only the lust precept, attempts failed again and again.}
{align:justify|The layman told how, lust arising, he often silently recited Nam mô Quan Thế Âm Bồ tát! But lustful thoughts still defeated him—so he suffered greatly, begging the master's guidance.}
{align:justify|The master instructed:}
{align:justify|"Your diligent practice deserves praise. Lotuses are holy—for from mud unstained. Shakyamuni Buddha left beautiful wife and beloved children, leaving home to practice—modeling the straight practicing road for us! Home-dwellers able to end lust equal monastics—exemplary world-transcending practitioners…"}
{align:justify|Reciting Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva's sacred name can help halt lustful minds. But do not wait for lust to rise before begging the Bodhisattva's rescue—ordinarily ever recite 'Nam mô Quan Thế Âm Bồ tát!'—only so may lust be left!}
{align:justify|Most vital: first understand principles clearly—grasping the teachings, lust-precept keeping naturally succeeds. The more practiced, the more the truths dawn—faith firmer and steadier.}
{align:justify|I urge you both to study and memorize the 'Four Clear Teachings on Purity' in the Shurangama Sutra—then free moments silently recite, or softly read. Morning and evening strolls, cycling, bus-riding, outings—whatever… use all existing time to practice… Also memorize the Great Compassion Mantra, the Shurangama Mantra. Daily recite the 'Four Clear Teachings on Purity' at least one round—more, better. Above all before sleep recite one round. Beginners may take this as liturgy (home-dwellers may take the 'Four Clear Teachings on Purity' as morning-evening liturgy). Reciting aloud, not only self benefits—but surrounding beings (mortal eyes unseen) kneel listening—all benefit—so naturally boundless merit accrues.}
{align:justify|Diligently practicing so, within three months lustful thoughts in body and mind shall surely end—and ending nature ends too. Not only self ending lust—but even lustful thoughts in mind no more. Then you shall live most free and easy—no words can fully tell—this holy state only lust-enders fully feel, with wondrous peace within!}
{align:justify|Once lustful thoughts in body and mind all ended clean—even unmemorized, the 'Fourfold Pure Clear Instructions' may lifelong be practiced (for the Buddha's words are carved in the heart).}
{align:justify|Then discover the saying 'Nothing on earth more blissful than lust' is false! For lust's thrills last an instant—no true bliss at all—for continuing only wearies more—essence spent—then no bliss but misery… besides lust blazing breeding many ills—spousal conflicts… leading to divorce—even causing death, short life!}
{align:justify|Home-dwellers need proper restraint—but practitioners must end lust! For: 'Lust unended, dust unexited.'}
{align:justify|Some, lust ended a while, chance nocturnal emissions, wet dreams appear—normal phenomena—ignore them—they naturally cease.}
{align:justify|Once lust ended, not only body hale—but essence gathered, preserved—able to form relics firm beyond compare. The longer lust ended, at cremation the more relics remain. Relics are holy objects with Buddhas and Bodhisattvas—worldly sincere worship gains great benefit!}
{align:justify|So practitioners skillfully ending lust, killing, stealing, lying… shall surely attain the Bodhisattvas' supreme awareness.}
{align:justify|WHY DOES THE INFANT CRY}
{align:justify|One early spring, Tiet Nhan (a neighboring family) suddenly telephoned me for aid—telling how, New-Year visiting his boss's house, he met husband and wife fretting helpless over their newborn (barely a month old) screaming unceasing two days now. Hospitals finding no cause for the crying, their hearts burned like fire.}
{align:justify|Seeing this, Tiet Nhan hastily called me for rescue (I too clearly heard the infant wailing fiercely)—then passed the phone to the master.}
{align:justify|The master told Tiet Nhan to hand the phone to the baby's parents, asking:}
{align:justify|"Does your household love seafood (shrimp, crabs, fish…)?"}
{align:justify|They answered:}
{align:justify|"Yes. Taiwanese commonly eat these—we too!"}
{align:justify|The master said:}
{align:justify|"If now you both resolve to eat seafood no more, and recite the Buddha's name delivering the creatures you ate—your child shall instantly stop crying!"}
{align:justify|Not understanding, they challenged:}
{align:justify|"The baby's crying—what has it to do with our eating shrimp, crabs, fish…?"}
{align:justify|The master explained:}
{align:justify|"Taiwanese Buddhism flourishes—yet you two know nothing of Buddhism?"}
{align:justify|They answered:}
{align:justify|"We are outsiders—following Taiwan's Lao religion—never forbidding fresh live seafood…"}
{align:justify|The master said:}
{align:justify|"The consciousnesses of the aquatic creatures you ate hold grudges, lurking awaiting revenge. But your blessings unexhausted, young yang thriving—so these creditors turned harassing the little one. They manifest every ugly shape to frighten the child—do you see both his little arms waving ceaselessly midair? Resisting the strangely-shaped spirit-demons harassing… (frightening him crying unceasing). Creditors thus make you fret and worry—mind and strength withering—soon achieving vengeful aims!"}
{align:justify|Know: infants under three normally still hold flesh-eye powers (seeing realms human eyes cannot)—past three, worldly cravings growing gradually cloud all—so they see normally like adults. To stop creditors harassing and frightening the baby, right now husband and wife must at once vow to eat seafood no more! Declare so—then for the creatures you ate recite the sacred name 'Nam mô A Di Đà Phật' delivering them—done, the child shall stop crying at once. I know you disbelieve—but will you not test and see?"}
{align:justify|The other side hesitated a while—helpless, they vowed: "From now on we eat seafood no more!"…}
{align:justify|Then the master taught them reciting the sacred name 'Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!' They repeated but a few lines—and the baby's wails softened—then gradually ceased entirely.}
{align:justify|This startled and shook the Taiwanese boss—thanking the master unceasingly. His wife too burst crying, thanking mechanically over and over.}
{align:justify|The master told them:}
{align:justify|"Now the baby has stopped crying. From today onward, daily for the creatures you ate, diligently recite the Buddha's name delivering them. Nor is that all—hereafter release captive animals abundantly to dissolve old karma. Find time to visit Great Compassion Meditation Monastery inviting sutras home to read…"}
{align:justify|Husband and wife both promised surely to comply, diligently bowing and reciting…}
{align:justify|Two days later, Tiet Nhan with the couple came seeking Master Diệu Pháp to thank him. But the master had left the night before. I told them to offer gifts to Great Compassion Meditation Monastery.}
{align:justify|CAUSES OF MISFORTUNE}
{align:justify|One autumn day in 2001, a layperson led in a woman of about thirty, looking most haggard. She knelt before Master Diệu Pháp weeping, pouring out her misfortunes endlessly—forgetting dozens of guests present…}
{align:justify|"Master, I suffer so—begging Layman Hach to lead me here, asking your path of life. As a child I loved solitude, shunning crowds—playing alone, strolling alone… I lived most orderly, responsible… But at nineteen villains raped me—then I ached to suicide—so I left school a year. Two years later raped again… though villains were at last law-punished, my spirit nearly collapsed—studying no more. At twenty-five my parents set me socializing, introduced around… But my first boyfriend, near a year, cheated my love then left. Next year I met another—acting just like the first. I have nearly no faith left to live—misery, tears ever streaming. Please explain: why is my fate so unfortunate? Any way to change it?"}
{align:justify|The master invited her to sit. Musing a while, he asked:}
{align:justify|"Why wear no makeup today?"}
{align:justify|Hearing it she looked puzzled—then apologetically said:}
{align:justify|"Mrs. Hach told me… meeting you, wear no makeup…"}
{align:justify|The master said gravely:}
{align:justify|"I ask not blaming your bare face—but warning: this life's many misfortunes begin with your excessive makeup! Ever grooming beauty, clothes ever fashionable, fond of revealing, seductive dress… Though solitary and eccentric, you love all admiring gazes—loving to entice the opposite sex's eyes!… Am I right?"}
{align:justify|She nodded.}
{align:justify|The master went on:}
{align:justify|"Male-female desire—all share it—I blame nothing. But women are by nature weaker—so carefully guard yourselves. Chinese say: 'Adorning beauty invites lust; flaunting wealth invites robbers.' Meaning: wealth must hide skillfully—never flaunt! Craving to display riches makes yourself robbers' targets! Over-attending beauty, gorgeous makeup, or overly revealing seductive dress—stirring the opposite sex at a glance to lustful thoughts, blazing desire… they cannot self-control—naturally meaning to harm you. So, avowing yourself an (orderly, responsible) girl—you should skillfully guard yourself—never dress revealingly, stirring lustful minds, loving thoughts in the opposite sex…"}
{align:justify|As for spouses, all bear affinities, depending moreover on former lives' causes and karma. Past lives' 'who owes whom, who debts whom'… surely this life seeks out (meeting) collecting debts… Do you not see handsome youths wedding plain wives, or beauties wedding truly ugly husbands? So all good men and women in the world must never ape film stars' revealing fashions—for: 'Grooming, revealing invites lust; flaunting wealth invites robbers.'"}
{align:justify|If hereafter you wish a happy family, ever guard mind and will upright and pure, often reciting Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva—then though fate arranges 'evil-affinity spouses,' they may turn 'good-affinity spouses.' Do you hear and understand?"}
{align:justify|"Yes, clearly!"}
{align:justify|"Good! Act accordingly! Within a year, you shall meet your ideal lord. As for your former unfortunate fate—this still depends on your former lives' sown causes. Hence they say: 'To know past lives' causes, behold this life's fruits'… But from now on, keep the five precepts, practice the ten goods, read many sutras—gradually all shall clear."}
{align:justify|The woman gladly obeyed the master's teaching.}
{align:justify|KIM DOANH}
{align:justify|Kim Doanh, a girl of about thirty, tearfully told Master Diệu Pháp her misery:}
{align:justify|"At sixteen, seventeen I chanced upon 'Shakyamuni Buddha's Life'—at once unforgettable—loving it, unable to set it down. Then I read Buddhism's basic books—learning Buddhism's holy land Mount Wutai—ever dreaming, longing to climb it once—but graduating, then working—till twenty-seven the wish came true…"}
{align:justify|Just reaching Mount Wutai, Kim Doanh felt most familiar—indeed Mount Wutai was her homeland. Entering temples, lodging in guesthouses, meeting bhikkhunis—she felt them kin—settling, unwilling to leave—truly forgetting home—even forgetting her leave overstayed—till the nun reminded:}
{align:justify|"Are you not returning to work?"}
{align:justify|She woke with a start, remembering—hurrying home. Home, she learned the family nearly sent men to Mount Wutai seeking her—since leaving she never once telephoned—overstaying leave before creeping back—worrying her mother heart-ill. Family scolded her roundly—her mother declaring:}
{align:justify|"Henceforth go nowhere—unless you want me dead soon!"}
{align:justify|But that Mount Wutai visit left her longing ever after—even dreaming repeatedly of wearing monastic robes. Before long, she resolved to leave home—her mother fiercely opposing. She told her mother:}
{align:justify|"Mother believes in Buddhism too—is leaving home not good? Besides, brother, sister, nephews and nieces surround you—is that not enough? Barring my leaving home—do you know how I suffer?"}
{align:justify|Her mother flatly said:}
{align:justify|"Speak of this no more! Whoever wishes to leave home may—except you! While I live, never shall you leave home! Leave home, and I… die at once, watch!"}
{align:justify|No way left, she thought 'only imitating the Buddha—fleeing home to leave it'… quietly quitting her job, leaving a letter, secretly leaving home.}
{align:justify|She climbed Mount Wutai, entered a temple, begging the abbot to leave home. But the abbot told her first not to shave—dwell here practicing—see if monastic life suits? Barely half a month, her brother found her—telling how, her just gone, next day mother sent seekers at once—but none knew which temple—seeking continuously half a month—to now tracing her dwelling. Her brother added:}
{align:justify|"Mother, over your leaving home, has fallen gravely heart-ill, hospitalized—living or dying unknown…"}
{align:justify|So she must follow her brother back—mother just discharged, lying home healing.}
{align:justify|She lamented to the master:}
{align:justify|"Truly I 'leave' no 'home.' But my heart and mind remain at temples. Past thirty now—yet still unfree—hardly realizing my dream. Daily I live soulless… Please teach me—what shall I do?…"}
{align:justify|Miss Kim speaking, tears streaming, sobbing choked…}
{align:justify|The master, waiting her calm, said:}
{align:justify|"Have you not yet quit meat?"}
{align:justify|"Not yet."}
{align:justify|"Then begin quitting meat at once. Daily remember practice—morning-evening liturgies evenly. Though at work, hearts ever reciting the Buddha's name—memorize the 'Great Compassion Mantra,' the 'Heart Sutra,' or the 'Fourfold Pure Clear Instructions,' often upholding."}
{align:justify|Evening sutras done, sit forty minutes to an hour. Daily examine this life's ten-evil faults—resolving repentance. Evenly practicing so equals monastics—read many sutras—above all the 'Shurangama Sutra'—read much to grow wisdom.}
{align:justify|Dwelling home, you may still spread teachings benefiting beings—conditions permitting, saving others—never force! Buddhists must prize beings' benefit—how much more your mother! Never to satisfy your own dream harm her! Your leaving-home time falls about three years hence (if now you can let go of all clinging, vigorously practicing as I teach)—then surely hope exists…"}
{align:justify|Miss Kim, hearing the master's teaching, reverently obeyed.}
{align:justify|That evening near rest, I asked the master what affinities moved Miss Kim to leave home?}
{align:justify|The master told:}
{align:justify|"'A hundred years ago, a young man practiced the Daoist path on Mount Wutai—admired by an aristocrat's daughter. One day the lady with her maid visited the Daoist temple—bowing to the Daoist—offering a small cloth bag. The Daoist accepted—then opening, seeing silver within—deeply moved.}
{align:justify|So both sides came and went—gradually feelings grew… the Daoist, unable to withstand feminine temptation—at last wed the lady. Fearing gossip, the lady led her maid with her—husband and wife into green mountains and clear waters building a home. Married, the two lived happily, respecting like guests… The husband still loved practice—wife single-mindedly tending, caring—the husband's loving threads ever tangling… the lady's maid too stayed serving them to life's end.}
{align:justify|Miss Kim today is that Daoist's later incarnation. Deep love turned her female. Her mother is the aristocratic lady—while Kim Doanh's father is the maid. Formerly wholehearted, perfect in service—so this life reborn male—yet toward the lady and Daoist of old, still wholeheartedly tending'…"}
{align:justify|Hearing the master's instruction, I could only sigh—practitioners not cutting, ending loving clinging—at last hardly escaping rebirth.}
{align:justify|Truly:}
{align:justify|Practice is hard—hard as climbing heaven}
{align:justify|Teaching is arduous—as planting lotuses in fire}
{align:justify|Ending lust is hard—hard as cooking sand to rice}
{align:justify|So hearing teachings, believing, practicing, vigorously ending lust, removing love… is practitioners' root.}
{align:justify|DO SUTRA RECITERS STILL MEET CALAMITIES?}
{align:justify|Question: The "Ksitigarbha Sutra" teaches—picturing heaven, earth, ghosts and spirits ever guarding reciters, bowers to Buddhas—letting no fierce calamities enter doors, no water, fire, theft, robbery… occur—meaning all evil dissolves. But I see many lay reciters still meeting calamities: sickness, villains, car crashes, plane falls… myself often meeting unpleasing matters… I even see virtuous high monks and laypeople suddenly gravely ill, passing? Though never doubting the "Ksitigarbha Sutra's" descriptions, questions linger—please explain further.}
{align:justify|Answer: Please read the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" closely—never snip lines expounding personal meanings. Know: whenever Buddhas and Bodhisattvas teach, mentioning those gaining benefit, the passage always opens: "Good men, good women." Clarifying: all "good men, good women" bowing to Buddhas, reciting sutras, urging others to believe and learn Buddhism, gain a hundred thousand ghosts and spirits guarding day and night. Inversely: "If not good men, good women, yet bowing to Buddhas, urging others practicing… they merely form Buddha affinities, planting wholesome seeds—never at once drawing heaven, earth, ghosts, spirits guarding—for they merely believe, never truly learn Buddhism, never practice! Unsown good seeds—whence good fruits? So never truly gaining benefits."}
{align:justify|Then what measures "good men, good women?"}
{align:justify|The Buddha calls home-dwelling and home-leaving men and women "good men, good women." Good means: loving teachings, believing Buddhas. Though home-dwellers keep spouses, all their words and deeds guard like monastics'. But drinkers, gamblers, drug users, brawlers, adulterers, philanderers, robbers, thieves, liars, ten-evil doers… none are called "good men, good women." Planting evil seeds, they must reap evil fruits—how hope heaven, earth, ghosts, spirits guard them?}
{align:justify|Ms. Lam, working in China, asked Master Diệu Pháp thus:}
{align:justify|"A layman in Canada prospered greatly. Temple donations, sutra and wholesome-book printings and gifts—no less than hundreds of thousands of dollars—but recently a car crash gravely injured him—single-mindedly reciting the Buddha's name… till his last breath never ceasing—passing before fifty. So some doubt Buddhism—please explain."}
{align:justify|The master asked:}
{align:justify|"Do you know this layman's private life?"}
{align:justify|"Not well!"}
{align:justify|The master explained:}
{align:justify|"Before taking the Three Jewels' refuge he defiled many young girls—after refuge, though restrained, still unpure—moreover never truly repented former lustful conduct. Thanks to last-moment unceasing Buddha-recitation, boundless sins dissolved too—temples held deliverance rites besides—plus lifetime merits donating temples, printing sutras, books… so now he is already born into the Trayastrimsha Heaven. Without Three-Jewel donation merits and last-moment recitation, he would suffer 'spirit dissolved and scattered.' Meaning: the more promiscuous or deviant offenses, the more the spirit dissolves—hells must be borne—then if humanly reborn, reborn intersex (half male, half female), low wisdom—not only self-miserable but scorned, despised…"}
{align:justify|So refuge-taking disciples must surely end evil, practice good—never presuming 'donating Buddhas, Dharma, Sangha, printing many sutras… surely Pure Land rebirth—so sleep pillowed high'… Know 'good repaid with good, evil with evil'—an unchanging law for all. The Buddha, toward precept-breaking fallen disciples, pities—but cannot help. Then how hope a few incense sticks or sutra rounds grant every wish?"}
{align:justify|Understand clearly: precepts the Buddha laid down never control disciples—but warn them: 'Unguarded precepts mean falls, punished by cause and effect's law.' So, wishing neither hell after death nor calamities in life—each must consciously keep precepts! Else, repentance comes too late…}
{align:justify|Ms. Lam said:}
{align:justify|"He died barely thirty days ago—Canadian temples held deliverance rites—does it help him? Can he reach the Pure Land?"}
{align:justify|The master answered:}
{align:justify|"Even as I teach you, he too hears and has seen right from wrong—giving rise to great repentance… thereby from the Abhasvara Heaven rising toward the Blessed Love Heaven—seeming to rise toward the Extensive Fruition Heaven besides—for I see the Extensive Fruition Heaven flickering…"}
{align:justify|"Why flickering, please?"}
{align:justify|"Like elevators—whichever floor reached, that floor flickers…"}
{align:justify|Ms. Lam asked:}
{align:justify|"Then can he reach the Pure Land?"}
{align:justify|The master answered:}
{align:justify|"Uncertain—needing a pure-virtued person to recite forty-nine 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' rounds and three 'Wonderful Dharma Lotus' sets for him…"}
{align:justify|Lam asked:}
{align:justify|"What is a pure person?"}
{align:justify|"One keeping precepts strictly—no killing, stealing, lust, lying (home-dwelling or home-leaving alike)… all may serve!"}
{align:justify|"Then inviting Canadian temple monks delivering him—will that do?"}
{align:justify|Answer: Unclear.}
{align:justify|Ms. Lam asked:}
{align:justify|"Then… inviting you delivering him—will that do?"}
{align:justify|"My energies suffice not reciting these sutras…"}
{align:justify|"You spoke but a few lines and he already rose to high heavens—then speak more—tell him to the Pure Land world—it shall work!"}
{align:justify|"If a few lines sufficed, Amitabha Buddha would have ferried all beings to the Pure Land long ago! Understand deliverance rites' might thus: when the fourfold assembly—pure-virtued Buddha disciples—recite sutras, recite the Buddha's name—many beings from empty space come hearing teachings, gaining benefits accordingly… This very merit grows the departed's faith and vows—helping them reach the Pure Land. But only sleeping long within lotuses—never at once 'blossoms opening, Buddhas beheld.' Among the departed's fellow practitioners in Canada, pure-virtued ones exist—you may invite them…"}
{align:justify|Lam: Then let me telephone inviting all to try? I worry… so much reciting—forty-nine days nearly expired—if too late, what then?…}
{align:justify|"He has already risen to the Extensive Fruition Heaven—no intermediate body more—so never bound by forty-nine days? Whenever recitation completes, all well!"}
{align:justify|"Splendid—let me invite reciters—done, telephoning the master—may I?"}
{align:justify|"Granted!"}
{align:justify|Question: Why do virtuous high monks too fall gravely ill and die?}
{align:justify|Answer: Buddhas and successive high monks all manifest common mortal forms born into the world—their physical bodies too are earth, water, wind, fire combined. Birth, age, sickness, death are common law—and practitioners are intimately tied to beings besides. When their worldly missions complete, most manifest sickness to leave the dust. Some bear sickness' signs without torment—for instance, liver, lung, stomach cancers unfelt—needing no painkillers. (Only modeling practices like sitting meditation, Buddha-recitation, sutra recitation, mantra holding… giving rise to Buddha-power blessing miracles—urging disciples and fellow practitioners to diligence, good practice meeting impermanence.)}
{align:justify|Naturally some manifest sitting, lying, or illness-free passing. Called fine, wholesome manifestations—teaching people diligent practice. Buddhist biographies record a great many!}
{align:justify|Master Diệu Pháp tells: when Master Hsuan Hua fell ill, sometimes before disciples he too manifested pain—but hearing disciples' sorrowing tears, he at once switched to peaceful looks, cheerfully saying:}
{align:justify|"You make mountains of trifles!"}
{align:justify|Then speaking and laughing normally. Later disciples learned the master was testing, trying all.}
{align:justify|BODHISATTVAS IN SECRET PRACTICE}
{align:justify|Master Diệu Pháp tells: once visiting the City of Ten Thousand Buddhas to audience Master Hsuan Hua, he chanced upon a Dharma-protecting layman at the City killed in a car crash.}
{align:justify|This layman had not seen wife and children for years—now reunited. Then driving them from the airport home—nearing the City of Ten Thousand Buddhas—disaster struck—wife and children lightly hurt—he alone died.}
{align:justify|Someone asked Master Diệu Pháp:}
{align:justify|"This layman left family in Taiwan—alone coming to the City of Ten Thousand Buddhas Dharma-protecting, also teaching the City's students. His heart is most good—yet why so violent a death?"}
{align:justify|Master Diệu Pháp answered:}
{align:justify|"This man is an Equal-Enlightenment Bodhisattva reborn—come aiding Master Hsuan Hua! Dharma-protecting is his original vow! His sudden death repays former lives' grievous debts—simultaneously warning the world life is impermanent—practice diligently! For in a past life he once harmed a pair of great birds—so returning from the airport, repayment's hour come—the two birds' (creditors') consciousnesses spread wings before the windshield, blocking his view—so, road unseen, he struck a pole, dying!"}
{align:justify|The master asked his wife and children:}
{align:justify|"Did your husband urge you both to America quickly?"}
{align:justify|His wife said:}
{align:justify|"Yes—days earlier 'he' kept urging by telephone—saying though no longer beside us, we must strive practicing well. Then I grew annoyed hearing it repeated—now reflecting, I understand 'he' had prepared beforehand…"}
{align:justify|The master explained:}
{align:justify|"He wished you both in America meeting him one last time—seeing him off."}
{align:justify|The master's words greatly consoled the wife and children—also urging their faith and practice. They bowed to him.}
{align:justify|Those present, believing the master, reverently bowed too.}
{align:justify|But many doubters raised objecting voices reaching the master:}
{align:justify|"That layman—what goodness? No great gifts to the City of Ten Thousand Buddhas—how call him 'Equal-Enlightenment Bodhisattva reborn'? If he is an Equal-Enlightenment Bodhisattva, then I am… a Buddha reborn!"}
{align:justify|In the 'Fourfold Pure Clear Instructions' of the 'Shurangama Sutra' the Buddha teaches most clearly: even 'prostitutes, widows, cheats, thieves, butchers'… may be 'Bodhisattvas, Arhats reborn manifesting'—how much more laypeople and monks?… These reborn ones, even awakened, never beat breasts boasting 'I truly am a Bodhisattva, an Arhat'… except at life's end secretly leaving last words—or after passing, wise ones revealing their identities… They themselves silently come, silently go. All to teach people—manifesting 'life is impermanent'—all acting as the Buddha arranged… awaiting your awakening, all shall clear naturally.}
{align:justify|We common mortals, still deluded and unclear, should never judge recklessly—lest we create evil speech karma, offending boundlessly. Great Master Tinh Am passed at forty-nine—we must not with mortal eyes comment: 'his practice was poor, hence short-lived!' For all states before our eyes are trials testing practitioners—'if truly a true practitioner, one sees no worldly faults!'"}
{align:justify|"'Seeing no worldly faults' means not that, seeing people do evil, we never criticize, advise, or restrain… but precisely that we must meet things with the Buddhist view."}
{align:justify|CAREFUL SPEECH}
{align:justify|One autumn morning in 2000, Master Diệu Pháp was teaching dozens at a layman's house—when suddenly through the gate stepped a middle-aged man—kneeling before Master Diệu Pháp, urgently begging the master to come saving his wife.}
{align:justify|The master told him not to hurry—slowly tell all.}
{align:justify|It seems last month his wife began painfully difficult defecation—before long urination too grew hard—half an hour, urinating unfinished—then two hours, toileting undone. So hospital examination revealed internal hemorrhoids—grown big as steamed buns (no exaggeration). Now walking is hard—mind most miserable. Doctors scolded her—why wait till now for examination? She said painless and itchless—never knowing so grave an illness.}
{align:justify|He sighed reporting:}
{align:justify|"Doctors say immediate surgery is needed—yet affirm great danger—for before, he operated internal hemorrhoids many times—among them some died of great hemorrhage—but their piles were never big as hers. We are perplexed, not knowing what to do—hearing a master is passing this region—we venture begging your compassion saving my wife's life."}
{align:justify|The master settled a while, then said:}
{align:justify|"Your wife practices most well—most diligent! Have not husband and wife already slept in separate rooms?"}
{align:justify|"Yes, Master. This region has several couples practicing the 'Fourfold Pure Clear Instructions' in the 'Shurangama Sutra.' Since my wife firmly wishes so, I comply. Though less devout than she—unwilling—still I know she acts rightly—so I bear with her. Perfecting her perfects me. Why, practicing so diligently, does she draw such results?"}
{align:justify|The master smiled, saying:}
{align:justify|"Are such results not good?… Bodhisattvas fear causes, beings fear fruits! Lawful practitioners surely meet retribution-obstacles—it may be called karma manifesting before us—precisely through her diligent practice earning early karma-repayment—wholly no evil! Else, so great a pile—why painless? But presently she is most unstable—lead me to your house at once!"}
{align:justify|His house was not far—those present surging along.}
{align:justify|The patient, Mrs. Tran, long practiced outsider paths—knowing Buddhism, she guided the whole Tran clan—siblings down to grandchildren… dozens all taking Buddhist refuge. From sixty-year-old elders down to ten-year-old children—all willingly quit meat for vegetarianism—in common words: 'one knife cutting all clean!'… Moreover most memorized the Shurangama Mantra—even Tran's second and third elder sisters, illiterate, studied character by character—yet at last memorized the whole Shurangama Mantra—besides diligently temple-going, sutra-reciting, morning-evening practice…}
{align:justify|Witnessing this, I was utterly moved. Other morning-evening liturgies like the Great Compassion Mantra, the Ten Minor Mantras—her grandchildren mostly knew by heart. Laywoman Tran, the great family's 'command headquarters'—even her brother, a plant director, followed her practicing.}
{align:justify|Now she lay peacefully on bed—looking as if awaiting death's arrival…}
{align:justify|Spying Master Diệu Pháp entering, she seemed most moved—gratefully saying:}
{align:justify|"Oh! The master comes saving me!…"}
{align:justify|Speaking, palms joining, about to sit—the master stopped her.}
{align:justify|The master sat on the chair by her bed, gently asking:}
{align:justify|"Did you once open a cloth shop?"}
{align:justify|Mrs. Tran answered:}
{align:justify|"Yes—but closed now."}
{align:justify|Quarreling with customers then, did you curse them? the master asked again.}
{align:justify|"Business often brings such matters—especially odd customers—but I never cursed anyone!"}
{align:justify|The master pressed:}
{align:justify|"Like… a woman past thirty insisting returning you a cloth length—both sides quarreling—you once uttered wounding words—think hard and check whether this happened?"}
{align:justify|The master's words hinting, Laywoman Tran looked moved, saying:}
{align:justify|"Oh—I remember—truly it happened! Daily I always examine myself—whatever faults remembered, repented… How could I forget this clean away?"}
{align:justify|Then she told:}
{align:justify|"Roughly six years ago, a sister pointed at cloths displayed outside my shop, wishing to buy. So I took that very kind inside the shop—cut her several meters. But next day, she came wandering back, demanding returns. How could I accept then? I said:"}
{align:justify|"'You chose carefully—then I cut and sold—now returning—whom shall I sell to?…'" }
{align:justify|But she stubbornly said:}
{align:justify|"'The flowered cloth you cut inside the shop differs from that displayed outside…'" }
{align:justify|And holding her bought cloth, she compared it with that displayed outside. Since outside goods, displayed many days—sun- and dew-bleached—naturally colors faded a little paler. But the cloth I sold her was true original color—never weathered… Yet she insisted it differed! Shouting loudly, demanding returns!}
{align:justify|Then truly I flared—retorting:}
{align:justify|"'How can indoor and outdoor cloth match? Like your face and your… bottom!'"}
{align:justify|Then truly I meant an analogy explaining for her understanding—could that count as… cursing?}
{align:justify|Seeing Mrs. Tran most agitated, the master smiled saying:}
{align:justify|"Then were there not crowds ringing around?"}
{align:justify|Mrs. Tran said:}
{align:justify|"Yes—for a bustling market—so many people. And her shouting loudly, arguing unreasonably so—naturally drew crowds ringing—moreover none backed her returns—all deeming her most unreasonable!"}
{align:justify|The master said again:}
{align:justify|"Your analogy given, the ringing crowds roared laughing—even teasing her to… drop her trousers comparing face and bottom for ease?…"}
{align:justify|"Yes… it seems so!…"}
{align:justify|"Then think hard—your analogy—reasoned, may hold. Toward men, the matter bears no great fault—but toward women, it must rank a kind of humiliation! Now you have received lay Bodhisattva precepts—such speech karma created—how unrepented? It seems at last you still allowed her returns?…"}
{align:justify|"Yes. Then someone advised me—thus harms business—better generous a little—bearing loss letting her return. So many ringing now—may even advertise for me… I heard reason—so acted. And she then had nothing more arguing… and I agreed to her returns. But that my analogy humiliated her—then I never thought. Now your reminder: those bygone words truly stirred humiliating effects. Why did I never think then?"}
{align:justify|Master Diệu Pháp said:}
{align:justify|"One resolving great practice—not only ending all evil, practicing all good… but this life's created karma—must surely recall repenting—only so may karma dissolve. Precisely through your diligent practice—yet forgetting that bygone speech karma—so itknotted into this evil fruit. Evil fruits ripening—one must fall. Now seal yourself examining—then tomorrow meet me!"}
{align:justify|Next morning Mrs. Tran cycled to audience with the master.}
{align:justify|She said:}
{align:justify|"Master—truly miraculously unexpected—the steamed-bun pile has flown wingless away!"}
{align:justify|This taught surrounding Buddhist learners greatly. Above all Mrs. Tran's younger sister. Once she opened a shoe shop several years—then famed as a chatterbox yielding to none—whoever provoked her—she scolded rattling near two hours. After learning Buddhism, though knowing repentance—still Mrs. Tran's story this time moved her to mightily reform—striving examining—sifting all her own faults.}
{align:justify|Frankly, my own mind fared no better—startled too: carefully checking all my own faults.}
{align:justify|WHAT CAUSES IT?}
{align:justify|Not long before, my friend's family emigrated to America—then Mr. and Mrs. Dien returned visiting kin—the wife gloomily telling me her younger brother Dien Kiet's illness in America. Hoping this homecoming I might lead her to audience with Master Diệu Pháp—asking the illness's causes (the Dien family took Buddhist refuge in 1993—ever maintaining Dharma-spreading work).}
{align:justify|Meeting the master, she detailed her younger brother's illness.}
{align:justify|Dien Kiet, green-carded in America, married a Chinese girl there. At first they lived most happily. Then for unknown reasons Dien Kiet grew suspicious of a most good coworker—thinking friend X used electronic 'remote control' devices secretly harming Kiet—causing headaches, whole-body discomfort…}
{align:justify|So Kiet dared not work—staying home. But ever feeling friend X with electronic devices secretly attacking him. Kiet said had past qigong practice not lent some skill—long since harmed dead!}
{align:justify|When others asked why friend X would harm him, Kiet said jealousy of his talent. For his computer skills were better…}
{align:justify|So Kiet ever worried—eating, sleeping uneasy—papering walls with foil—saying it blocks electronic interference—also building a big hollow iron chest, ventilated—to sleep inside—covered above, lined below…}
{align:justify|But lying days, still feeling electronics' attacks unstoppable… So Kiet took a metal basin—upturned on his head—then hid under beds or in corners—anytime, anywhere—as crises peaked… His spirit nearly collapsed. Doctors called Kiet paranoid schizophrenic, prescribing drugs. But Kiet firmly refused—insisting he was not ill!}
{align:justify|Ms. Dien worried greatly—fearing her brother's illness worsening—consequences past imagining. For in her clan two already (a cousin-uncle and Third Uncle's son) suffered exactly this paranoid illness years before—results: the cousin-uncle suicided—Third Uncle's son, though living, lies long hospitalized—living yet as if dead.}
{align:justify|Now that illness descends on her brother—surely undruggable… She wished to ask the master—what fault causes this? Why do both parents' lines—all three—suffer identical strange ills? Shall hereafter this illness bequeath to descendants?}
{align:justify|The master told Ms. Dien:}
{align:justify|"Among your forebears, one was a writer—fond of horror-ghost fiction… for amusement—gravely harming countless readers. These works frightened viewers' hearts trembling—breeding fearful superstitions; nights panicked, sleep uneasy. Many youths, reading these novels, minds terrorized—deeply imprinted. Some fear darkness—dare not walk nights… Some, in special straits—for instance, startled by some sound—panicked into illness and death…"}
{align:justify|"'Such causes, such fruits.' For these horror novels, spreading among people, harming generations of readers—and at last retribution returns—harming the scribbler's own descendants."}
{align:justify|Whichever books people love reading—spread the longer. But wholesome books—granting people knowledge, peace—helping people advance—guiding filial piety, humanity; deference above, kindness below; just, clean; law-abiding… draw great blessed retribution—improving society's moral climate.}
{align:justify|Conversely, writing base things—novels, plays, television, films trumpeting terror, lust, theft, robbery, lewd filth… harms not only present society but bequeaths calamity many generations after.}
{align:justify|So pen-holders must surely understand cause and effect—never for momentary fun and profit scribble nonsense—leaving people's poison—bequeathing endless calamity to descendants' generations.}
{align:justify|Know: though now they fame and taste myriad joys—impermanence still comes. Eyes closing, nothing carried away—only evil karma carried—leaving disasters, miseries… to later lives and descendants. 'Harming others harms oneself!' Tell me, Ms. Dien—did any forebear write horror fiction?"}
{align:justify|"My family straits are rather complex—as a child I knew little… for political reasons the family scattered each direction… So of grandsires and forebears… I know still less. Grown, once I heard mother sketch a little. Thereby knowing my father sprang from famed scholar-gentry stock—grandfather and great-grandfather both Qing-dynasty scholars. I heard an aunt once translated some great author's horror fiction into Chinese. But who wrote horror fiction I know not… yet in a family mostly writers—most fond of composing thrillers… surely it exists…"}
{align:justify|I deeply believe the master's teaching—else why would my clan suffer identical strange ills in succession? Three have caught it already. Now my heart worries greatly—not knowing when those horror-ghost novels shall vanish from the world? Then must this dreadful illness bequeath generation after generation tormenting descendants? I respectfully ask master—is any way to save my brother? Any way banning this bequest?"}
{align:justify|The master smiled answering:}
{align:justify|"Fret not—naturally this must break. Without solutions—would I not add your heart-ill? Just now I spoke of 'karmic links'—for indeed sufferers of this illness exist. The patient's former life surely sowed like evil seeds. Suppose: they once frightened people—scaring some to death… so this life they are born into like families—merging into one clan bearing 'karmic retribution' descending…"}
{align:justify|Because you know and believe Buddhism—moreover striving Dharma-protecting—so blessings stored and Buddha affinities formed for family—so your brother hopes to recover. But surely you are not wholly vegetarian—am I right?"}
{align:justify|"Right, Master."}
{align:justify|"Then… returning home—can you lifelong vegetarianism?"}
{align:justify|"No problem, Master."}
{align:justify|"Good. Then hold a repentance assembly at a temple—delivering those harmed reading those horror novels. Repent on forebears' behalf (for elder authors) composing such poisonous novels."}
{align:justify|Afterward you yourself may dwell home—reciting ten straight 'Shurangama Sutra' rounds dedicating to Dharma-realm beings—using this merit dissolving forebears' sinful karma. Done—henceforth your clan shall know none catching this illness! Besides, daily remember offering a water cup before Buddhas—daily sincerely reciting one 'Universal Gate Chapter of Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva' round and three Great Compassion Mantras—then giving your brother to drink—his spirit shall gradually mend."}
{align:justify|Ms. Dien reverently noted Master Diệu Pháp's instructions.}
{align:justify|Now Ms. Dien has returned to America. Since this story warns well of cause and effect, I write it out sharing for your counsel—also hoping through this short piece reminding all to vigilance—careful sowing seeds.}
{align:justify|I dedicate this merit to Mr. Dien Kiet—praying his swift recovery.}
{align:justify|Quả Khanh}
{align:justify|Part 2}
{align:justify|PRAYING-FOR-A-CHILD EXPERIENCES}
{align:justify|October 1, 2006, Master Dieu Chan passed Yangzhou opening a meditation retreat—passing my place she dropped Dharma-milk—teaching many fine practice matters to visiting laypeople. Then before the Buddha hall she earnestly reminded me:}
{align:justify|"Laypeople learning Buddhism a decade—also resolving everywhere giving—yet sutra and mantra texts unwilling reciting fluently! Giving is precious and hard—but studying sutra treasuries to deepen—growing wisdom, breaking ignorance… more needful and weightier!"}
{align:justify|Hearing the master's teaching, I felt ashamed—thinking hereafter surely striving diligent practice. My family life is most happy—my daughter teens—clever and bright—yet still I thirst bearing one more boy—silently wishing him Buddha-teachings' influence from womb-teaching—then lifelong acting on the Buddha's words—truly continuing His wisdom-life. This dream ever burned in me—but now my daughter is twelve—long years passed, still unconceived—so my heart regrets greatly.}
{align:justify|After learning Buddhism, I diligently practiced, reciting sutras… then miracles appeared. One day marketing—just seeing fresh fine apples—I stood transfixed—staring… my heart suddenly craving intensely—like pregnant craving. Then hospital examination—truly pregnant!}
{align:justify|This glad news left husband and wife rejoicing—happiness boundless.}
{align:justify|But after excitement, I thought: "My husband nears fifty—I near forty—if the born child is no boy—what then?" Thinking a while, I woke with a start, thinking: "A Buddhist, I must never abort—for abortion too is killing—retribution most miserable!"… Then Master Dieu Chan instructing—husband and I decided keeping the child.}
{align:justify|Early pregnancy, my body most uncomfortable—ever sleepy—limbs weary, languid, uneasy. Fellow practitioners said during pregnancy reciting the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' and the 'Universal Gate Sutra' greatly benefits babes. So I resolved for my child—trying the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra'…}
{align:justify|Oh! True miracles—daily I recited one round—no more nausea symptoms—mind no more vexed and scattered—reciting wholeheartedly attentive—about two hours completing one round.}
{align:justify|Festival days (Buddhas' and Bodhisattvas' birthdays or feast days)—I with fellow practitioners released captive animals. I also invited dozens of monastics and laypeople at 'Thong Giao Temple'—together reciting a week-long 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' and three-day 'Universal Gate Sutra'—dedicating merit to the fetus.}
{align:justify|I hoped this child (so hard conceived) would bear profound blessings and wisdom—becoming future pillars of the nation. Six months pregnant, affinities let me meet layman Quả Khanh in Beijing—'Karmic Retribution in Present Life's' author—hearing him affirm the babe in my womb was female—I drooped disappointed and sad.}
{align:justify|For before, a Chinese-medicine teacher also judged my womb's infant female—even experienced mothering friends all said my pregnancy was a girl! But I already have a girl—now most hoping to bear a boy—yet why unfulfilled?"}
{align:justify|Worried, I asked layman Quả Khanh:}
{align:justify|"Then… what shall I do?"}
{align:justify|He answered:}
{align:justify|"Though you believe in Buddhas—knowing giving, releasing captive animals, printing wholesome sutras and books… creating great merit—yet your husband still uneats vegetarian—while your precept-keeping is impure! If you truly wish bearing male—husband and wife, vow before Buddhas: 'From now on vowing quitting all meaty foods, lifelong vegetarianism—vowing strictly keeping five precepts—begging Buddhas grant a boy able hereafter to spread teachings benefiting beings'… Daily also reciting one 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' round—reciting Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva ten thousand times—done—the female fetus shall turn male!"}
{align:justify|I hastily asked to be sure:}
{align:justify|"Please—one month left till birth—doing so… truly hope exists?"}
{align:justify|Quả Khanh answered:}
{align:justify|"The Buddha teaches: 'All is mind-made'—sutra teachings are wholly true, never false—disbelieve, and count me unspeaking!"}
{align:justify|Seeing my faith insufficient, he told me:}
{align:justify|"In Henan a pregnant woman—three ultrasounds all female—seven days left till birth—husband and wife together vowing: 'If bearing male, vowing quitting all meaty foods, stepping onto Buddhist learning!… Result: Buddha-power blessed—a week later they bore male."}
{align:justify|This story grew my faith. That evening before Buddhas I vowed: "Only grant me a healthy clever boy—henceforth I vow strictly keeping five precepts—lifelong vegetarianism—learning Buddhism, reciting the Buddha's name—and giving a hundred thousand (some 16,000 USD) printing sutras as Buddhist works—moreover hereafter, able, I vow widely doing blessed good—spreading teachings benefiting beings…"}
{align:justify|The fetus daily greater—yet still I persisted reciting sutras—vegetarianism, releasing captive animals—daily driving myself everywhere—body never uncomfortable. Hospitalization's first day I still drove myself releasing captive animals. Though the due date arrived, no birth signs appeared—so I drove myself to hospital pre-birth examination.}
{align:justify|Thirteen years earlier bearing my eldest daughter I hemorrhaged—now older bearing a second—truly my heart worried…}
{align:justify|Examining, doctors said:}
{align:justify|"Little amniotic fluid—fearing fetal peril—hospitalize at once."}
{align:justify|Hospitalized—three days running—daily doctors injected labor-inducing drugs—yet no birth signs appeared—I went ultrasound examining further—seeing fluid grown more than before—till day four doctors stopped inducing drugs—letting me rest a day. Day five I walked from hospital to a nearby temple bowing Buddhas (each hall, each Buddha, each Bodhisattva I kowtowed thrice. Start to finish some dozens of bows. Then I returned to hospital—feeling never weary or uneasy. Day six my belly began aching slightly—yet persisting reciting the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra'—just completing one round—waters broke—belly aching fiercely—still persisting reciting the Buddha's name.}
{align:justify|In the birth room about fifteen minutes—doctors said the baby's head showed—I still kept reciting the Buddha's name—bearing a chubby boy easily.}
{align:justify|The babe's first sight was his father—the whole family boundlessly glad—while I rejoiced unspeakably.}
{align:justify|Friends hearing I bore male—crowding bearing gifts congratulating and giving all kinds: pig trotters, carp, birds, chickens… guests gone, I gave surviving creatures to family releasing them all.}
{align:justify|I nursed—but too little milk. Family all said my meatless diet—no pig-trotter soup—explained scant milk.}
{align:justify|Over-worrying for my son, I no longer firmly kept precepts—thinking: "Eating triple-clean meat surely harms nothing."}
{align:justify|Meat-eaten, indeed more milk came—but my child suckling a little, refused more—then fell ill. Doctors called it excess red blood cells—unabating, transfer to 'Beijing Hospital' must follow.}
{align:justify|But the boy's illness ever grew—my milk abundant, yet he refused suckling—I must feed him other things—thinking: "Why, newly born, my milk scant yet he suckled smacking? Now milk abundant—why does he stubbornly refuse? I racked brains seeking causes… 'Can pig-trotter soup explain it? Plainly I vowed—promising vegetarianism—begging Buddhas and Bodhisattvas grant me a clever, wise boy… but I broke vows—meat-eating again. I used pig-trotter soup urging milk—so my milk too tainted meaty foulness—the babe sucking disagreed—so fell ill… Precisely so he surely refuses suckling?'…"}
{align:justify|Thinking thus, lying in bed, suddenly I feared trembling—heart full of remorse—tears streaming… I rose donning my ceremonial robe—stepping down—kneeling before Buddhas repenting and re-vowing—firmly from this hour: 'vowing quitting meaty foods utterly—praying the babe soon past peril'…}
{align:justify|Repented and vowed done, I tried nursing…}
{align:justify|Oh—joy—the babe suckled—when doctors drew blood testing—excess red cells ceased—truly miracles past thought!}
{align:justify|Afterward I gave three hundred thousand alms—offerings—good works dedicating blessings to the babe.}
{align:justify|Now the babe is eighteen months—daily automatically kneeling before Buddhas kowtowing worship. The babe is especially intelligent—face bright—fair as pearls and jade—all who see love him.}
{align:justify|Since then ever after, I believe deeper: 'Buddhism is life's and the path's treasure'—only practicing teachings accordingly—surely reaping boundless benefit.}
{align:justify|Here too I thank the great kindness of masters and laypeople wholeheartedly aiding us…}
{align:justify|Quả Anh}
{align:justify|Quả Khanh adds: this is a true story—proving the Buddha's superb wisdom—further proving the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' a destiny- and karma-transforming sutra most wondrous. This is wholly true, no falsehood. Hopefully readers may vow reciting the 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' daily one round or weekly one round—surely bringing you and family wondrous past-thought goods—noting: reciters must strictly keep precepts—utterly quit meaty foods, lifelong vegetarianism—then boundless merit exists and triple-world exiting hopes.}
{align:justify|Besides, this story also wakes us: never betray, defy vows!}
{align:justify|ADVERSE AFFINITIES BETWEEN PARENTS AND CHILDREN}
{align:justify|In present life, meeting many unpleasing matters—unclear of causes, unseeing past-created faults—minds suffer deeper. The Buddha often teaches cause and effect in many sutras—wholly true, no falsehood! Sowing one seed as cause—conditions ripening, meeting… fruits—whether wholesome or evil seeds!}
{align:justify|If all hearing Buddhist teachings believed, accepted, practiced accordingly—knowing gathering minds keeping precepts—purifying body, speech, mind—careful from words to deeds—thoughts ever wholly wholesome—surely worldly people would meet little misery. The true cause-and-effect story below has rung an alarm bell—good and evil retribution ever present—following us like shadows.}
{align:justify|A middle-aged woman, face miserable, gloomy. Her gaze strengthless, she weakly told me:}
{align:justify|"Quả Hồng—I bear a hard matter begging your guidance!"}
{align:justify|"Please speak!"}
{align:justify|She pointed at a middle-aged man sitting behind her, introducing:}
{align:justify|"This is my husband—married, we bore one boy—work busy—no time tending the babe—so gave him to grandmother nursing for us. Since grandmother single-handedly raised him, grandmother-grandchild feelings are most warm. Loving him too much, she grabbed doing everything instead—letting his hands touch nothing. Though the boy was eight, nine already—still she washed his face, combed his hair, bathed him… Even at ten, at meals she still fed him—habituating him—unfed, uneating."}
{align:justify|When the boy turned thirteen, grandmother's health ever weakening—old—willing but strengthless—no longer tending her grandchild—so handing the boy back to our care.}
{align:justify|The boy, a dozen years habituated to dependent living—husband and I early saw this error—so worried greatly. Also advised grandmother and grandchild—but hopeless correcting that bad state. Seizing the receiving chance, we firmly resolved reforming, straightening our son toward self-reliance, better living…}
{align:justify|Unexpectedly, frets vied descending—discovering we were nearly hopeless reforming our son. Mornings rising—undressed by us, unmoving. Thirteen already—even brushing teeth, washing face… we must still serve him. And at meals—unless food 'offered' to his mouth—uneating—silently hugging his schoolbag straight to school.}
{align:justify|Now the boy is sixteen—but washing face, bathing… (pardon) even… wiping bottoms must be parents serving, attending all! Telling you shames and grieves us—for now all is nearly hopeless reforming… Yet watching him at school—with schoolmates in all respects fine—nothing abnormal.}
{align:justify|Husband and I grieve miserably thinking of our son's strange home displays: that manner—awaiting parents bringing clothes draping—then extending hands—rice offered to lips—then opening mouths—never caring chopsticks—refusing hands touching any work… this emperor-like life—lolling awaiting service—hereafter how can he stand firm in society—how survive? His father and I daily shed tears—misery past caring to live! Please save us!…"}
{align:justify|Facing the haggard mother—miserable past caring to live—my heart tangled a hundred ways… observing parents pouring hearts raising children, I understood why they repay such heavy retribution—sighs unstoppable! Deeper savoring cause and effect's law—never erring a hair—this couple's story is living proof—ringing alarm bells—reminding all:}
{align:justify|"'In ancient times, a young master of a great rich house—his mother doting utterly—cherishing like pearls and jade. Tending her son most considerately—ordering servants ever waiting around. Besides the nurse tending the young master—his mother even assigned a maid and a manservant together befriending, playing, serving him. The young master, these two servants ever closely attending—bore warm feelings—trusting and loving them like kin.}
{align:justify|Time smoothly passing—the young master gradually maturing—toward these two servants love ever growing—so most favoring, indulging them.}
{align:justify|So this serving pair—exploiting the young master's trust—freely bullied and blustered. They did much cunning wrong—often stealing his money and goods selling, spending, pleasure-seeking to content… Their stolen assets from the young master—too many to count. These two servants bore never shame or repentance—instead ever swelling slippery deceit—plotting together 'grabbing one huge haul from the young master then fleeing'… but their schemes never succeeded as wished…}
{align:justify|Time passing… rebirth's wheel turning. 'Such causes, such fruits!'… This life, the pair of greedy thieving servants of the young master of old—have become husband and wife—while the young master is reborn their son. Their raising children unusually toilsome—precisely repaying bygone faults—for they owe him far too much—too much to ever count.}
{align:justify|The young master's former-life mother—this life reborn grandmother—still keeping loving doting on him exactly as before. As for the 'servant' couple—toward their former mistress, now mother—naturally ever meekly obeying—never daring argue—mother decreeing, only 'yes' known—reverent respect for her ever latent within.}
{align:justify|Seeing grandmother doting overfond, they deemed it natural—but later wishing to discipline their son—no longer in time. From former lives' evil seeds sown—this life fruits ripened—and this is the evil retribution husband and wife together must bear!"}
{align:justify|Watching the mother sitting sobbing choked, I gently advised:}
{align:justify|"Never resent your own child—much less loathe life—but willingly accept. Now, clearly understanding karmic sources—both should sincerely repent former lives' sins. For long ago failing servants' duties—instead greedily stealing masters' goods—seizing excessively spending to content… Who knew this life must still repay all in full—principal and interest to the master—so misery past telling!…"}
{align:justify|Starting now ever after—both must before Buddhas sincerely repent bygone faults. Surely keep precepts, vegetarianism—daily for the former-life young master reciting one 'Ksitigarbha Sutra' round—hereby aiding, growing his wisdom-life blessings. Meanwhile, must beg grandmother advising her grandchild for you: 'Grown—tend and care for yourself! Refusing self-reliance—hereafter becoming useless—society's refuse!'…"}
{align:justify|If grandmother agrees—willing advising her grandchild—your son shall surely obey. You two must strive repenting, reciting sutras—vegetarianism, reforming… Done—your boy shall surely change swiftly. For his nature is never lazy, unwilling working—but karmic powers influencing, ruling—so unacting!"}
{align:justify|I finished speaking—fine medicine given—applying and practicing is yours! Done as I advise—all shall turn well!}
{align:justify|This true cause-and-effect story weighs my heart—thinking of today's world: 'How many families meet unfilial children—drug addicts—living dazed—or day and night sitting in internet cafés never caring home? Early loves then abortions—unwed hastily trial-living, cohabiting… besides wrong education's influence and base surroundings' pull—does karmic retribution partly rule within? Fathers and mothers meeting like contrary scenes should examine themselves'…"}
{align:justify|Precisely creating evil karma draws miseries descending… so all of us must vow: lifetime after lifetime keeping five precepts—practicing ten goods—fulfilling duties—never greedy, never craving—never selfish, self-serving.}
{align:justify|Know: we are sinking and floating in the six realms—ever cycling rebirth in birth-death's bitter sea—then forging kinships, forging enmities—owing debts, repaying debts… Truly you forever hide never—escape never—cause-and-effect nets cast most dense and subtle through heaven and earth!}
{align:justify|Only receiving the Buddha's teachings and practicing accordingly hopes liberation one day.}
{align:justify|Quả Hồng}
{align:justify|KILLING FOXES, MEETING RETRIBUTION}
{align:justify|This story my grandmother told—with her consent, I write it sharing with dear readers. You must surely 'End all evil, practice all good.' Story figures are all true—all my grandmother witnessed. Here is grandmother's original telling:}
{align:justify|"In 1951 Beijing newly liberated—then I was about five or six—dwelling with father outside Yongding Gate. Yongding Gate then was still a desolate remote land. Facing our house lived the Tat family—husband, wife, children and grandchildren together.}
{align:justify|Then behind their house stood many jujube trees—I often with little friends played there picking jujubes eating.}
{align:justify|One day I heard old Tat boast: behind his house foxes appeared—he resolved trapping foxes.}
{align:justify|Within days, I heard old Tat caught a fox. Moreover he flayed it alive skinning for a neck-scarf.}
{align:justify|Days later I visited old Tat's house playing—just inside the courtyard meeting Aunt Bien—a neighbor of old Tat's.}
{align:justify|She said:}
{align:justify|"Old Tat has fallen ill bedridden—walking no more!"}
{align:justify|I ran straight within—then his son and grandson stood bedside tending.}
{align:justify|Strange—old Tat normally a sturdy man—over one meter seven—but now seeing him lying huddled on bed—shrunk to only seventy, eighty centimeters—moreover whole body trembling—face deformed—mouth straining stammering speechless. Hearing his family tell:}
{align:justify|"Last night he still howled, wailing most piteously…"}
{align:justify|We little ones hearing so feared—together turned tail running.}
{align:justify|That was the last time I saw old Tat—for days later he passed. They say at last breath his body dried up—limbs like foxes'—face too like foxes'.}
{align:justify|The family coffined him in no coffin—only a wooden box. Hearing neighbor Bien tell:}
{align:justify|"That night he died—many foxes gathered in his courtyard—both crying and leaping"…}
{align:justify|Hearing the story done, I asked:}
{align:justify|"Did you misremember telling inaccurately?"}
{align:justify|She answered:}
{align:justify|"Absolutely not! This dreadful happening ever haunts, printed deep in her mind. All her life she can never forget old Tat lying sick on bed!"}
{align:justify|My grandmother then told the following story:}
{align:justify|THE UNFORTUNATE BRIDE}
{align:justify|In 1965 I was eighteen—joining the Youth Corps—stationed at Hoang Duong, Mongolia. In the village, near our team, lived the Pham family. The Phams had an eldest son and a youngest daughter. The Pham son wed a Shaanxi wife (her name I no longer clearly recall)—orphaned young—formerly dwelling with her brother and sister-in-law—gentle-natured, most capable and upright.}
{align:justify|Husband and wife loved each other most closely. But married long without children, the bride suffered her unmarried sister-in-law joining her mother-in-law treating her most ill—pointing at cats scolding dogs—hinting, breathing bitter sarcastic words, implying: 'Our house, ill-fated, wedded the thing that cannot bear!'"}
{align:justify|They often abused and tormented the bride—feeding only leftovers. New Year came, yet no new clothes sewn—but this gentle, honest, simple bride never once retorted—enduring all cruel torments most well.}
{align:justify|Years later, the sister-in-law neared marriage—the harsh mother-in-law told the bride:}
{align:justify|"'You ill-omened thing! Forbidden from attending my daughter's wedding!'"}
{align:justify|She nagged, humiliated on… grieving the bride most—at last unbearable—indignant, she drank poison suicide—then from her room crawling outside—to before her mother-in-law's door, expiring.}
{align:justify|Seeing it, the mother-in-law feared greatly—hastily seeking a sorceress—begging suppression aid. The medium said this bride was a petty person—coffined, great long iron nails must pin her limbs—stones stuffed in her mouth, pressed on her chest… to suppress her—leaving her forever unable rising returning… else the whole family suffers hauntings!}
{align:justify|The mother-in-law at once ordered the blacksmith many great long iron nails… then nailing the bride's corpse—then stones stuffed in her mouth, pressed on her chest—then burying the corpse underground.}
{align:justify|When her brother and sister-in-law visited—asking why their sister died? The Pham house explained evasively—unceasing scorning the bride as base, vile girls… The brother's couple, little schooled, understanding little—so resigned. The Pham house compensated them a little money then dismissed them.}
{align:justify|As proverbs say 'Seeing injustice on the road, all lend feet' (meeting roadside wrongs, all around join hands)…}
{align:justify|Neighboring folk learning the matter—sought the Pham house proposing: the dead must surely receive a settled answer—never leaving her departing in obscurity, so wronged!}
{align:justify|The Phams, reason-spent, must let neighbors carry the husband to a great hospital examining. Result: the husband bore a no-sperm condition—so never fathering. So the bride was wrongly scolded—a piteous death—wholly unaware this cause.}
{align:justify|Days later, grandmother- and father-in-law suddenly both suffered half-body paralysis—he left-paralyzed, she right-paralyzed. As for the old blacksmith—nights never sleeping—he lamented to all:}
{align:justify|"'Every night a loose-haired woman stands at my house—clattering bowls and cups uproariously!'…"}
{align:justify|He knew his foolish share in virtueless work (shaming conscience)—so retired to his Hunan home village.}
{align:justify|Before long came news: mountain logging, he fell dead.}
{align:justify|The sister-in-law named Kim—once sturdy—eating most heartily—one day suddenly quit food and drink—hospital examination revealing end-stage cancer—days later dropping dead.}
{align:justify|The daughter dead—the half-paralyzed old couple later, on a home-village parents-visiting trip—midway were struck dead by a vehicle.}
{align:justify|The unfortunate bride's husband—understanding all sources forming today's tragedy—regretted never examining sooner… so before his wife's grave, weeping, speaking apologies… having her corpse exhumed removing all nails and stones—changing her into new clothes.}
{align:justify|Afterward the Pham house held only him alone.}
{align:justify|As for the sorceress, I know not—for this neighboring house stood near the corps—so I know only this much. But surely the sorceress shall receive the retribution she deserves.}
{align:justify|MY SECOND UNCLE}
{align:justify|I pondered very long—mustering courage to tell a cause-and-effect story touching my elders. Rightly, as a junior telling elders' tales is truly disrespectful—but I hope this story's contents shall mightily warn all and deepen faith in cause and effect.}
{align:justify|Now I tell of my Second Uncle: 'In my home village Second Uncle is society-recognized as an exemplary good man—honest, loyal-natured—yet his end was unwholesome: in 2005 he fell gravely ill and passed.}
{align:justify|Second Uncle was a Party cadre—diligent all his life. Living, he served as Provincial Party Secretary of the State Security Bureau.}
{align:justify|Born in 1949, Uncle served on the provincial executive committee—just and clean-natured. Then city-level leading cadres were just granted private cars—each with own car and driver. Second Uncle too was granted one—yet rarely used it—often cycling to work—while his driver must drive trailing behind him. Making our clan all laugh at this: private cars granted, yet refusing rides—'blessed yet unknowing enjoyment!'…}
{align:justify|Outside officials too deemed him old-fashioned—unknowing keeping with times—excessively upright—so working went poorly—often criticized.}
{align:justify|Later he caught a rare strange illness: bloody urine and stools—then intestinal hemorrhage—undergoing many surgeries—even once, barely operated, bleeding torrentially—long hemorrhaging—malnourished—moreover much cut open—gravely ill till all understood his organs had rotted, ruined…}
{align:justify|Grave illness racked him two, three years—at last every blood vessel too burst and bled—he passed in utmost painful torment at fifty.}
{align:justify|He left amid unceasing buzzing public talk. All deemed him a good cadre rare in a century—yet an honest loyal man—lifelong never erring—why did his end bear so piteous illness?! (While corrupt officials opposing him thrived in power—freely enjoying pleasures, in peace)… Then where is fair karmic retribution?…}
{align:justify|Later I gradually understood all causes…}
{align:justify|Since Uncle served the State Security Bureau, guns and ammunition abounded… plus his nature loved hunting—often mountain hunting and dam fishing…}
{align:justify|Hence, over a dozen years, he wantonly killed—slaying countless lives—so at last drew piteous painful retribution… for with ready guns and ammunition, he massacred many creatures: leaving them bleeding unceasing—burst bellies, pierced intestines—organs protruding all… precisely why his end bore strange painful illness—scenes exactly like that!'…}
{align:justify|Telling thus far, I drew one conclusion: however cleanly, justly you live—lifelong straight and upright—yet planting killing seeds—when retribution arrives, still you must bear it—never through other good deeds shielding, escaping sins…}
{align:justify|All loving killing or rejoicing joining killings—surely take warning…}
{align:justify|Cause and effect's law is absolutely fair—each person's straits project their created karma. Some evil yet see no retribution—because: past or former times they did much good—so this present life—that fortunate blessings remain most great—sufficient suppressing evil fruits from ripening (naturally their evil deeds never vanish—or perhaps meanwhile they secretly do good unknown to us—unseen, or undeemed good—for instance, filial piety counts as great good).}
{align:justify|So living, sow good seeds clean and whole—never live drunken, die dreaming… Living disbelieving cause and effect—carelessly creating evil—consequences most piteous… Please be careful!}
{align:justify|THE STORY THAT MADE ME WEEP}
{align:justify|That autumn I was twelve—my family's most piteous time: Father gravely ill, operated—working ability lost. My Second Brother failed university exams—while Mother misused pesticides—ten acres of heading rice lost. Also then I fell gravely anemic—gasping—needing urgent transfusion.}
{align:justify|Our house then drowned in debt—Mother, brow-knitting, pondered every way—yet knew not where to find my transfusion money.}
{align:justify|While the family fretted over my critical illness—neighbor Granny Ly stepped over—gravely telling my mother:}
{align:justify|"'Fourth Aunt—hurry rearward and see—your sow has farrowed!'"}
{align:justify|Mother, reminded, woke with a start—hastily running to the rear pigpen—seeing the sow licking clean blood off her piglets—Mother burst crying. She rejoiced gratefully—for the sow's litter arrived rescuing the family in time—so planting a Money Tree by the pen thanking…}
{align:justify|When Mother helped the sow farrow done—seeing the sow tired to foaming mouth—panting unceasing… Mother knew her age—having enriched our house near nine years… this litter numbered sixteen—seeing Mother-sow tired—hastily sending my brother fetching soy milk for her drinking—the sow gratefully flicking both ears—Mother viewing the rosy chubby piglet litter—telling my brother:}
{align:justify|"'This time all well—count Son saved…'"}
{align:justify|But 'distant water saves no near fire'—piglets must nurse at least three months before selling—while my illness daily worsened—barely ten days piglets born—then I fell gravely ill—unable leaving bed. Then Mother's heart burned like fire—pacing endlessly indoors—at last sighing telling my brother:}
{align:justify|"'Tuyen—go villageward inviting Butcher Vuong—we sell the sow for Son's treatment money…'"}
{align:justify|My brother hearing Mother—sadly standing transfixed—then dissuading:}
{align:justify|"'Mother—the sow still lives and piglets need suckling… Son's illness matters—but Mother-sow too needs living…'"}
{align:justify|But at last, my brother, eyes brimmed with tears… had to slink villageward seeking Butcher Vuong.}
{align:justify|Mother hastily built a stove—setting a great pot by the pigpen—tending fire boiling water…}
{align:justify|When Butcher Vuong came—nearing the pen eyeing observing—sighing telling my mother:}
{align:justify|"'Fourth Aunt—count me aiding your merit… truly even killing this sow—little meat comes!'"}
{align:justify|Water boiled bubbling—Mother opened the pen. The sow as if foreknowing—though Mother held milk calling coaxing again and again—still stood transfixed in the pen—refusing out. Alternately eyeing Mother—then my brother—then unceasing licking her piglets sporting chasing within the pen… Suddenly she dropped with a 'thud'—uttering 'grunt grunt' nursing-call signals—the piglets hearing—vying running lying ranked round her belly suckling smacking…}
{align:justify|Mother sighed telling Butcher Vuong:}
{align:justify|"'Then please wait! Let her nurse her young one last time?…'" }
{align:justify|Butcher Vuong nodded.}
{align:justify|Piglets, milk-drunk, scattered playing—but Mother-sow unceasing calling—summoning young to suckle… till the last piglet left her teats—then Mother-sow rose—slowly walking outside. Mother-sow out of the pen—yet turning back eyeing her brood with lingering love… my mother, moved by Mother-sow's love—turning away, wiping tears… while my brother stood rooted…}
{align:justify|Butcher Vuong muttered alone:}
{align:justify|"'Never have I seen so clever a sow—temperament humanlike!'"}
{align:justify|Mother-sow alternately eyeing Mother—then my brother and Butcher Vuong—then suddenly turning—darting into the pen—running round the pen like mad. Mother seeing so, puzzled eyeing—asking Butcher:}
{align:justify|"Mother-sow… wishes doing what?"}
{align:justify|Mr. Vuong shook his head—meaning unknowing…}
{align:justify|Running a while—all discovered Mother-sow's twin milk rows swollen sagging—white milk streams flowing… So Mother-sow ran thus stimulating milk flowing—wanting her young drinking full once more—but though Mother-sow called—naive piglets stirred not…}
{align:justify|My brother, moved by Mother-sow's noble act to streaming tears—a twenty-year-old youth, he burst crying—pleading Mother aloud:}
{align:justify|"'Mother—boo hoo!… I beg you—kill not Mother-sow—will you? Son's illness—let me find ways… boo hoo!…'"}
{align:justify|Butcher Vuong silently shouldered his tools leaving—Mother, tears streaming, nodding—then Mother-sow, costing her owners no calling effort—stepped out of the pigpen…}
{align:justify|Afterward, transfusion saved me—my brother donating blood for me.}
{align:justify|Later, Mother-sow bore our house seven, eight more litters—in the final farrowing, Mother-sow died of labor— the whole house silently shedding tears—at last Mother proposing burying her under the tree by the pigpen…}
{align:justify|To now, each time we siblings return home visiting parents—ever fondly eyeing the great tree, lush-branched beside the pigpen—fondly recalling the faithful sow our house once raised.}
{align:justify|I earnestly beg you all to eat vegetarian—never for one tasty moment—sacrificing small creatures' parents—or eating young or ending their lives…}
{align:justify|Sincerely thanking all.}
{align:justify|Buddhist Thai Son}
{align:justify|MY FRIEND'S YOUNGEST UNCLE}
{align:justify|Friends all know I believe in Buddhism—close playmates, whatever Buddhist questions arise ask me—or tell me their families' happenings.}
{align:justify|Today I tell one (among my friends' stories)—hoping your reading grows boundless wholesome faith.}
{align:justify|This happened when I entered university. That noon, lunch done, spare time—I meant seeking friends chatting—when suddenly meeting Sister B—she patted my shoulder asking:}
{align:justify|"'Heard you follow Buddhism?'"}
{align:justify|"'Yes.'"}
{align:justify|So B, interested, asked me several Buddhist matters—then I was most surprised and delighted. Surprised because: B and I were never close—schoolmates' ties mere few shared meals. B, knowing I believe in Buddhism, only showed respect. But delighted: for B, no Buddhist believer—now showing keen interest…}
{align:justify|Then I asked B:}
{align:justify|"'Why wish Buddhist understanding? No longer calling it feudal superstition?'…"}
{align:justify|So B told me a true story befalling her uncle.}
{align:justify|B's youngest uncle lived most virtuously—lofty-natured—gentle conduct—strictly keeping five precepts—vigorously practicing—decades as one day—a person fellow practitioners utmost revered.}
{align:justify|Young, Uncle worked abroad—heaven never fails the kind-hearted—at last Uncle succeeded—career glorious. Grown rich, Uncle at once sponsored parents, wife, children abroad living. Never did Uncle, rich, despise poor kin. Uncle ever treated all genial, kindly, humble.}
{align:justify|But a kind Buddhist like Uncle—suddenly fell cancer—and end-stage besides.}
{align:justify|This was like a 'home-exploding bomb'—Uncle's kin all shocked—none willing believing: so good a man meeting such evil retribution! Even some began doubting Buddhism—blaming heaven eyeless—why 'good men gain no good retribution?'…}
{align:justify|But B's youngest uncle stayed calm—understanding all as former lives' karmic causes and fruits. Just as doctors declared Uncle's illness incurable… not only was Uncle most composed—but also consoled kin and friends. (Though Uncle loathed abandoning parents, wife, children—yet once impermanence comes—who can escape?)…}
{align:justify|Uncle's practicing liturgy endured—decades as one day—wholly doing good, storing virtue—yet now meeting this evil retribution!}
{align:justify|All blamed old heaven unjust—but Uncle never raised resentful thoughts—even thinking though soon dying—still must use remaining time living well… meaning treating people more kindly—striving doing more good… then single-mindedly reciting the Buddha's name seeking rebirth.}
{align:justify|Suddenly a special inconceivable matter arose (just as B told it, I hearing scarcely dared believe. But this is a true story—having happened).}
{align:justify|One day, youngest uncle with family supermarket shopping—while Uncle strolled within the supermarket—suddenly feeling dizzy, bleary—vaguely seeing a white-robed old man approaching Uncle—holding up a fruit from the fruit rows telling Uncle:}
{align:justify|"'Quickly eat this fruit! Eat at once!'"}
{align:justify|Just as youngest uncle opened his mouth asking: Why?… the old man vanished. At once Uncle's spirit woke—more amazed seeing that fruit lying in his hand.}
{align:justify|Youngest Uncle looked around seeking… hastily asking kin:}
{align:justify|"'See the white-robed old man? Plainly here just now—where vanished?…'"}
{align:justify|The family all eyed Uncle amazed—answering together they saw no one—only seeing Uncle holding fruit in hand, muttering alone!}
{align:justify|This matter was truly puzzling—Uncle said nothing more—hastily running to the supermarket gate buying this fruit kind home eating—then Uncle thought little more.}
{align:justify|Unexpectedly, the end-stage cancer hospitals declared 'incurable'—after Uncle ate that ordinary (yet obscure-origin) fruit—then cancer cells wholly vanished—illness thereby healed.}
{align:justify|How does science explain this?}
{align:justify|After that strange happening, youngest uncle's whole family began believing Buddhism utterly. Together understanding the Bodhisattva's compassionate protection—She used great majestic power—within darkness secretly saving Uncle's life. This deeply moved kin domestic and abroad…}
{align:justify|Also thereby I learned the story—writing it sharing with all!}
{align:justify|Lien Hoa Tieu Binh}
{align:justify|THE FIERCE NEIGHBOR}
{align:justify|This is a story I heard grandmother tell (the persons concerned still live—moreover dwelling in my Nantou homeland—next door to my house). The story's contents run thus:}
{align:justify|My neighboring old man has three brothers—all disabled: one deaf, one mute, one lame. Their father long ago was also my grandmother's neighbor. They say his temper was fiery, cruel—loving tormenting and abusing creatures. Since all three born sons were disabled—he raged most unjustly—blaming heaven, scolding earth.}
{align:justify|Too puzzled, one day he asked the gods tracing causes clearly. At last this answer came:}
{align:justify|"'The cause: long ago unwed—his house raised a pregnant sow—it pen-broke running off. Fierce-natured, grass-cutting cleaver in hand—he swung chopping the sow—the sow screamed piteously—dropping writhing convulsing—blood spurting, dying—the piglets in her belly too gushed bloody, dying together. Count one blade of his ending several lives. Since he planted disaster's root there—so today bearing three sons in succession, all disabled'…"}
{align:justify|His deaf son (now also my home village neighbor) later too bore three sons—of whom two are hard of hearing—only one normal—while this man knows lifelong vegetarianism and most diligently bows to Buddhas.}
{align:justify|They say the two hard-of-hearing brothers' children later too suffer flawed senses, crippled limbs.}
{align:justify|Through this story, killing's retribution proves most dreadful. By my grandmother's word, this cause-and-effect story is a thousand true, ten thousand true.}
{align:justify|Virtue Magazine No. 44 – January 15, 1983}
{align:justify|Tang Chanh Hue}
{align:justify|AFFINITIES BETWEEN BODHISATTVAS AND ME}
{align:justify|This happened late 2003—then I guided tours at the Sanya company in Hainan.}
{align:justify|Daily I drove receiving guests—touring them round islands or sights. Then I loved reciting the 'Great Compassion Mantra'—whenever driving I often played mantra-recitation disks—tourists too loved listening—especially old folks—they loved it more.}
{align:justify|Daily I played tirelessly—for me, no need memorizing—I listened till fluent—often murmuring along—perhaps many-lives' good roots.}
{align:justify|Once, I toured Fenghuang Ancient Town in Hunan—staying three days—then preparing for the airport flying home.}
{align:justify|That morning, weather poor—pouring rain. I bustled renting a car—the driver asked 350 coins—then together we headed for the airport.}
{align:justify|Hunan folk all know—though scenery here boasts fine mountains—heavy rains spell traveling disasters. Meaning driving meeting rains is most perilous. (For mountain mud and rocks streaming down causing landslides or blocking roads—not only hindering traffic but wrecking vehicles, killing people).}
{align:justify|I, a far guest, knew nothing of this—boarding, then sitting eyes closed—heart unceasing reciting the 'Great Compassion Mantra.' I loved reciting mantras—no purposes at all—never seeking anything—only habitual reciting—free, reciting so. Never knowing reciting mantras would bring me what goods? In short—glad-hearted—I recited the 'Great Compassion Mantra' unceasing. One might say perhaps the 'Great Compassion Mantra' lent me peaceful blissful feelings—that is all.}
{align:justify|From Fenghuang Ancient Town to the airport takes three hours. Forty minutes out of Fenghuang—things happened. Mountain earth and sand, heavy rains forming mighty flows—earth and rocks pouring down—even aiming straight at our vehicle assaulting—front and rear both dented, crushed: windows shattered—vehicle gravely wrecked—but luckily driver and I were safe and sound—then my heart bore never fear—even thinking: 'Today my luck truly good—meeting great perilous disaster yet escaping—not losing life'…}
{align:justify|Years later, studying Buddhism I understood clearly: so not my luck—meeting great disaster escaping death—but Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva secretly protecting—She knew my fate held this disaster—and saved our lives. For the 'Great Compassion Mantra' once taught:}
{align:justify|"'Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva made great vows saving beings—then She rose from First Ground to Tenth Ground (a thousand eyes, a thousand hands manifesting the Bodhisattva's great loving compassion). Seeing beings anywhere bearing misery and disaster—She arrives there aiding.'"}
{align:justify|The sutra says: 'Whoever sincerely recites the Great Compassion Mantra shall ever gain Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin)'s protection'… this is a thousand true, ten thousand true—absolutely never doubt. Perhaps you disbelieve, calling my safety chance? Then I tell one more story—proving the Bodhisattva has 'eyed' me.}
{align:justify|This happened recently. My daughter tested into high school—though studies middling—yet she studies hard and spirited. She ever longed—hoping testing into a famed downtown school. For: 'Good virtuous friends flock—birds nesting phoenixes also fly far'… meeting fine surroundings—studies and conduct shall ascend.}
{align:justify|Before exams, my daughter told her homeroom teacher her wishes—filling her wished school's name: 'Shanghai Specialized High School' (a famed school—admission standards most high—most hard entering)… The teacher glancing—eyes bulging amazed—eyeing her head to toe, saying:}
{align:justify|"'You must be mad! Middling studies like yours never enter this school!'"}
{align:justify|So he flatly refused.}
{align:justify|But my daughter never yielded—she filled another school's name—also downtown—but admission standards harsher, harder than the former… This school's learning surroundings are most supreme—gathering all fine teachers—conduct and character most noble—many famed persons emerging from this school. Many illustrious families too send children studying here.}
{align:justify|The homeroom teacher seeing her writing so (thinking her 'madness' past cure) judged:}
{align:justify|"'Fine—grant your wish! Anyway I dare affirm you never enter here! But fine—dream vain dreams a little! Wasted efforts—strive bearing them, hear!'"}
{align:justify|My child home, heart most unhappy—grieved weeping telling me:}
{align:justify|"'Dad—the homeroom teacher scorns me—laughing seeing my filled school name—but truly I strive—I have struggled unceasing—boo hoo…'"}
{align:justify|I soothed, consoling her:}
{align:justify|"'Darling daughter—ancients often say: 'Where there's will, there's way'… High ambitions—strive studying hard—surely wishes granted. This is called 'Heaven's path repays the earnest!'… Dad promises support—doing many good deeds for you—widely storing hidden virtues dedicating blessings to you!'"}
{align:justify|Throughout my child's exams, I kept my promise. Daily at two in the morning I rose reciting sutras, reciting the Buddha's name—but these days for my child reverently reciting the 'Diamond Sutra'—dedicating this merit especially to her. For the 'Diamond Sutra' opens great wisdom—I hoped my daughter would dissolve evil karma—grow blessed wisdom—gain Buddhas' protection…}
{align:justify|At evening liturgy, I sincerely recited the 'Great Compassion Mantra'—single-hearted begging Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva blessing my child—granting her wishes.}
{align:justify|A week before results, I dreamed Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva dream-announcing—saying my child scored 597 points.}
{align:justify|Morning, at once I reported this glad news to wife and child. None believed. My daughter even said:}
{align:justify|This exam—passing or not—I dare think no more—for literature I did poorly. Perhaps this dream-omen 'inverts' reality, Dad!}
{align:justify|Seven days later, my child checked scores online—heart most fearful—heart drumming like battle drums (one might use 'soul trembling, heart startled' picturing execution-ground tension picturing it)… But she plainly saw on the computer screen her scores most high: literature exceeding by 15 points. I said:}
{align:justify|"'Quick—fetch the calculator adding all to test?'"}
{align:justify|After a while adding—exactly 597 points—neither more nor less.}
{align:justify|I hastened reminding:}
{align:justify|"'Remember last week Dad announced you results just so? Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva dream-announced—saying you passed—597 points!'"}
{align:justify|Then, mother and daughter both dazed a while—then extreme joy—the family glad past all telling—for the child's wish testing into the downtown high-level school came true. My daughter rejoiced speechless—stammering:}
{align:justify|"'Dad… Dad!…'"}
{align:justify|I said:}
{align:justify|"'Quick—bow thanking Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva!'"}
{align:justify|Our whole family hastened burning incense bowing—I bowed Her a hundred times—voicing boundless gratitude.}
{align:justify|I myself daily recite the Buddha's name fifteen hours—evenings reading the 'Great Compassion Mantra's' vow-text—then reciting 5 Great Compassion Mantras—then reciting Amitabha Buddha! Next reciting Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva!…}
{align:justify|On earth no luck is causeless—people meeting crucial moments—without their own blessings guarding them—can only rely on noble persons aiding…}
{align:justify|I am a common mortal of many sins—karmic obstructions deep—blessings thin, wisdom scant—without Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva saving—my life would long be done—drifting in evil destinies bearing misery… Plainly seen—my noble person is Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva! This too truly proves Her inconceivable majestic powers—'a thousand places praying—a thousand places answering; mercy's boat ferrying beings across misery's sea'!}
{align:justify|I share my true experiences—hoping Dharma friends toward Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva grow deeper faith. Meeting crucial, perilous moments—sincerely earnestly beg Her blessings. Believe deep, never doubt—wholeheartedly reciting Her name sweeping scattered deluded thoughts—helping minds clear merging as one—never harboring scattered mad thoughts.}
{align:justify|The Great Compassion Sutra teaches: vowing daily reciting about 5 Great Compassion Mantras—may help dissolve a hundred thousand myriad million eons' grave birth-death sins—at life's end—reborn as wished—even reaching the Western Pure Land World. If this vow unfulfilled—She never becomes Buddha. Vowing daily reciting about 5 Great Compassion Mantras—at life's end falling into three evil destinies—She never becomes Buddha.}
{align:justify|(Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva truly long became Buddha—named True Dharma Brightness Tathagata—Her words wholly true, never false).}
{align:justify|Layman Lam Long Dat}

{align:justify|If you say the Buddha is compassionate, why does He wait until sentient beings cry out and invoke His name before saving them? It is like the sun and moon shining their light for everyone, but whoever wishes to receive it must open the door. If the door stays tightly shut, the light cannot shine in.}
{align:justify|Radio and television stations broadcast on every channel for us to watch, yet we must tune in to the right frequency to see anything. Likewise, to connect with the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas we must invoke Their names: during the time we recite, our scattered thoughts subside, our minds grow clear, and communion with the Buddhas becomes easy. That is why we must invoke Their names.}
{align:justify|As for the father in this story, he is a model Buddhist disciple. Ordinarily, those who do not know the Dharma tend to chase after material desires, wealth and beauty, and pay little attention to their children and families. But the father here, besides cultivating himself through recitation, cares deeply and loves his child dearly. When his child went to sit for the exams, the father vowed to discipline himself, do good deeds, create blessings and accumulate hidden merit, dedicating it all to his child.}
{align:justify|Thus, once one becomes a Buddhist disciple, even without leaving the household life, one finds much greater happiness: thanks to the resolve to keep the precepts, husband and wife treat each other with faithfulness, the family lives in harmony and warmth, and life overflows with love. Because they create no evil and their hearts remain wholly wholesome, they enjoy blessings and joy right in this very life. This is precisely the benefit of Buddhist families.}
{align:justify|SIX STORIES OF CAUSE AND EFFECT}
{align:justify|Recently, a Mr. Truong residing in Hokou Village, Hsinchu County, wrote a letter recounting many stories of cause and effect that he had personally witnessed or heard told. He affirms that these stories are entirely true—real people, real events, with solid evidence—and asks that they be published so the world may take warning. He hopes that through them everyone will learn many good lessons and deepen their faith in cause and effect, so as to "Do no evil, and practice all that is good," and thereby improve the moral climate of society.}
{align:justify|Now I shall recount each story in order:}

{align:justify|Before I came to know the Buddha's teachings, I was very cruel by nature. Toward animals I felt no compassion, no pity, no sympathy whatsoever.}
{align:justify|More than twenty years ago, my whole family worked at a shoe factory, where a technical instructor, Mr. X, was very fond of eating dog meat. Once, Mr. X went out, bought a dog, and asked me to kill it for him. Feeling not the slightest hesitation, I smashed its head hard with an iron mallet. The dog howled pitifully, yet I remained utterly unmoved and kept striking blow after blow until its skull split open, blood gushed out, and it stopped twitching—only then did I stop.}
{align:justify|Moreover, whenever I happened upon a snake, I would devise every possible scheme to catch it, then crush its head with a rock. Before dying, the snake would writhe and roll in agony, while I watched calmly as though seeing nothing, my heart feeling not a shred of pity nor the slightest remorse for my inhumane cruelty.}
{align:justify|Later, I was afflicted with one painful illness after another. Seven years ago, while working on a road crew, a construction vehicle suddenly backed up; caught off guard, I was struck and suffered a brain injury. I had to be hospitalized and took a very long time to recover.}
{align:justify|After that, stomach pains flared up. I visited countless hospitals large and small and took every kind of Eastern and Western medicine, all to no avail.}
{align:justify|Finally, having no choice, I resorted to folk remedies people recommended. These recipes called for the flesh of many kinds of beings—dogs, cats, snakes, turtles, centipedes, chickens, ducks, and so on—steamed, boiled, stewed, or roasted together to take effect. As a result, I caused the deaths of even more living creatures. Yet after long years of taking all sorts of medicines, my illness not only failed to improve but grew steadily worse.}
{align:justify|Then I went to major hospitals for examination, where the doctors said that without immediate surgery my life could hardly be saved—so I had to undergo an operation. More than two-thirds of my stomach was removed, along with a section of my intestines, showing just how critical my condition was.}
{align:justify|Last year, while working at a factory, I carelessly let a stamping machine crush my finger to a bloody pulp. Blood flowed without ceasing and the pain pierced my heart; I wailed miserably and nearly fainted. Looking at my mangled, bleeding hand, my finger trembling uncontrollably, I suddenly recalled how I had once beaten snakes and killed dogs, with blood staining the whole yard red. And so I began to ask myself: "For more than ten years I have been tormented ceaselessly by strange illnesses and have met with every kind of crippling accident—can all of this be retribution for my past killing?"…}

{align:justify|A female colleague of mine named Tran once told us at a gathering that her father, Old Master Tran, had a peculiar fondness: he loved eating fish eyes. Whenever fish was served at a meal, he would always gouge out the eyes to eat, praising them as delicious, and he even instructed the family to buy plenty of fish—above all, the fish had to be alive, for only then did he find them truly tasty.}
{align:justify|Later, her father suddenly developed an inflammation in one eye. The condition broke out and worsened gravely, leaving many renowned doctors helpless. Soon that eye went completely blind, and the doctors removed it, replacing it with a glass eye.}
{align:justify|The following year, his remaining eye fell ill as well; before long he lost his sight entirely, and now he must live out his days unable to see the light.}

{align:justify|At my workplace there was a colleague very fond of shooting birds with a slingshot. His aim was remarkably precise—hardly ever missing. Every shot struck a vital spot, wounding the bird in the head. Once he took aim, not a single one escaped.}
{align:justify|For years everyone admired this talent of his, never sparing words of amazement and praise.}
{align:justify|Last year, while our unit was repairing a construction project, the crane hook on a truck suddenly snapped and fell from high above, smashing straight onto the head of a worker below. The heavy iron hook crushed the victim's skull like a split watermelon, brains splattering and blood pooling on the ground. All who witnessed it were horrified and dared not look. The victim turned out to be none other than that marksman colleague.}

{align:justify|I have an elder relative who loved meat, most of all pig's trotters, chicken wings and chicken feet. Not only did he eat them at all three meals every day, but whenever he drank with a few friends the table always had to include those dishes. One could say he loved meat to extremes and never tired of it.}
{align:justify|In old age, excess uric acid left this elder's hands and feet twisted and deformed, and frightful sores broke out across his face. Several patches of skin even began to rot. Though he sought cures everywhere, nothing healed him. Once, he carelessly fell and fractured his thigh; he groaned in pain for days before passing away. According to his family, in the days before his death he would often tremble and cry out in terror:}
{align:justify|"Someone has come to seize me! Someone wants to seize me! They want me to join them!"—chilling all who heard it.}

{align:justify|In my home village the old folks often told us young people a story as a warning:}
{align:justify|Thirty years ago in Taiwan, some poor people earned a living as itinerant peddlers. Once a cloth seller brought his goods into a village, and the women gathered around to look. They passed the cloth from hand to hand, examining and commenting in turn, yet no one bought anything. Seeing this, the seller packed up his cloth—but on checking his goods he found a bolt of fabric missing. Everyone denied taking it. Among the women, one fiercely insisted on her innocence and swore a terrible oath:}
{align:justify|"If I took the cloth as you say, may a car run me over and kill me!"}
{align:justify|Later she moved to Kinmen. One day, as she sat inside her house, a truck with failed brakes smashed straight into the house and crushed her to death.}
{align:justify|When the news spread, everyone buzzed with discussion, calling it a terrifying instance of karmic retribution.}
{align:justify|TALES TOLD BY A FORENSIC POLICE OFFICER}
{align:justify|I am a medical-legal police officer—what people usually call a forensic pathologist. I provide medical expert findings to two courts and four branches of the judicial police. Simply put, wherever a homicide occurs, the body is brought to us so we can examine the corpse, determine the cause of death, write up a complete report, and hand it to the court or the police bureau.}
{align:justify|All humans are born and must die, yet the causes of death come in unimaginable varieties…}
{align:justify|When we draw up our reports, we work with great seriousness and thoroughness. We must analyze the cause of death: the direct cause leading to death, the contributing causes, the overall conclusion on mortality…}
{align:justify|I dwell on these technical terms because I want to say that our society always has a proper, lawful explanation for death.}
{align:justify|For example, an old man over seventy was struck by an electric scooter, fell, hit his head on a cement pole by the road, and died. Examination showed head trauma, a fractured skull leading to death. Here the head injury is the underlying cause, the skull damage the direct cause, and immune-system failure a contributing cause.}
{align:justify|But does such an explanation truly supply a sufficient answer to the great question of "why people die"? In twenty-six years at the forensic institute, I have always felt bewildered by this phenomenon. For with the very same injuries, some die and some live?! That is, some with very slight wounds die, while others, gravely injured and given up for dead by all, miraculously survive…}
{align:justify|Later, when I encountered the Buddha's teachings and gained some understanding of the path, I could make sense of all these questions.}
{align:justify|Death is a part of this world, the endpoint of a life. Seen from this angle, we can understand the true face of death.}
{align:justify|In recent years I have taken care to study every case I handled thoroughly and record it in detail. The deeper I looked, the clearer it became: death never occurs at random—it offers powerful, irrefutable proof of the law of cause and effect.}
{align:justify|I often meet the relatives of the dead who look at me and recount their grief through tears:}
{align:justify|"Why did one so kind pass away so young? Why does that evildoer enjoy a long life? Why does a person careful all his life keep meeting sudden calamities? Why does a careless jaywalker walk away unscathed after being hit by a car?"}
{align:justify|I believe all such resentment stems from not understanding cause and effect.}
{align:justify|First, let me tell of the case that racked my brains and left me distressed… It was this very case that pushed me toward the Buddhist path. Though the case was not complicated, the investigation dragged on so long that I became deeply absorbed in it:}

{align:justify|In 2002, a 28-year-old man rose in the middle of the night and killed his parents, both over sixty. While the old couple slept soundly, their son tied them fast to the bed, then cut their throats, blood spattering the walls and pooling on the floor… The murderer fled that very night, and was later caught in a small neighborhood down south.}
{align:justify|The killer was married with a son. On the night of the murder, his wife—after a quarrel the day before—had angrily taken the child back to her own parents' home, thereby escaping the nightmare massacre.}
{align:justify|On the surface, this looked like a clear-cut case with utterly conclusive evidence: the procuratorate had taken confessions, and the full body of proof—physical exhibits and witnesses—was complete. It called for strict punishment and a swift verdict.}
{align:justify|At the first trial the death sentence was pronounced at once, but the defense lawyer argued that "the offender is severely mentally ill" and requested a psychiatric evaluation before further proceedings.}
{align:justify|The investigation dragged on with inconclusive results, so the case kept stalling… in the end the execution was suspended for two years. The killer's lawyer appealed again but was overruled, and the case closed at the final trial.}
{align:justify|It sounds quick in the telling, but in fact the case stretched over four years in all; I had to toil through painstaking investigations, gathering evidence seven or eight times, until the files piled up thick.}
{align:justify|The killer was the only son in the family, spoiled from childhood, with an extremely violent temper. After marrying he still lived with his parents and quarreled with them constantly. In the days before the murders, his parents and his wife had clashed; then the killer demanded money from his parents to buy a house, but they said they had no money for now and scolded him as a worthless idler who never worked and shamed them before the neighbors!… This provoked his murderous intent.}
{align:justify|But is that enough to explain the killer's motive? In this world, how many pampered children turn rebellious and clash with their families? Why was this killer so bent on murdering his own parents?}
{align:justify|In the files I found a chilling passage of testimony, in which the killer confessed frankly:}
{align:justify|"I had wanted to kill them for a long time—they're so useless! I asked them for money to buy a house and they refused—so why not just kill them! What use are they?… I planned it months ago (since they're my parents, I didn't want them to die too painfully). At first I thought of giving them… pesticide to drink, but halfway to the shop I turned back, thinking pesticide would burn their intestines. Then I planned to take them to the lake and drown them. But I never did it. In the end, after much thought, I concluded that… slitting their throats was best—not too painful, and death would come quickly"…}
{align:justify|Reading this confession chilled me to the bone. To speak of killing his parents as casually as killing… chickens or ducks! Investigating further, I was even more horrified to uncover this detail:}
{align:justify|The murdered couple ran a chicken-slaughtering stall, doing brisk business at the market. When I went to investigate at the market, the stall of the murdered chicken sellers was considered unlucky and bad for business, so everyone shunned it and no one would rent it. According to neighboring vendors, the couple used to tie up live chickens in batches, hang them on steel wires, then grip each chicken's head tight and slice its throat with a knife. Even the chickens' blood was never wasted—it fetched a good price. All their lives they grew rich from this trade. They say the throat-cutting technique was handed down from their ancestors.}
{align:justify|I remember this case so well because it was the one that first gave me faith in religion. As every analysis found its reasonable explanation, my belief in the law of cause and effect deepened, and the lesson was seared into my memory once I grasped the reason… That night I scarcely slept at all. The dead couple had spent their lives slicing chickens' throats, famed for their skillful killing… and because they cut creatures' throats that way, they drew upon themselves the calamity of having their throats cut by their own only son… Does such retribution not make us tremble with horror?}

{align:justify|Translating this far, I suddenly recalled a story told by a Dharma friend from Hanoi: near her home there was a couple who earned a comfortable living slaughtering pigs and selling pork. They had an only son of about fifteen, very gentle by nature.}
{align:justify|One day the boy came home from school and suddenly killed both his parents, hacking their flesh and flinging it all over the floor. Then he went upstairs to sleep.}
{align:justify|When the police came to arrest him, the boy was still drowsy with sleep. Everyone was horrified by the massacre and could offer no explanation for it.}
{align:justify|I leave you to ponder this. Now I invite you to continue with another story from the forensic officer:}

{align:justify|We usually think of death as bad and frightening, but there are cases where "being unable to die" is far more miserable and agonizing—a punishment heavier than death itself!}
{align:justify|This thorny problem arose at a mineral extraction company owned by a Mr. Zhao, founder of a "Coal Mining Company" in a small town.}
{align:justify|The company carried no workplace-safety insurance at all; everything was sustained through private dealings with powerful figures. Naturally, Mr. Zhao enjoyed very strong protection, so after a mining accident occurred, the locality could hardly investigate smoothly, and the case in that remote region was handed to us.}
{align:justify|A gas explosion collapsed the coal mine, killing one man and injuring two. Ordinarily such accidents were all settled smoothly according to an "unwritten rule": the owner need only deftly pay compensation to the workers' families to make it go away.}
{align:justify|But as ill luck would have it, just then a nationwide inspection order came down and had to be strictly enforced—so the company owner was arrested.}
{align:justify|When another investigator and I went to gather evidence from Mr. Zhao, the guard told us:}
{align:justify|"Mr. Zhao has diabetes and has been taken to the hospital."}
{align:justify|I rushed to the "Ministry of Justice Hospital" to meet Mr. Zhao. He looked past fifty, tall and striking, with a hearty voice and an extremely tough, forceful character—clearly not one to cooperate easily.}
{align:justify|He sat on the bed, indifferent to the questions I raised, cold and unconcerned, until, just as I was about to leave, he declared bluntly:}
{align:justify|"Within a month someone will bail me out of here!"}
{align:justify|He was not wrong: with the investigation blocked by many interfering causes, the work proceeded poorly, and in the end the case was settled by releasing him on bail, with only residential surveillance. But on the very night he rejoiced, eager to instantly regain his freedom, his diabetes and gallstones flared up violently. Though neither the Ministry of Justice nor the hospital had the least wish to keep him, his illness detained him and forced him to stay.}
{align:justify|From then on, Mr. Zhao never left the hospital again. More than four months passed before the case was finally decided: Mr. Zhao was sentenced to six years in prison. But he could never enter a jail, for his health steadily declined—so his sentence could only be served at the "Public Security Hospital."}
{align:justify|My work constantly took me to the "Public Security Hospital," so I often met Mr. Zhao. Thanks to his money, he still occupied a clean single room, but the window was barred with iron; every time we met, his eyes opened wide as if brimming with unspoken thoughts. Once he asked me:}
{align:justify|"Do you believe in cause and effect?"}
{align:justify|I answered:}
{align:justify|"Of course! I am a Buddhist, after all."}
{align:justify|He suddenly sighed and said nothing more.}
{align:justify|After a while his illness worsened and he had to be transferred; the prison guards handled the paperwork to move him out. Strangely enough, not only did neither the guards nor the hospital wish to keep him, but even the King of Hell refused to take him!}
{align:justify|When I met him again he had lain in a hospital bed for nearly eight months. One meter eighty tall, his weight had plummeted. We often met and talked, and he grew to trust me more and more… at times even entrusting me with certain errands.}
{align:justify|Though I came from the medical profession, I had never seen anyone so emaciated. His eyes had sunk deep into their sockets, his cheekbones jutted out, his lips were pale, his ribs protruded—each one plainly visible.}
{align:justify|As he breathed, the bones rose and fell, giving the feeling that the slightest touch would snap them. His arms and legs were even more dreadful to behold—skinny sticks with not a scrap of flesh. Gallstones required surgery, but because of his diabetes the wound lingered unhealed, then grew infected, ulcerating and spreading to the surrounding flesh. He looked like a skull mounted on a skeleton—enough to haunt one's dreams all night!}
{align:justify|I studied the course of his illness and found that, by all ordinary reckoning, a patient like him—his organs failing, his heart barely able to keep beating—could hardly stay alive, yet he lived on and on in torment, suffering extreme pain without dying. Although he often told me his sole wish now was… to die! If he could only die, that would be happiness itself. But even this "reward" heaven stingily withheld from him! He lived on, with every second steeped in utter agony.}
{align:justify|Once, craving death, he stopped eating and drinking, fasting for more than ten days until body and mind wasted to the utmost; the doctors were certain he must die. Yet he lived on—he simply could not die!}
{align:justify|A long while passed; buried in work, I never visited him. Then one day he had an aide call and invite me over.}
{align:justify|That evening, mustering his last strength, he told me his secret in a feeble voice…}
{align:justify|"More than ten years ago, when he had just begun mining, being short of funds he sent men to the railway station to trick simple-minded drifters ignorant of the law, hiring them to work down his pits.}
{align:justify|In this way he paid no wages at all, needing only a few guards to watch over these laborers. Once they had amassed his first pot of gold for him, he sealed up that mine shaft to bury the truth, abandoning the trapped workers to exhaustion, hunger, and thirst in the darkness—until at last they all suffocated!"…}
{align:justify|(What he told was later confirmed: when the police excavated the site, they brought up more than twenty skeletons.)}
{align:justify|After that, Mr. Zhao lay in the hospital for nearly half a year. Throughout those six months, almost every minute he endured extreme peril. Yet no matter how his body was racked and tormented with pain, he did not die. His entire fortune drained away into hospital fees, and in the end even his family stopped visiting him. Though his suffering peaked day and night, he wailed and moaned without pause, his cries tearing the heart. With his immune system gravely deficient, no wound anywhere on his body would heal; all grew infected, ulcerating and rotting.}
{align:justify|In the end, not a single sound patch could be found on him—his whole body was one festering, putrid mass.}
{align:justify|After that I never saw him again. From the nurses who cared for him I heard that after he died, when they wrapped his corpse in a sheet to move it, his bones crumbled and his flesh—rotten and suppurating—dissolved into a slimy heap; before his body reached the morgue it had nearly turned into a pile of rotting bones and flesh.}
{align:justify|What follows is an extraordinarily strange case that occurred last winter.}

{align:justify|At midnight, I received an urgent order from above:}
{align:justify|"Report to the scene and get to work at once!"}
{align:justify|It seems that outside the city there is a great bridge, and residents reported: "Someone has hanged themselves on this bridge!" When we reached the scene, we saw the suicide was a woman. From the examination, her resolve to die was clearly immense (the rope had tightened so hard her neck bones broke).}
{align:justify|Investigating her background, we learned the deceased lived nearby (but the story did not end there, for a still more dreadful sight awaited us)…}
{align:justify|Arriving at her home, we started at the sight of the naked corpse of a baby not yet one year old lying on the bed. When the police burst in, the child's father still did not know his wife had killed herself; he wept and wailed, reproaching his wife loudly to the police and telling the whole tale.}
{align:justify|As it turned out, the matter was nothing complicated: the husband had gone out drinking with friends, leaving his wife home alone with the child. After he returned, his wife wept and raged, insistently accusing him of seeing another woman outside, of carrying on with Ms. X… Hearing this, the husband flared up, stormed out of the house, and went back to his drinking friends. At home, the wife brooded with mounting fury and sent her husband an "ultimatum" running roughly: "Come home and mind the child—I cannot care for it any longer!"… But the husband, dead drunk, had no mind to spare for the messages on his phone…}
{align:justify|Not until he returned did he discover that the little boy, kicking about, had thrown off the blanket and frozen to death. (Country houses in winter have no heating and are bitterly cold).}
{align:justify|The poor husband, still unaware his wife had killed herself, kept venting his furious blame at the police, saying it was his wife's fault the boy had frozen to death… Until the police broke the terrible news—"Your wife has already hanged herself!"—at which the husband could not utter a word and collapsed in a faint.}
{align:justify|Over a petty quarrel, two lives were lost, and a family's happiness was shattered… We could only sigh. After a thorough examination, the case was confirmed as suicide, and the matter was quickly closed.}
{align:justify|But during the investigation we uncovered one more detail: old Luu (the bicycle-repair shop owner in the village) was the true cause of this tragedy. Asking around, we learned that Mr. Luu, past fifty, divorced for years, now lived alone.}
{align:justify|By nature he loved to fabricate gossip and spread tales—with no cause at all he could spin a great drama out of nothing. It was he who had whispered poison and told the wife that her husband was having an affair! What is more, he embroidered it with vivid, meticulous detail, as if it were solid truth. When we asked about the husband's affair that he had trumpeted, we learned it was pure fabrication.}
{align:justify|Many villagers complained and reported: "Mr. Luu loves to spin gossip and stir up trouble—many families, believing him, have fallen into discord, husbands and wives turning against each other, fathers and sons embittered, even coming to blows and quarrels"…}
{align:justify|Whenever Mr. Luu learned that his words had set people at one another's throats… he beamed with delight, sitting in the tavern looking utterly smug.}
{align:justify|Yet in the end he could not be prosecuted, for his conduct fit no criminal charge (though it sowed calamity)… The wife hanged herself for believing his wild tales… but he could not on that account be charged with murder! So we could only warn and caution him.}
{align:justify|To our surprise, within a month the local police informed me: Mr. Luu had met with trouble!}
{align:justify|"One night, Mr. Luu went to friends' house and got dead drunk. After being helped back to his repair shop, at midnight he crawled out looking for liquor, groped about, grabbed a jar, and in his stupor took a swig. Little did he know it was no wine at all, but a jar of chemicals—liquid used for painting metal engines, extremely low in moisture: once brushed on, within two seconds it can freeze solid whatever it touches.}
{align:justify|Mr. Luu collapsed unconscious on the spot; luckily someone nearby rushed him to the emergency room in time.}
{align:justify|Examining him, the doctors found his tongue frozen stiff as ice—swallow even a little of this chemical and you are done for! The doctors declared: from now on, not only would Mr. Luu never speak again, but even his palate could hardly be saved! Henceforth he could only be fed through a tube"…}
{align:justify|These are a few stories of retribution I wish to share with you.}
{align:justify|According to medicine, every death is caused by illness or injury. Even dying in one's bedroom or passing away in deep sleep always comes from disease or other causes.}
{align:justify|I am a Buddhist disciple, and also a medical doctor. Toward the scientific view I am always respectful, yet I must say: the vision of science is always limited! Each of us will certainly die, but the time of death and whether the manner of dying is painful are truly connected with what we do, and always bear the imprint of cause and effect!}
{align:justify|Had I not known the Buddha's teachings, conducting investigations I too would think simply and conclude: death comes from injury, illness… or execution for crimes, and so on…}
{align:justify|But over years of examining many homicide cases, after probing deeply into them, I have always discovered the truth that "good is repaid with good, evil with evil"—without the slightest error.}
{align:justify|The Buddha's teachings say "the karma of sentient beings is unfathomable"—how true! This single line can explain the questions of birth and death in our daily lives.}
{align:justify|Thu Dĩnh, July 9, 2013}
{align:justify|Translator's note: We see clearly: words can save people, and words can kill people!}
{align:justify|Mr. Luu used his tongue to embroider and invent stories, taking it as amusement, never knowing he was planting seeds of great evil, bringing calamity to so many in the village. Had his tongue kept working, there would have been still more victims. And so his whole tongue, with the palate—every organ that could bring disaster upon the world—was… eradicated.}
{align:justify|Every day in the papers and on television we see reports of idle youths who, late at night, gather on overpasses to hurl stones that shatter car windows, or smash the signal lamps at railway stations… taking it as fun, never knowing these are acts that wreck their own blessings!}
{align:justify|Given hands yet using them for no one's benefit, but to sow harm—hurling stones that shatter glass can also gravely injure people!… Worse, a broken railway signal cannot signal, easily causing catastrophic traffic accidents for passersby. Thus not only do they erode their own blessings, rank, and power, but evildoers, besides being born into unhappy destinies, may draw upon themselves the retribution of handlessness.}
{align:justify|Whatever limb or organ performs deeds useful to people draws fine retribution; conversely, if it only sows danger… it will be removed. Depending on one's blessings, the evil retribution ripens sooner or later—but never fails to come.}
{align:justify|Only from ignorance, from never being taught cause and effect, from not understanding… do people commit offenses. So what is needed is to help people understand cause and effect clearly. Once this principle is clear, with no overseer needed, people will on their own do what benefits themselves and others, willingly shunning evil and doing good.}
{align:justify|THE KARMIC RETRIBUTION OF SLANDER AND FABRICATION}
{align:justify|A friend of mine had always created bad speech karma, loving embroidered tales and spreading groundless rumors—all the more harmful since he worked in communications. One day he suddenly ran to apologize to me: two years earlier he had posted articles online stirring up a baseless sex scandal to ruin me and my clients, causing us endless harassment and trouble, so that friends dared not work with us.}
{align:justify|I was eager to know what had moved him to reform and change so greatly.}
{align:justify|He told me frankly: six weeks earlier he had returned south for the funeral of his eldest uncle's wife, Aunt Thom, and learning her full story left him deeply shaken!}
{align:justify|Aunt Thom had two sons: the elder, brilliant and accomplished, died in a road accident months earlier while driving home. The younger, fresh out of university, over some heartbreak or other set himself on fire—a ghastly death.}
{align:justify|As for Aunt Thom herself, cancer of the throat and tongue required surgery on her neck and tongue; unable to speak, she lay tossing on her bed in agony, enduring every torment. When her illness reached its final stage, each time she opened her mouth the stench made everyone want to vomit—no one dared come near. Both sons were dead, and now the mother… At the funeral, only her husband (the eldest uncle) remained to preside—truly pitiful.}
{align:justify|Then the second uncle—the eldest's younger brother, who had left home more than a decade before—returned with his children and handed his elder brother a farewell letter (in his wife's own hand), containing two lines written in blood:}
{align:justify|"Rouge lips and white teeth, spinning reckless lies that ruined another's honor—"}
{align:justify|your line shall be cut off without descendants, and after death you shall fall into the tongue-cutting hell"…}
{align:justify|The eldest uncle finished the letter, collapsed to his knees, burst into loud sobs, then kowtowed to his younger brother begging forgiveness; the two brothers embraced, weeping together…}
{align:justify|My friend asked his mother, and only then learned the whole cause:}
{align:justify|Long ago, the eldest uncle's wife, vying for her in-laws' favor (secretly fearing the old folks would bequeath more property to the accomplished second-uncle household), had fabricated a vicious rumor: "The second uncle's wife is carrying on with a coworker"… leading her parents-in-law to misunderstand the second uncle's wife, while husband and wife quarreled too…}
{align:justify|Wrongfully accused, the second uncle's wife burned with rage; she cut her finger, wrote the farewell letter in blood, then drank pesticide and killed herself. The second uncle was heartbroken, but at the time the eldest sister-in-law's power was too great for the truth to be sorted out, so he could not yet make the letter public—he could only swallow his tears, take his two small sons east to build a new life, and never return for years.}
{align:justify|My friend's mother had at the time joined in spreading that vicious rumor, echoing and trumpeting the slander throughout the village. Seeing the retribution that crashed upon Aunt Thom's household, she was terrified; and witnessing her own sons suffering for years with kidney inflammation, tormented by illness, unable to work normally, my friend's mother was utterly shaken—she led the whole family before the second uncle's wife's memorial tablet, kowtowing like worshippers before the stars, begging forgiveness for those bygone sins…}
{align:justify|Learning all this, my friend's own mind was shaken as never before. He reflected that for years he too had created much unwholesome speech karma, often writing online articles that spread scandal, sowed discord, and harmed people… his heart filled with remorse and dread… so he resolved to come and beg forgiveness of his victims (myself among them), vowing to reform from here on"…}
{align:justify|My mother, hearing the story, warned me: "In choosing a lifelong partner, be extremely careful—for marry an unkind wife, foul-mouthed and ill-hearted, wanton in speech karma and crooked in talk, and calamity will reach your descendants"… Mother also reminded me always to remember Master Cheng Yen's teaching: "Speak like fragrant flowers, never like venomous snakes!"}
{align:justify|All who recklessly create speech karma… look upon the eldest uncle's wife and take warning. Today she stands as a horrifying example to the whole village. I recount this story hoping readers will guard their speech karma carefully and avoid such errors.}
{align:justify|PLEASE DO NOT DO DEEDS THAT ERODE VIRTUE}
{align:justify|Dear good teachers and fellow cultivators,}
{align:justify|I have not learned Buddhism long, my understanding of the teachings is not profound, and I dare not discourse at length—I only ask, from my own experience, to share a warning with everyone: a thousand times, ten thousand times over, do nothing that erodes virtue. Do not kill living beings, steal, engage in sexual misconduct…}
{align:justify|Here, I also thank the Buddhist website for giving me the chance to recount the offenses I once committed, so all may take warning. If reading this article you wish to revile, scold, or despise me, all are welcome—for that is my good fortune.}
{align:justify|I was born into an ordinary family, and from the time I could understand things I did something utterly virtueless: hogging the outhouse.}
{align:justify|I was very small then. At the time my great-grandfather (my maternal grandmother's father) lived with us.}
{align:justify|Great-grandfather was old; when he needed the toilet, arriving even a little late meant he could not hold it and would soil himself… all over his person. And if that happened, my grandparents would berate great-grandfather fiercely—very harsh to hear.}
{align:justify|Back then I understood nothing, but I loved mischief: the moment I saw great-grandfather rise to head for the outhouse, I would dash in ahead of him, slam the door shut, and though great-grandfather moaned and cried out outside, I would not open it. Only after great-grandfather had soiled his trousers would I come out. Because of me, grandmother scolded great-grandfather terribly, leaving him deeply miserable—and the chief cause was me; the trouble arose through me.}
{align:justify|Yet I kept playing this game, causing great-grandfather much suffering. Not until later, when great-grandfather lay unconscious, would he often moan in his daze, calling my name, pleading: "Open the door quickly." At the time I did not know I was acting virtuelessly.}
{align:justify|Then great-grandfather passed away; once he even appeared in my mother's dream, instructing her that I was small and understood nothing, begging her not to beat me! Though still very small, whenever Mother told me this I felt remorseful and sad. I would often pull the blanket over my head and weep bitterly. Even now, remembering it, my heart aches.}
{align:justify|Perhaps from those evil causes, those wicked childhood acts, I now receive their retribution. Though still young, I feel my plight differs little from great-grandfather's. I constantly need to urinate and cannot hold it—a great torment. Going to the hospital finds no cause; everything is normal.}
{align:justify|Before, I did not understand; only after learning Buddhism did I grasp that this is precisely the retribution for the evil karma I once created. Now I can only bear it, tasting its flavor to the full—known to me alone. So I beg you, fellow cultivators: ten thousand times over, do no evil—sooner or later retribution will ripen right upon your own person!}
{align:justify|As a child I was very naughty, fond of catching dragonflies, crickets, insects of all kinds… Cruel by nature then, I loved tearing off legs, snapping wings, tormenting them to death for fun. Our house stocked many medicines; I would mix boiling water with drugs into a potion, then use a syringe to inject it into crickets' bellies and other insects… I tormented them cruelly! Remembering now, I truly created oceans of killing karma. Sins piled high! Then I went on to wreck anthills, catch crickets… I poured water to flood their holes, then dammed them with mud to trap them… grave sins indeed.}
{align:justify|Later, growing up, our family kept dogs continuously (for each one died, so another had to follow)—whenever a dog disobeyed, I beat it savagely.}
{align:justify|Writing this now, recalling those scenes, I see myself as… NOT HUMAN! I sincerely repent my crimes! Sowing evil seeds then, I must surely reap their fruit.}
{align:justify|And so, a few years ago, I developed cracked skin: whenever the weather turned cold my hands swelled badly, nights I could not sleep, hands and feet aching as if gnawed by countless insects—a torment one could die of!}
{align:justify|Then, not knowing I was repaying karma, I ran about seeking cures of every kind—pills, injections, poultices… all useless; instead the illness grew worse.}
{align:justify|Only after learning Buddhism did I understand: all of it was karmic retribution, for long ago I had ravaged small creatures, so I had to bear such retribution. Rightly so. And thanks to learning Buddhism and bowing to the Buddhas, for nearly two years my illness has no longer been grave; my hands still crack, but greatly improved—for this I thank the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas! Now whenever illness strikes, especially karmic illness, I meet it calmly—for having created evil, I must accept its consequences.}
{align:justify|One last matter I wish to warn all about: ten thousand times over, do not commit sexual misconduct. At the time I understood nothing, and knowing nothing of the Buddha's teachings, in my vigorous youth, fond moreover of pornographic books and films, I was often aroused and gratified myself. Once lust arose with no taming, it gradually became a bad habit—so bad that merely glancing at nude images aroused me and I had to gratify myself, until even bathing aroused me, growing into habit.}
{align:justify|Before learning Buddhism, knowing nothing of the path, whenever lust arose I gratified myself, taking it as natural. But doing it constantly changed even my appearance (truly so—I do not lie: with a base character, one's looks turn base as well—believe me: whoever is caught in this vile habit should abandon it utterly; it will greatly benefit you!).}
{align:justify|Originally my looks were quite majestic, my features refined, but constant self-indulgence changed my appearance into utter coarseness. My health worsened too, from drained essence and exhausted strength… added to the childhood offense against great-grandfather, the retribution I bore grew heavier. Urination difficult, suffering on every side…}
{align:justify|After learning Buddhism, I understood and recognized these as unwholesome acts, and single-mindedly vowed to abandon them. But once lustful thoughts arise, they surge powerfully, hard to bear—yet self-indulgence afterward feels sinful, even worse.}
{align:justify|Only a few months ago did I fully conquer and master myself. Through persisting every week in bowing to the Buddhas at the temple, sincerely prostrating in repentance. Then I heard teachings on the great compassion of Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) and the benefits of reciting Her sacred name—so whenever lust arose, I silently recited "Nam mô đại từ đại bi Quan Thế Âm Bồ tát!" Having recited, I vowed on: "May You protect me to cut off my lustful mind"—reciting and vowing thus without cease, with truly wondrous results: lustful thoughts dissolved of themselves, truly vanished entirely!}
{align:justify|New to practice, at first lustful thoughts arose two or three times a week, then for two or three weeks none arose. Now not even their shadow remains, for in my heart there is only the Buddha! For this I must thank Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin)! Without Her, I might still be sunk with no way to save myself!}
{align:justify|Not knowing the path, most people take indulgence as the greatest pleasure—but taste once the peaceful bliss of desirelessness, and you will never wish to return to that muddy realm.}
{align:justify|On doctrine I dare not spin profound mysteries—I only see that, since I repented and resolved to reform, whatever illness comes has grown lighter; even repaying karma is not too painful and can be borne. Urination troubles me no longer.}
{align:justify|Fellow cultivators, this is my own lived experience; the faults I committed I always hid, daring to tell no one. Today I muster all my courage to lay them bare, hoping that any who committed faults like mine will swiftly reform and find peace together. Had I never encountered the Buddha's teachings, the retribution I must repay would likely be heavy, truly dreadful…}
{align:justify|But thanks to hearing the Buddha's teachings, knowing repentance, remorse, correction, and renewal, vowing to remake myself, today I live very happily. Clearly the Buddha's teachings bring happiness to humankind! I offer my experience, sharing it sincerely, hoping all will take warning—never step onto the wrong path I trod, for it is a road of death!}
{align:justify|Lastly, I thank you all for patiently reading this clumsy essay to its end. If you find me too base, too cruel, contemptible… then scold me loudly and often—for I deserve it; I blame you not, but must thank you…}
{align:justify|If you can take my story, tell it to friends as a warning… helping those who once acted as I did to wake soon, escape the wrong path, nothing could be more precious!}
{align:justify|I wish you all: conduct pure and clean, precepts strict and grave, body, speech, and mind ever pure, hearts ever happy…}
{align:justify|Respectfully written by one in repentance.}
{align:justify|October 8, 2012 – 11:52}
{align:justify|THE LOVE OF THE MANDARIN DUCKS}
{align:justify|I am an atheist, following no religion at all. But seven years ago a strange thing happened that changed my whole life.}
{align:justify|I had been married just six months. Late that night I woke to use the toilet, my head still groggy with sleep, when suddenly a white blur swept past me—so I quickly opened my eyes… and saw clearly a female ghost in white, her face smeared with blood, filthy…}
{align:justify|Even now I don't know how I found the courage to swing my hand… and slap the ghost hard across the face, striking her until she vanished!}
{align:justify|After that night, though I never saw the ghost again, a sense of ill luck grew ever clearer.}
{align:justify|First my husband fell ill and had to see the doctor for days on end. Then my turn came: at midnight my belly suddenly ached violently, and only hospital treatment cured it.}
{align:justify|So we both plainly felt we had been "hit by evil." To dispel it, we went everywhere seeking masters (to perform exorcisms). But nothing worked; the calamities never eased, raging on as before.}
{align:justify|We were utterly miserable then, with no solution to be found.}
{align:justify|By chance I met a friend (a Buddhist disciple) who urged me to learn Buddhism—to use the Buddha's teachings to solve this thorny problem!}
{align:justify|Gravely ill and desperate for a good doctor, with no other way (we just had to rush to embrace the Buddha's feet)—and so we came to Buddhism…}
{align:justify|After learning Buddhism, we began vegetarianism, releasing captive animals, and reciting the "Ksitigarbha Sutra"—after several dozen copies, one night I dreamed the female ghost appeared, weeping as she told me her life story.}
{align:justify|It turned out that centuries ago she had been my husband's wife; in that life she hanged herself and fell into the ghost realm! She had searched for her husband for hundreds of years, until in this life she finally found him here.}
{align:justify|Learning the truth, I felt sympathy, yet my heart burned with resentment. For now I understood why my husband suffered frequent nocturnal emissions… It was this she-ghost (every night she crept in to sleep with my husband)!}
{align:justify|I refused to yield: knowing the ghost could come (only at midnight) to bewitch my husband, I stood guard—near that hour I would strike the wooden bell and recite the Buddha's name loudly. From chronic worry, and rising every midnight to recite the Buddha's name "on guard" for my husband, my health steadily failed from severe lack of sleep—yet I stubbornly held out, "fighting" thus for nearly two years. At last I reaped the result: heart disease—I collapsed! (Not frightened senseless by the ghost, but fainting from utter exhaustion)…}
{align:justify|This could not go on—I could bear it no longer; tormented in body and mind, my heart disease lingered, and peaceful life seemed impossible. So in early spring, my husband and I decided that this April we would together enter a temple and leave home, ending this bizarre war of grievances between humans and ghost… But before we could act, the she-ghost struck first: this time she entered my husband outright, savagely bent on killing me.}
{align:justify|Truthfully, years of grueling battle with her had left me exhausted and heartbroken… so at death's door, I too felt like giving up. I thought: "Let me take her blade and end all debts—only wishing to be reborn in the Pure Land at once, shaking off every sorrow"…}
{align:justify|Just as I closed my eyes awaiting… the she-ghost's killing stroke through my husband's hands, suddenly came a "Clang!"… the ghost had flung the knife to the ground, grumbling: "Kill you and I fall into hell besides! What a wretched bother!"}
{align:justify|Now it was my turn to rage—I seized my husband's body (with the ghost soul still lodged inside) and beat it black and blue!… Only then did I learn what it means: "to beg life and be refused, to beg death and be refused!"}
{align:justify|My heart and mind tormented beyond bearing, I had but one recourse: lodging my husband in a nearby temple. After that, every night the she-ghost came seeking me to harass, wailing noisily in my ears, lamenting: the temple has Dharma protectors guarding it—I cannot get in at all… The ghost's racket left me no sleep at all; at last I had to plead, begging the temple master in his compassion to hold a deliverance rite for the ghost.}
{align:justify|As this temple practices Buddha-recitation single-mindedly, the rite aimed to deliver the ghost to the Pure Land. When the Buddha-recitation assembly was held, its effect was triumph beyond compare! For under the Buddha's empowering might, I beheld the former lives of our trio, seeing plainly all that happened in past lives, untangling the knot for all three (two humans, one ghost)—the thousand-year wheel of rebirth at last unveiled:}
{align:justify|The pair of mandarin ducks}
{align:justify|Countless ages ago, a beautiful pair of mandarin ducks dwelled by a still, secluded lake. A little boy often came to play and discovered them; he would bring them food over a long time. One day at the lake he found the drake standing alone and sorrowful; the duck was nowhere to be seen? Searching everywhere, he spotted a vicious hawk in the sky carrying the duck away in its talons.}
{align:justify|Helpless, the boy brought food to comfort the drake. But the drake only drooped, refusing to stir. The next day the boy visited again; still it fasted, refusing everything. Days later the drake died. Grieving, the boy buried it, and never came to the lake to play again.}
{align:justify|The mandarin ducks take rebirth}
{align:justify|Time flew; centuries slipped by. The mandarin ducks circling through rebirth at last met again, both born into the human realm: the drake as a man, the duck as a woman, wedded as a devoted couple knit in deep affection.}
{align:justify|One day the husband was away, and his wife quarreled so fiercely with her mother-in-law that in a moment of despair the young bride hanged herself. Dead, she fell into an evil destiny, bearing a ghost's body; but unable to let go of her love for her husband, the ghost single-mindedly sought her lord through boundless misery…}
{align:justify|Through round after round of the six realms' rebirth… down to the present life, centuries later!}
{align:justify|The ghost's husband in this life was again born a man, while I was that little boy of long ago who once fed the mandarin ducks… (in this life born female); our affinities ripened, and I wed the man (the drake of ages past) as husband and wife, while the harassing ghost is the later incarnation of the duck. For years our three tangled in combat—only now is the connected truth laid bare… It turns out to be a "tragicomedy" woven of converging affinities.}
{align:justify|Under the Buddha's profound, inconceivable, wondrous power, I was humbled into absolute faith; from then on we cultivated diligently and tasted the sweet fruit of practice. Summing up seven years of practice, I draw a few fine lessons, briefly noted here to share with all:}
{align:justify|I. VEGETARIANISM BRINGS MANY BENEFITS:}
{align:justify|Four years a lifelong vegetarian, I have found vegetarianism has these merits:}
{align:justify|– Grave illness heals easily: that year my husband's white blood cells fell until he had no immunity left; thanks to lifelong vegetarianism combined with treatment, little money spent and the illness cured. Now his white cells are back to normal, and the doctor exclaims: "A miracle!"}
{align:justify|– Blessings increase: since going vegetarian I have done no work at all, living only on rental income from leased rooms—yet money remains to save, life leisurely… For vegetarians seldom fall ill, and even catching something, a little medicine cures it. Vegetarian food is very cheap; daily, my husband and I cannot eat more than ten coins' worth.}
{align:justify|– Karmic obstructions dissolve: vegetarians forge no enmity with beings, so karma melts faster than for meat-eaters, and practice meets fewer obstacles.}
{align:justify|– Rejuvenation: I am thirty-eight this year, yet thanks to vegetarianism I look ten years younger than my age. (Young ladies who love youth and beauty—go vegetarian!)}
{align:justify|II. ADVERSE AFFINITIES ARE BLESSINGS TOO}
{align:justify|At first I always thought meeting that ghost was utter bad luck, utter misery! Sickness meant no work, no earnings. But through it I savored the suffering of "parting from what we love" and understood the wheel of rebirth and the law of cause and effect. Because the ghost harassed us unceasingly, our life grew extremely bitter; she pressed and drove us until two atheists like us had to step onto the path of learning Buddhism. What is more, with the ghost appearing daily, I had to recite the Buddha's name nearly ten hours a day, at least fifty thousand invocations daily—I persisted thus for three years. Thanks to it, the great gate of the Pure Land world has opened wide before my eyes (such an ending is truly a gift!)… No words of mine could ever tell it fully… So when adverse affinities appear, they are good chances for practice (like one chased by a fierce tiger—naturally running for dear life, cultivating as if life depended on it)…}
{align:justify|III. BUDDHA-RECITATION IS WONDERFUL}
{align:justify|My path of practice is peculiar indeed. Pressed hard by the ghost, I had to stay alert and practice at all times. To quickly be rid of the she-ghost (to send her far away), I had to exert my utmost.}
{align:justify|New to practice, first I recited sutras and mantras: late nights the Shurangama Mantra, mornings the Amitabha Sutra, at nine the "Ksitigarbha Sutra," afternoons the "Diamond Sutra" with three Great Compassion Mantras, evenings yet another "Ksitigarbha Sutra."}
{align:justify|Sutra recitation took long and wearied me. An atheist by birth, haunted by a ghost into a devout believer (forced to practice hard). Keeping such recitation for nearly a year, one morning as I reached the dedication verse, the ghost covered my sutra and harassed me, leaving me sad (tinged with despair too, seeing the ghost fear no sutra or mantra)…}
{align:justify|(In truth it was not that sutras and mantras lack power, but that newly practicing, my mind still brimmed with scattered greed, anger, and delusion, so no swift result could come.) But then I lost faith and sought a new way.}
{align:justify|My husband and I rented a house on a desolate hill (a dozen graves around) and spent six straight months there practicing "seated contemplation."}
{align:justify|First contemplating, we contemplated the Sutra of Immeasurable Life. At sunrise we sat contemplating; at dusk we sat two hours watching the sky darken. Free daylight hours we recited the Buddha's name. Months of contemplation yielded nothing—and worse, we went astray! For closing my eyes showed only blackness: our practice simply did not reach. Worse still, my temper flared fiercer and fiercer, and I saw ghosts ever more clearly. We endured about half a year, then gave up, packed up, "lowered the flag," and returned.}
{align:justify|Sutra and mantra recitation, seated meditation, contemplation… nearly two years seemed hopeless, so I tried turning to single-minded recitation of Amitabha Buddha's sacred name—and suddenly discovered: daily, I need only rise at midnight and recite the Buddha's name with utterly sincere heart, and the ghost absolutely dares not near the bed—vanishing without a trace. Sometimes in summer, around eight in the evening I would stroll, walking and reciting, perfectly peaceful. Meeting ghosts, I would see them withdraw to both roadsides, kneeling with palms joined in utmost reverence. (Truly, the Buddha's name manifesting before one makes ghosts retreat in reverence.) In truth, the earlier hard "grinding" practice laid the foundation, training our minds to grow familiar with the Buddha's teachings, more easily gathered in stillness.}
{align:justify|In March 2010, I met and heard Dharma Master Tịnh Không expound "Master Shan Dao's Pure Land Thought" and understood the sacred name's wonder ever better—realizing my former fruitless methods simply did not fit.}
{align:justify|For our capacities are base and mean, our bad habits many. Trusting deeply in Amitabha Buddha's compassionate vow-power to gather us in, thinking quietly "if I strive in single-minded recitation I shall surely be reborn in the Pure Land"—from utter misery thirsting for liberation in this very life—we cultivated bravely, reciting the Buddha's name with earnest resolve, single-mindedly relying on His shelter. So we bent our hearts to His name at every moment: walking, standing, sitting, lying… however long, reciting single-mindedly without pause, striving to form assured karma.}
{align:justify|Through long Buddha-recitation, great change appeared (clearest in my husband). Long dwelling with a ghost, his spirit was dimmed, his face darkened. Once he learned to gather his mind in practice, persisting in single-minded recitation, his nights' sleep grew peaceful, his body's condition began returning to normal, his face no longer ashen but bright and clear.}
{align:justify|In early practice, reciting the Buddha's name made him drowsy—but now he can recite diligently all day; though the ghost never wishes to release him for Pure Land rebirth, she can no longer approach to harass, nor hinder his recitation in any way. She stopped seeking me to trouble, and I found the knots of attachment in my own heart fading too—willingly letting go of every worry and clinging, entrusting all to the Buddha's arrangement. My mind at peace, I became happy!}
{align:justify|Coming to Buddhism, I moved from a mixed-practice mindset toward single-minded Buddha-recitation, forming assured karma. Through clearly understanding and deeply trusting that wholehearted single-minded recitation ensures rebirth, I settled my mind securely in the recitation—and went on to introduce this Dharma gate to others.}
{align:justify|Also through single-minded recitation, the Buddha's power blessed me, opening my mind to see clearly the thousand-year cause and effect of three lifetimes, remembering all three of our former lives; I have even beheld Amitabha Buddha's holy countenance and gazed upon the West's holy realm—all bestowed by the sacred power of the Buddha's name.}
{align:justify|I share my story with all. May all who read it benefit.}
{align:justify|Thừa Ngọc – April 11, 2011}
{align:justify|Translator's note: Perhaps from strong emotion the writer repeats, narrating confusedly… I have done my best to arrange it for readable order. Where passages remain hard to follow, I beg readers' pardon—my ability is limited. If readers gain benefit, that is the translator's great reward.}
{align:justify|THE MIDNIGHT CALL}
{align:justify|I am twenty-eight this year. After years of tender love, on May 28, 2011, my boyfriend and I walked hand in hand into the wedding hall to build a marriage. Those days, I was immersed in brimming happiness. One day late in August, I visited my mother.}
{align:justify|By chance I heard Mother say: someone or other, every night at exactly 10:05, calls my name from downstairs! Hearing it frightened me—it seemed too strange, so I kept pressing Mother:}
{align:justify|"Did you mishear, or suffer some auditory hallucination?…"…}
{align:justify|Mother insisted:}
{align:justify|"Absolutely no mishearing. At first Mother thought this area has many named Oanh and assumed people were calling by coincidence, so I paid no mind. But gradually I had to notice, for it kept repeating—three months now by count."}
{align:justify|Now I recall it, admiring Mother's courage: all that time, every evening before ten she would climb to the rooftop terrace, gaze down, scanning all around hoping to catch the prankster?… She waited long and saw nothing—but the moment she came inside, that woman's voice would ring out at exactly 10:05, calling my name.}
{align:justify|Helpless, Mother had to tell me. Then, worried and afraid… I too assumed some cunning ruffians were at work—for under heaven how could so queer a thing occur?}
{align:justify|Full of anxious questions, fearing trouble might befall Mother, wanting to verify truth from falsehood… I decided to sleep at Mother's that night.}
{align:justify|I stared at the clock on the wall, its tick-tock ticking steadily, my heart uneasy and roused to the utmost. To ease the dread, Mother and I chatted while we waited…}
{align:justify|At exactly 10:05 my ears clearly heard a woman's gentle voice calling my name; gooseflesh rose all over me, sweat pouring down. Then, unplanned, Mother and I blurted as one:}
{align:justify|"Did you hear it?"}
{align:justify|Indeed! At last I had to believe the strange thing was real! It turns out in this world there is such a thing as "Ghosts calling at the door!"…}
{align:justify|Puzzled past understanding, my mind terrified, panicked to the extreme! Yet that calling voice felt somehow familiar, intimate…}
{align:justify|That night I lost all sleep—more precisely, "dared not sleep," from fear… All night I sat on the bed, daring not to doze, frightened to the limit. Truly, I had never heard anyone tell of such a thing. Panic besieged me until my mind went limp. Past midnight I dozed a little and dreamed of a thin woman with short hair, hugging a great pile of money to her breast; as I moved closer, she shot me a hateful glare… Thus I endured till dawn. I was truly frightened, not understanding what all this could be.}
{align:justify|At dawn I sought masters to take a look. They could not see the cause, only saying "It seems this one has come demanding your life"… and warning me not to let Mother go out at night, lest danger strike with no safety assured. Besides, the master told me to use five kinds of grain against the ghost. I did exactly as told, but to no effect—the calling continued.}
{align:justify|Mid-Autumn Festival approached; the master said if by Mid-Autumn the name-calling ceased, thereafter it would call no more. But the strange voice kept calling my name every night without pause—what could it be?}
{align:justify|In a blink it was the ninth of the eighth lunar month, only days to Mid-Autumn. My gut burned like fire, fearing trouble for Mother. Father died early; only Mother and I keep each other company, leaning on one another to live. I hold Mother dear as my own life—without her, how could I face the remaining years?}
{align:justify|That morning I kept calling around for a master skilled at readings; I desperately wanted the ghost to leave—she seemed to bear me immense hatred. Zealous friends pointed me to various masters' addresses. Then I was bewildered, often asking myself: "What wrong could I have done—if indeed I have…" what should I do?…}
{align:justify|Amid the turmoil, I suddenly remembered: years before I had visited a gifted lady reader. So I decided to go to her house. It was long ago, so I remembered little of her. After contacting her by phone, she asked me to come after eleven in the morning. So past eleven, I arrived as agreed.}
{align:justify|Arriving, I learned she follows Buddhism. Her house was airy and clean, enshrining a statue that looked familiar, though I could not tell which deity it was. After I told my story, the lady (let me call her Lan) recounted a thousand-year-old tale of vows, resolving my doubt.}
{align:justify|My first question then was:}
{align:justify|"Is my mother in any danger?" (For I feared for Mother most.) Lan answered:}
{align:justify|"Nothing at all!"}
{align:justify|Only then was I relieved, breathing easily. Then Lan settled her mind a while and told my former lives' tale. Briefly summarized:}
{align:justify|"In the first life, under the Zhou dynasty, we were husband and wife—the one (who calls your name nightly now) was then male, a prime minister. I was female, his concubine. But I killed my husband, forging the tangled grievous affinity of the first life.}
{align:justify|In the second life, under the Tang dynasty, the other was master, I servant. Again, listening to villains' instigation, I killed my master.}
{align:justify|Down to this present life, she is a ghost, come demanding my life." Lan also wrote me a poem, whose last two lines bear pondering, like: "Meeting Bodhi guarding us in peace, helping me dissolve this enmity"…}
{align:justify|Lan asked me:}
{align:justify|"Do you wish to dissolve it?"}
{align:justify|I nodded hastily. Obeying her, my mind raised no stray thoughts, thinking but one thing: I would surely carry out every request well. Then she had me kneel before the Bodhisattva's statue; I could not hold back tears, not knowing why I so wanted to weep. She set me three days: each day I must recite Amitabha Buddha three thousand times and chant two repentance sutras; she also gave me a copy of the "Universal Gate Chapter of Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva" and the "Diamond Sutra," and furthermore chanted mantras for me—though I could not understand, yet reciting Amitabha Buddha's name my tears streamed down, sobbing…}
{align:justify|Afterward, Lan said:}
{align:justify|"Rest easy! Tonight she will come no more…"}
{align:justify|My heart then half-believed, half-doubted; sincere though I was, meeting such a thing the first time, my heart brimmed with questioning doubt…}
{align:justify|Leaving Lan's house, it was already late afternoon; friends and I ate while discussing the matter. A companion who came along declared:}
{align:justify|"Who knows if it works—but I don't believe it anyway!"}
{align:justify|Just as she spoke, the poem's line surfaced in my mind: "Meeting Bodhi guarding us in peace… helping me dissolve this enmity"…}
{align:justify|I explained to my friend, but her face stayed full of doubt…}
{align:justify|Whatever my friend's criticism and scorn, I held my ground: "Promised is promised—I must do it!"}
{align:justify|After supper I went to Mother's, telling her everything. Mother too brimmed with doubt, finding it beyond imagining—living more than half a life, she said, this was her first encounter with so strange a thing. No one believed—even I still doubted—yet I had to act. For Mother, I must act!}
{align:justify|Bidding Mother farewell, I returned to my own warm nest; my husband worried for me but utterly disbelieved such queer affairs. So he said little about it.}
{align:justify|That evening, alone at home, I reread Lan's poem, recalling the ancient former lives recorded there, unable to stop tears flooding my face.}
{align:justify|Boundlessly sad, I thought: "How could I commit so grave a sin? A human body is so hard won, so precious… yet I heartlessly killed them twice—how could my hand be so cruel?"…}
{align:justify|Then I wept again, wondering silently why she had not awakened me sooner. Feeling utter misery, I opened the repentance sutra and chanted, tears streaming.}
{align:justify|Then I stayed three days at Mother's. On the road my mouth never ceased reciting the Buddha's name! At Mother's I chanted repentance sutras. Though my mind tensed to the utmost, sitting on the bed I recited the Buddha's name unceasingly, not knowing why my heart so wanted to weep. Glancing at the clock, I saw past 10:30. Today, truly, the voice calling my name came no more. Past midnight I dozed off, dreaming the girl held my hand in bright joy, her gaze seeming to say: "Very well done!"… I passed a peaceful night.}
{align:justify|Waking in the morning, my mind settled for now, though still a little worried. By evening, seeing Mother bustling about, my heart felt panicked and sad; wanting only quiet, I asked Mother to stop working and recite the Buddha's name with me. Then I noticed: wind blew in gusts, very cold. So cold I broke out in gooseflesh, sweating all over. I asked:}
{align:justify|"Did you open the window, Mother?"}
{align:justify|She answered:}
{align:justify|"No!"}
{align:justify|My hands froze; I felt yin energy flooding in to cover all—she had come beside me. It seemed she did not trust me. I told her to rest easy, solemnly promising I would recite the Buddha's name well, praying for her escape from misery's sea—begging her to trust me.}
{align:justify|Reciting the Buddha's name, I wept, reciting on until my mind grew peaceful and glad, able to smile. Then my hands gradually warmed again.}
{align:justify|On the third morning (the final day), I resolved to strive in recitation and repentance: mornings I chanted ten rounds, afternoons two, evenings two; throughout these three days I stayed in touch with Lan. When I visited Lan, she gave me layman Quả Khanh's "Karmic Retribution in Present Life." Reading it, I found so many excellent stories of cause and effect… I thought "I should recite the Ksitigarbha Sutra, dedicating it to those I once killed and harmed"… So that evening I chanted five rounds.}
{align:justify|That night I slept deeply. One thing I forgot to tell: on the second afternoon, something strange occurred. Then I craved to hear Amitabha-recitation music, so I searched online… Sitting quietly before the computer listening, tears covered my face, feeling myself a sinner… So I knelt, expressing remorse and repentance—only then noticing: the computer screen trembled violently while I wept on, and suddenly a scene appeared: I was carrying two kinds of fruit—bananas and grapes—to Lan's house. I did not understand it, so I paid little mind…}
{align:justify|The matter was at last resolved; on the third day, sure enough, the ghost's calling came no more. Brimming with gratitude, on the fourteenth lunar day I bought grapes and bananas for Lan (exactly the scene I had seen on the computer). Lan told me to bow before the Buddha's statue; bowing to Him, my heart swelled with emotion, moved to tears—a very strange feeling.}
{align:justify|On the afternoon of the fourteenth, my husband and I visited Mother; he bought donkey meat, which I normally love—I can eat seven or eight slices… But that day, at the fourth slice I could eat no more. Then my stomach suddenly ached, and I vomited violently.}
{align:justify|Then I thought: "Oh no! Could I have done something wrong again?"}
{align:justify|The next day, Mid-Autumn Festival (the full moon of the eighth month), I dreamed a terrifying nightmare: in the dream, a vast abyss yawned bottomlessly out of sight, echoing with the ghost's scolding voice:}
{align:justify|"Living as you do, when shall I ever escape misery? Prepare for your death!…"}
{align:justify|Startled bolt upright, dawn not yet fully come. Glancing at my husband sleeping soundly beside me, I gently composed myself, silently reciting: "Amitabha Buddha! Grant me the answer—just now the dream terrified me; what have I done wrong?"…}
{align:justify|Reciting a while, I dozed off; in dream a line of verse appeared—waking, I hurriedly wrote it down but could not recall it all, remembering clearly only four words: "GO VEGETARIAN, RECITE THE BUDDHA'S NAME!"}
{align:justify|Morning came, Mid-Autumn day; I told Mother everything and declared that from here on I would be vegetarian. The family all wondered why I refused meat; Mother, ever loving and caring toward me, guessed I must be dieting…}
{align:justify|Watching everyone eat meat and drink wine, I craved none of it. Thus I have eaten vegetarian ever since, never craving meat.}
{align:justify|The household gradually calmed; Mother's spirits improved too. Once very brave, now climbing to the rooftop terrace she feels a little fear.}
{align:justify|At work, in free moments she too recites the Buddha's name! She says her spirits are much better now. My husband, though unbelieving, does not oppose my vegetarianism and recitation—he needs only my peace and safety.}
{align:justify|Since meeting the Buddha's teachings, more than a month has passed; at first I practiced diligently because creditors demanded their debts—recently, hearing the teachings often, my heart stirs and tears flow unchecked.}
{align:justify|I am young, early successful, holding fame and fortune in hand… After living through this, I understand people's ties to one another, and see how precious life and deep affection are. Most important, I have truly awakened to and felt the Buddha's teachings' immense, inconceivable power—this moves me to the core—}
{align:justify|I deeply believe in karmic retribution; what happened has educated me, taught me life's meaning, and shown me to "tolerate others' faults, be strict with myself, live filially toward parents—in all deeds benefit self and others."}
{align:justify|In learning Buddhism I keep correcting, adjusting each small fault of mine. Naturally this reform demands sustained, persevering practice over long years—for whatever you do, the Buddha must always dwell in your heart.}
{align:justify|Thu Oanh}
{align:justify|Translator's note: In the Ksitigarbha Sutra, chapter six, it is recorded:}
{align:justify|"The Buddha taught: O Universal Vastness! If in future ages sentient beings dream of ghosts, spirits, and dreadful shapes or other apparitions, grieving, weeping, gloomy, sighing, and so on… these are all fathers, mothers, siblings, spouses, and kin of one life, ten lives, a hundred lives, a thousand lives past, now fallen into evil destinies, unable to escape, with no blessed power anywhere to look to for rescue—so they inform those of flesh-and-blood ties or past-life connections, hoping to find a way to save them from the evil paths. Therefore you should use spiritual power to make those (who meet such strange scenes) before the images of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, wholeheartedly recite this sutra, or invite others to read it three or seven rounds complete. Thus, though their kin dwell in evil destinies, hearing the recitation of those rounds complete, they shall be liberated, and appear in dreams no more."}
{align:justify|At first I doubted this "night-calling" phenomenon, and wondering, I could not finish translating the story—when suddenly I heard that a friend's relative died in a sudden accident, leaving life very young and an infant child behind. After her burial, every night the whole family heard her voice calling her child's name piteously…}
{align:justify|My friend is a monastic—naturally no lie in the account. So, confident and reassured, I strove to finish translating this story to share with readers.}
{align:justify|Below is the self-account of a psychic. Master Hsuan Hua often said: "One penetration, all penetration! Attain the path and psychic powers follow." The "psychic" here refers not at all to the weak-nerved haunted by yin attachments, nor the weak-willed who constantly see ghosts (while ordinary folk of strong yang never see them). The psychic meant here is one of special attainment, ever using his ability to guide the world toward the path of true self-reform.}
{align:justify|A few lines introducing Lien, the king of prophets:}
{align:justify|He is Taiwanese, famed as a great fortune-teller, able to contact the invisible realm we cannot see through. The one Lien Sinh communes with is named the "God of Fortune"; in the course of his work, the deeper he understood cause and effect, the more he revered Buddhism—taking refuge, vowing to strictly keep the five precepts, lifelong vegetarianism, dedicating his special ability to helping the world turn back toward goodness.}
{align:justify|Here is his fine essay sharing the law of cause and effect:}
{align:justify|THE GOD OF FORTUNE}
{align:justify|Friends, never lament your bad luck! For in truth, at the start of this human life, our blessings and destiny may have been enormous—meaning you could have attained and enjoyed superb, optimal fortune… But through ignorance of cause and effect we have unwittingly wrecked our blessings (through unvirtuous thoughts, words, and deeds).}
{align:justify|Our own unwholesome thoughts, words, and deeds have summoned and shaped our own stumbling, thwarted fate. Do not hastily call this reasoning superstition! Life truly holds a destiny (though we make that destiny ourselves, and it then "follows" us, binding us). But "destiny can still turn, according to the good or evil karma we create!"}
{align:justify|All whose careers succeed, who attain great wealth and rank, believe this principle. Living without wisdom, one easily sinks into hardship and poverty. Hearing me say this, please do not rush to scorn it as "superstition"—take the trouble to think and ponder to its source, or test it yourself and see (then conclude).}
{align:justify|I tell you: the senses' perceptions are untrustworthy—we are so often fooled by them! Consider: what percent of all existing light can human eyes see? What share of all existing sound can our ears hear? What percent of the world's smells can the nose smell? What percent of all flavors can the tongue taste? We can feel things—but what percent of all beings?}
{align:justify|Just because our senses cannot reach or fathom something (cannot see, hear, taste, or touch it)… shall we flatly declare it does not exist?…}

{align:justify|Back then I joined Survey Unit 5802. At the time the deputy regiment commander, Wei Qingping, held a stack of money and told me to answer at once how much he was holding.}
{align:justify|I said: Fourteen stacks!}
{align:justify|He himself did not even know exactly how much he was holding, so hearing my answer he counted it—and his eyes bulged in amazement, for the result matched exactly. This softened his atheist mind, and from then on he took refuge in Buddhism, resolving to recite the Buddha's name and chant sutras.}
{align:justify|There was a man of blunt, forceful speech (I call him "Iron Mouth"), who used to mock and laugh whenever he saw people believing my predictions. One day he came to me, chattered on about heaven and earth at length, then asked:}
{align:justify|"Can you guess what I did last night?"}
{align:justify|I answered: Played mahjong!}
{align:justify|This "Iron Mouth" froze for a moment, hardly daring to believe it—"how could it be so right?" He said:}
{align:justify|"Right, I did play mahjong! But can you guess how much I lost or won?"}
{align:justify|This was a great challenge, with everyone staring fixedly at me.}
{align:justify|I answered: You lost eight hundred and eighty coins!}
{align:justify|Iron Mouth shouted:}
{align:justify|"Right! Right! Right! Truly a godlike guess! Unbelievable… How can such a thing exist in this world?"}
{align:justify|Then Iron Mouth explained:}
{align:justify|"Originally I had lost only eight hundred. Having lost, I was about to leave when the friend beside me offered to lend me 80. I thought: 'Borrow these 80—maybe I can win it back!'… In the end I lost that too, exactly 880. That's how it happened!"}

{align:justify|A young man who did not believe in predictions was dragged to my place by his family. He sat huddled in a corner, wanting nothing to do with it. Urged by his family, he shouted:}
{align:justify|All you fortune-telling prophets are frauds—why believe a bunch of wandering charlatans peddling scams? Every one of them is a swindler!}
{align:justify|I calmly told him:}
{align:justify|"In this world there is the true and the false! There are indeed many swindlers. Why not use your wisdom and think for yourself, to tell the false from the true…?"}
{align:justify|He answered:}
{align:justify|"I don't care! You're just a swindler anyway!"}
{align:justify|I said: I know all about your affairs!}
{align:justify|"I don't believe it!" he retorted bluntly.}
{align:justify|I smiled and said: Yesterday you fell off your bike! Your right leg bled. Right?}
{align:justify|His eyes widened in astonishment, and he said:}
{align:justify|"I never told anyone—not even my family knew. Only I knew. How do you know so well?"}
{align:justify|Then he rolled up his trouser leg and showed everyone the wound. His right leg was indeed injured, the blood already dried.}
{align:justify|There are a great many such accurate predictions. But there are also many that miss. Why do they miss? Listen as I explain:}

{align:justify|A high official named Dang wished to run for Minister. Three others (named Zhao, Tran, and Luong) were contesting the post…}
{align:justify|Mr. Dang asked me:}
{align:justify|"Can I win election as Minister?"}
{align:justify|I answered:}
{align:justify|"Yes!"}
{align:justify|About half a year later, the one elected Minister was not Dang but Tran. Mr. Dang flew into a rage, came to interrogate me, blaming me for first saying he could become Minister—so why had it not come true?}
{align:justify|"What kind of fortune-telling is this? Is it accurate at all? Aren't you deceiving people? Explain yourself!" Mr. Dang snapped irritably.}
{align:justify|I blushed crimson, unable to utter a word.}
{align:justify|Mr. Dang asked again:}
{align:justify|"Didn't you once predict that I… 'would make it'? What is this, then?"}
{align:justify|I could only answer:}
{align:justify|"Truly, back then I heard the God of Fortune confirm it—whatever he said, I relayed exactly!…"}
{align:justify|"The God of Fortune? So where is this 'God of Fortune'?"}
{align:justify|"The God of Fortune is invisible! He oversees people's fortune and rank! I can see and speak with him, but you cannot…"}
{align:justify|"Utter nonsense!"}
{align:justify|Mr. Dang sneered, his extreme displeasure written all over his face.}
{align:justify|Just as I stood being scolded and questioned, you can picture the "sorry spectacle": utterly flustered at the time, completely dispirited, I could only gaze silently at the sky, crying out to heaven alone… But suddenly a beam of light shot into my eyes—the God of Fortune had appeared. He looked at me with a gentle smile and wrote out a single character: "LUST." Beneath the character he even recorded the exact day and month of the offense…}
{align:justify|I promptly told Dang:}
{align:justify|"You lost the election because you violated the precept against lust!"}
{align:justify|Mr. Dang answered:}
{align:justify|"No!"}
{align:justify|"On that very day… of that month!"}
{align:justify|Mr. Dang insisted:}
{align:justify|"There's really no such thing!"}
{align:justify|I was stunned: the God of Fortune had clearly written "LUST" with the offending day and month noted in plain detail, his instruction perfectly clear—how could it not exist? Unconvinced, I pressed:}
{align:justify|"Think it over carefully?!"}
{align:justify|Mr. Dang pondered, counting the days and months on his fingers for a while, and finally still answered:}
{align:justify|"No, there isn't!"}
{align:justify|At this point the God of Fortune explained to me: "On that day and month, Dang peeked at the neighbor girl bathing"…}
{align:justify|Hearing the God of Fortune charge him with "spying on a young lady bathing," I found it so funny inside I could hardly keep from laughing. I told Dang:}
{align:justify|"It wasn't adultery or lovemaking… but peeking at… the neighbor girl bathing!"}
{align:justify|Mr. Dang, hearing this, froze for a moment, said nothing, then slunk away with bowed head!}
{align:justify|As far as I know, Mr. Dang's offense went like this: "Rightly, Mr. Dang deserved to become Minister, but a few months earlier a young woman had moved in next door to him. She was very beautiful—tall and lovely. Mr. Dang noticed her and kept staring. His house had a window facing the girl's bathroom. On that day… of that month… the neighbor girl entered the bathroom, forgot to draw the curtain, and was seen by Mr. Dang… He fetched his binoculars, raised them, and gazed at her from head to toe and toe to head… his heart wildly excited, his mouth sighing in admiration, murmuring his wish: If only I could spend one night with her, my life would not be wasted! At that moment Mr. Dang's eyes looked, his mind fantasized, his heart brimming with wicked thoughts…}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune said: Although Mr. Dang committed no act amounting to a crime with the neighbor girl, still—had he glimpsed her bathing, a decent man would have turned away at once. Instead, not only did Mr. Dang fail to turn away, he picked up his telescope and studied her minutely from head to toe; not only did his eyes stir and his heart stir, but lustful intent blazed up fiercely… though it led to no act of adultery, it still counted as violating the precept against lust! That is why he lost the ministerial post! Tell him: he must cultivate virtue and reform himself for about six more years before he can serve as Minister…}

{align:justify|Once, Lieutenant General Lu Co came to see me and said:}
{align:justify|"Master Lien, I hear your predictions are brilliant, so today I have come to ask your advice… I recall that my parents once consulted Master Diep (also a king of diviners, famed for never-erring prophecy), asking him to read my fortune.}
{align:justify|Mr. Diep said that at eighteen I would place first nationwide, then in the army be selected for the Research Institute; at twenty-seven I would earn a doctorate, then spend three years in America earning another doctorate. At fifty-three I would rise in rank to Full General.}
{align:justify|Lu Co went on:}
{align:justify|"This Mr. Diep is unlike other masters: whoever wants him to read their fortune must pay a very high price and queue up with an appointment—walk in casually and he won't see you at all! And he won't read for anyone of small destiny either! Mr. Diep read mine with utter accuracy! True enough, at eighteen I took first place nationwide; as for his prediction that at twenty-seven I would earn my doctorate, he was off by a little (for it was two years later—only at twenty-nine—that I earned it). But his prophecy of my three years in America earning another doctorate was spot on! Yet he said that at fifty-three I would rise to Full General, and there he was wrong! For now I am fifty-six and still a Lieutenant General, nowhere near Full General. So I would ask Master Lien to see for me: why have I still not been promoted to Full General?"}
{align:justify|I used divination to read for Lu Co. I formed the mudra, finished reciting the mantra, and the God of Fortune appeared like a tiny star, growing and radiating brilliant light… I then asked about Mr. Lu's whole life. The God of Fortune answered exactly as Master Diep had predicted.}
{align:justify|I wondered:}
{align:justify|"But why was Mr. Lu two years late in earning his doctorate?"}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune explained:}
{align:justify|"Rightly, Mr. Lu could have graduated as predicted, but in his youth, after a drinking bout, he followed his friends to a brothel for amusement; egged on by them, and wanting to prove himself bold and dashing, he slept with a prostitute. Because of this fault his career was delayed by two years!"}
{align:justify|I asked:}
{align:justify|"Why should that be?"}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune said:}
{align:justify|"Never visit brothel prostitutes, never linger at the gates of painted beauties." A gentleman always cherishes his own person and guards his virtue bright as jade, and so dreads and shuns such filthy affairs! Through ignorance, Mr. Lu willingly sank, staining his own character and tainting his life. Because of this wrongful way of living he ruined his fortune and harmed his body; reckless indulgence not only infected him with gonorrhea but invited great calamity…}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune further explained:}
{align:justify|"A two-year delay in the degree is but a light punishment. The venereal poison he caught would surely kill him. Doctorate upon doctorate earned… yet hereafter he will become a doctor… of death!"}
{align:justify|My tongue shrank—I was speechless. Then I asked the God of Fortune:}
{align:justify|"Rightly, at fifty-three Lu Co should have risen to Full General—so why, at fifty-six, is he still a Lieutenant General? What blocks his promotion?"}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune silently wrote two characters: MAC THU.}
{align:justify|Bewildered, I asked:}
{align:justify|"Mac Thu?—What does that mean?"}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune answered:}
{align:justify|"It is a person's name!"}
{align:justify|"And what has this person to do with Mr. Lu?"}
{align:justify|"Everything!"}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune said:}
{align:justify|"Lu Co's destiny is excellent—he is a rare genius, excelling in both letters and arms. But alas, in youth his fault of sleeping with a prostitute delayed his career by two years—and that is only a light punishment! After middle age, unrepentant and unreformed, he again gave rise to wicked thoughts, craving male beauty: he fell into homosexual love with a man named Mac Thu. This man is handsome and talented, dashing and refined—his subordinate! Lu Co has lived with Mac Thu for eight years now. With this offense, keeping his Lieutenant General's rank is already lucky—how could he dream of Full General? He only knows how to crave rank for himself, utterly blind to the grave karma he is creating! Know that one rich in blessings who commits sexual misconduct loses power and rank, while one of scant blessings, with no rank left to lose, loses years of life…"}
{align:justify|I asked:}
{align:justify|"Then… what lies ahead for Lu Co?"}
{align:justify|"It will fall upon his descendants!"}
{align:justify|"What does that mean?"}
{align:justify|"Childlessness, early death!" the God of Fortune answered.}
{align:justify|"But each bears his own deeds—why must his descendants carry it?"}
{align:justify|"This is collective karma drawing them together. A father who stores up blessings also enriches himself and his children!"}
{align:justify|Hearing this, I was deeply shaken.}
{align:justify|First I told Lu Co only why his doctorate had come two years late.}
{align:justify|Lu Co said:}
{align:justify|"That happened. In youth, running with friends, they lured me along to buy pleasure. I never imagined it would bring such ill consequences—beyond all expectation!"}
{align:justify|Then I explained why he could not become Full General. I wrote the reason in two words: "Mac Thu." He glanced at it, then bowed his head in silence, saying nothing.}
{align:justify|I asked:}
{align:justify|"Is it true?"}
{align:justify|He nodded:}
{align:justify|"Yes!"}
{align:justify|Then Lu Co stood up and said to me:}
{align:justify|"Lien Sinh, you are truly the king of prophets—your readings are godlike! At last I understand: though a person's destiny is fixed, it can change! Shifting back and forth, all of it is made by the mind alone!"}
{align:justify|"Well said! I hope you understand, and from now on reform yourself well to avoid evil retribution!"}
{align:justify|As Lu Co left, I handed him a slip of paper with a few lines of counsel:}
{align:justify|Husband and wife, man and woman dwelling together}
{align:justify|That is the right way—no error allowed!}
{align:justify|Crooked lust invites deep harm…}
{align:justify|Both self and others are ruined; rank and fortune alike are lost}
{align:justify|Descendants cut off—such sorrow}
{align:justify|Wealth scattered, a thousand years of grief}
{align:justify|Halt and wake up, quickly, quickly}
{align:justify|Lest your children fall into calamity}
{align:justify|To be human, live upright and true}
{align:justify|Karmic retribution never errs—it is clear!}
{align:justify|Not long after, Lu Co's only son was struck and killed by a vehicle. As for Lu Co himself, calamity befell him too—childless indeed, just as foretold!}

{align:justify|Vien Mau was the director of a metal workshop. Long ago, when he came to ask about his destiny, the God of Fortune answered:}
{align:justify|"Fifteen years from now you shall become a great wealthy merchant."}
{align:justify|The result: more than ten years later, Vien Mau's business failed and he had to close down; having borrowed far too much capital, debts piled mountain-high, and he fled abroad to escape his creditors. From then on he drifted in exile, unable ever to return home.}
{align:justify|Abroad, Vien Mau lived in great hardship. He peddled small goods with meager earnings, then hired himself out as a construction laborer—once the director of a factory, now clambering up and down strangers' rooftops; unskilled at the trade, he was dismissed. Vien Mau then took kitchen work, scraping by day to day.}
{align:justify|Later, hearing I had settled overseas, Vien Mau rode a freight truck to find me. He swayed atop the truck for three days and two nights before reaching me.}
{align:justify|Meeting him that morning, I started. The Vien Mau of old wore elegant Western suits, hair slicked glossy, stepping out to a polished car with driver and secretary in attendance… But the Vien Mau of today: grizzled hair, face lined with wrinkles, weathered and shabby, haggard and despondent, wretched and pitiable…}
{align:justify|I invited him in, poured tea, served refreshments—he looked as though he had eaten nothing.}
{align:justify|Vien Mau asked:}
{align:justify|"You said that fifteen years later I would be a great rich man—is this the time?"}
{align:justify|"How many years has it been, then?"}
{align:justify|Vien Mau answered:}
{align:justify|"Exactly fifteen!"}
{align:justify|Then he added accusingly:}
{align:justify|"Plainly you spoke wrong—your readings are worthless!…"}
{align:justify|"I…" (tongue-tied, words failing).}
{align:justify|Vien Mau's face showed aggrieved displeasure as he asked:}
{align:justify|"Why are your readings wrong, your words worthless?"}
{align:justify|"That is…"}
{align:justify|Vien Mau went on:}
{align:justify|"Long ago, when I was still a glorious director, I invited you to my plant to read the feng shui, design and correct every spot exactly as you taught… I also asked you to read my fortune—you affirmed that fifteen years later I would surely strike it rich, reaching the absolute pinnacle of success, the highest point of my life. Now it is exactly fifteen years—nothing in sight; instead, more wretched than ever! How do you answer?!"}
{align:justify|"I…" (sweating all over)…}
{align:justify|Vien Mau said:}
{align:justify|"Now I am at the end of my road—what do you say I should do?"}
{align:justify|Let me read for you again, shall I?…}
{align:justify|"Read again?… What good ever came of reading?" Vien Mau flared.}
{align:justify|I closed my eyes… and suddenly saw the God of Fortune, leading a small child by each hand…}
{align:justify|I asked: Who are these children?}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune answered:}
{align:justify|"Both are Vien Mau's children!"}
{align:justify|"Ah! I understand—for more than ten years Vien Mau has killed, aborted… hence these two infant ghosts."}
{align:justify|So I cried out:}
{align:justify|"Vien Mau, you are guilty of killing—your lover aborted two children…"}
{align:justify|Vien Mau answered:}
{align:justify|"True! But can abortion really be so grave an offense?"}
{align:justify|The God of Fortune appeared again, shaking his head at me; he raised a hand toward empty space, where a hermitage appeared, and out stepped a beautiful, graceful nun leading two small children by the hand…}
{align:justify|At that I was terrified (understanding the whole cause)…}
{align:justify|I said:}
{align:justify|"Vien Mau! You sowed seeds of a short life, committing the grave crime of defiling a monastic. This is why your blessings and rank collapsed and you sank into utter destitution! Those two children are hers, are they not?"}
{align:justify|Vien Mau's face turned ashen, sweat pouring over him! He stammered:}
{align:justify|"But… that nun loved me too!"}
{align:justify|"Heavens!" I sighed. "You must know this is a great taboo! Every monastery has precepts and Dharma protectors… monastics are those who have vowed to leave the mundane life for pure practice—if you go seducing a monastic, the offense grows by a degree. It was your unchecked conduct, daring to raise lust toward a nun until she bore two children, then having them aborted and killed… How enormous this sin is—your present dead end is of your own making!"}
{align:justify|"Is that so?"}
{align:justify|"Certainly!" I answered.}
{align:justify|"Then what must I do?"}
{align:justify|"You must vow to strictly keep the Buddha's prohibitive precepts, vowing to correct your faults and renew yourself from here on. Confess all your faults before the Buddha, sincerely bow in repentance again and again… and from now on, in every stirring thought, be careful and watchful, swearing never again to touch sexual misconduct. Forever cut off the very root of sin, act always openly and uprightly, and truly step onto the path of proper reform. Nor is that all: hereafter your mind must always hold wholesome thoughts, and at all times (by mouth or in writing) urge worldly people never to commit sexual misconduct, and never to raise wicked thoughts toward monastics. The sutras teach: 'One who keeps the precept against sexual misconduct sees the five blessings grow, and falls not into the three evil destinies'… You must strive in repentance, lifelong vegetarianism, diligent sutra recitation, doing good… only then may you redeem and eradicate your evil. Toward monastics especially, wholeheartedly support them in guarding their chaste, pure conduct. For the cultivator's goal is attaining the path, ending birth and escaping death; in the 'Four Teachings on Purity' of the Shurangama Sutra, the Buddha gave monastics most explicit instruction: 'They must cut off and eliminate the lustful mind!' This is the cultivator's first indispensable condition—the lustful mind must absolutely not be harbored. For nursing it, one shall surely stray onto the demons' path. Should you or anyone raise wicked thoughts to harass monastics, that is demonic conduct, and after death the retribution shall be utterly dreadful…"}
{align:justify|Hearing this, Vien Mau promised to comply. I gave him a few cautionary verses:}
{align:justify|One who has left the world to practice}
{align:justify|May not indulge in love's delights}
{align:justify|Wrecking a cultivator's precepts and conduct}
{align:justify|Denies the Buddha's house its purity}
{align:justify|The gods' eyes see to the very end}
{align:justify|Men and women in secret trysts}
{align:justify|Dreadful, harrowing evil retribution!}
{align:justify|Quickly reform—cut off wrong!}
{align:justify|I saw Vien Mau off, giving him two thousand dollars as capital, hoping he would henceforth resolve to practice, forever correcting his faults and renewing himself. Vien Mau's story reminds me of the film "The Thorn Birds," telling of a true-cultivating priest's love for a young girl, who toward the priest ever raised desire and a conquering heart…}
{align:justify|Today's films, books, and media love to trumpet tangled clergy-lay romances that never succeed, calling them beautiful loves—eager to screen, post, and publicize them… whether true or not, this corrupts morals and shames cultivation… greatly harming society's moral climate…}
{align:justify|A cultivator who violates lust must, by cause and effect, understand: the retribution is harrowing and dreadful. For having understood the teachings yet still offended.}
{align:justify|As for worldly people, the sutras say: "One who creates lust karma shall be repaid: spouse unfaithful, line cut off without descendants, after death fallen into the three evil destinies—becoming animals, hungry ghosts, hell beings—for hundreds of thousands of ages, hardly escaping, never human again"… These words are no scare tactics—they are entirely true!}
{align:justify|Not to speak of distant hell retribution—of flower-retribution alone: in this very life, violators of the lust precept lose position, ruin honor, squander wealth! The lustful suffer many ills, age early, live not long, are reviled by society, bind deep enmity, and end ruined in body and name!}
{align:justify|I think of the women and men who seduce monastics, and the tangled unwholesome ties between those within the faith, or between faith and world. Forbidden affairs that should never be… yet they exist. (Naturally, monastics are not yet sages—they are ordinary humans, not saints; who is without fault?) But let me remind you all: having resolved to practice, as monastics you must guard the precepts as your very life, understanding clearly the frightful, measureless retribution lust brings—this fault must never be taken lightly, never treated as jest or play!}
{align:justify|Remember these true stories I have told, and stay on guard. You must often repent your faults, cultivate diligently, and strictly keep the precepts to redeem your errant thoughts—only then can such sins be cut off.}
{align:justify|The lust precept is one of Buddhism's four grave precepts; saints and sages long ago recognized lust's misery, and so abandoned worldly pleasure to reach pure ultimate bliss.}
{align:justify|Toward wholesome books and scriptures that benefit human hearts and the world's morals, we should proclaim and support them, spreading them widely. Understand clearly: chasing bodily pleasure is vain—indulging lust falls into utter misery. Do not sport along in jest, nor madly sing its praises…}
{align:justify|Guarding a bright and pure heart helps society grow good and peaceful… if the moral climate stays sound, people's hearts grow honest. In all things, lead by personal example. Keep yourself chaste, your character bright as jade! Never do what harms body and mind. No calamity is more terrible than lustful defilement. Harboring no wicked thought is itself self-cultivation!}
{align:justify|Lien Sinh}
{align:justify|Translator's note. I was struck by the author's opening line: "In truth, at the start of this human life, our blessings and destiny may have been enormous… but through our poor understanding of cause and effect, we have: unwittingly wrecked our own blessings—through unvirtuous thoughts, words, and deeds"…}
{align:justify|In China, "fortune-telling physiognomists" swarm thick as flies! Naturally some are true, some false (though the false always outnumber the true). Even so, fortune-telling is so common in China that for a few coins anyone can have a whole life's destiny read.}
{align:justify|Finishing this story, I hesitated and wavered greatly! Not knowing whether to publish it or shelve it—for some details troubled me…}
{align:justify|The Buddha speaks of six realms: gods, humans, asuras, hells, hungry ghosts, animals. We see only the human and animal realms, so the other four grow remote… and among humankind, a few see through to the invisible realm—many see ghosts, but few see heavens, dragons, hells…}
{align:justify|Gods belong to the non-human realm: wholesome gods and evil gods. Wholesome gods usually promote goodness and uphold the Buddha's precepts. Evil gods answer the five desires and promote lustful deviance (for having created blessings and practiced, they too became gods).}
{align:justify|They say "Buddha and demon differ by a single thread." So close a distance means straying is easy—one slightly improper thought and you fall into the deviant realm at once!}
{align:justify|See the figures in these stories: richly blessed, yet one small slip, one slight offense, and the loss is beyond imagining!}
{align:justify|Vien Mau himself, offending, was punished in this very life—though only flower-retribution (bloomed, not yet fruited), it sent him plunging wretchedly. As for the nun who offended—how to explain her? One might say deviant and upright, Buddha and demon are hard to tell apart—so those poor in understanding cause and effect, lacking care, though wearing the Buddha's robe, easily stray and become demons' disciples unawares… If, offending, one knows the offense, sincerely repents in remorse, strives to cultivate virtue, and never repeats it, then the deviant can turn upright.}
{align:justify|In old temples two gods are often enshrined, called Mr. Good and Mr. Evil. Mr. Evil's face is fearsome, eyes glaring, tongue lolling… yet why is he enshrined? Because he sweeps away the deviant aiding the upright, cutting off evil to assist good.}
{align:justify|I pondered dozens of times, shelving the story, deliberating three or four months… then at last decided to publish, hoping: "Perhaps many, reading it, will 'rein in the horse at the abyss's edge' in time, will 'carefully guard virtue in mind and will… to enrich their own blessings!'?"…}
{align:justify|Reading this story does not mean that, hearing anyone claim godhood, you shut your eyes and hail them long live—you must think, testing their words with wisdom: do they accord with the Buddha's five precepts, with the laws the Buddha laid down? For the precepts the Buddha laid down were not to glorify Himself, but to protect people living happily, never violating cause and effect, never drawing dreadful retribution.}
{align:justify|Mr. Liao Fan of old, hearing physiognomists predict a miserable destiny, resolved to "live wholesomely, cultivate virtue, and turn his fate"—proving "destiny lies in one's own hands!"}
{align:justify|Having read this story, if you wake up, guarding body, speech, and mind, striving to found conduct and cultivate virtue—most precious. But if you merely wait on gods, hoping someone will pronounce your fate for you, believing blindly then drifting along, sorrowing and rejoicing at the "master's" words—that is most unwise!}
{align:justify|Some claim to be immortals and saints, boasting miracles—yet belong to deviant possessors—as the "Shurangama Sutra" describes (up to fifty kinds of skandha-demons!) To tell them apart is easy—for such deviants love slandering precepts, praising meat-eating, lust, indulging wine and flesh… ever exalting their egos, claiming sainthood, yet quick to anger—everything aimed at cheating money, seeking gain…}
{align:justify|Lien Sinh in this tale is kind-hearted—ever aiming to help people enrich virtue. He even spent his own money helping Vien Mau rebuild his life.}
{align:justify|I have witnessed many families enjoying utmost wealth, fortune, and power—yet merely for this lustful deviance falling into ruin—short-lived, houses wrecked, lines destroyed. Pray forgive me for faithfully translating and publishing this story without omitting any detail. May all cherish their present blessings and guard themselves well—so blessings and wisdom daily grow, conduct ever purer.}
{align:justify|THAI MINH THONG}
{align:justify|Not long ago, Mr. Thai Minh Thong told me his family's true story of retribution:}
{align:justify|Minh Thong's great-grandfather was very rich, with vast fields, raising buffaloes for plowing.}
{align:justify|One day the great-grandfather was ringing a calf's nose; in pain it lashed out, striking a fatal spot, and its master died. Minh Thong's grandfather, seeing his father die tragically, blazed with rage, seized a mallet, and resolved to beat the young buffalo to death.}
{align:justify|The mother buffalo, seeing her master wield a weapon, hastily knelt, bellowing in grief, tears streaming from her eyes, pleading with him not to kill her calf—but the grandfather's rage burned uncontrollably, so he pounded the mallet again and again onto the calf's head until blood splattered and the calf dropped to the ground… The grandfather then hacked off its legs, hung the body on a tree, and—though the buffalo still lived—flayed it, slicing off flesh piece by piece, tormenting it to the utmost. Since local farmers eat no buffalo meat, he just sliced the flesh and flung it about the ground, leaving it to rot and breed maggots. Tortured and abused in agony, the buffalo endured this pain for three days before dying.}
{align:justify|Afterward, the grandfather dreamed a small child spoke to him:}
{align:justify|You made me part early from my mother's love, then cruelly flayed me, sliced my flesh… tormenting me three days before I died. I vow revenge, so that three generations of your line shall know no peace, suffering "house wrecked, line destroyed."}
{align:justify|Later the grandfather married and had a son (Minh Thong's father); when the boy turned three, his wife died. Three years after, the grandfather remarried.}
{align:justify|Minh Thong's father grew up, married, and fathered Minh Thong. When Minh Thong turned three, his mother left home for no reason. At six, his father remarried—so both generations of the Thais early lost their birth mothers and suffered stepmothers' abuse.}
{align:justify|Today, grandparents, parents—all have passed away. After marrying, Minh Thong fathered two children; but when the elder was three and the younger one, his tender marriage suddenly broke apart—when the elder turned six, he divorced his former wife and married his present one.}
{align:justify|As a boy, Minh Thong was struck hard on the head by his younger brother, gravely wounded. The dent remains. Ordinary people walk with straight shins and thighs, but whenever he walks, the cartilage at his knee juts out, knocking with each step—so the bone had to be surgically removed. Worse, serving in the army he developed belly pain; the doctor misdiagnosed "appendicitis—operate at once." But the injection accidentally damaged a spinal nerve, leaving him unable to bend and hardly able to stand long.}
{align:justify|Discharged, he then broke his leg in an accident; a steel pin inside now makes movement harder still. Nor is that all: before sleep, his wife must always pound his leg with a mallet before he can sleep—every single day.}
{align:justify|Minh Thong affirms: I am my grandfather reborn—my head gravely struck, my belly wrongly cut in pain, every night enduring the mallet pounding my leg to sleep—all repaying retribution!…}
{align:justify|This year Thai Minh Thong is thirty-one; attending a Buddha-recitation retreat he received a wondrous response, recalling former lives, which he recounted to me.}
{align:justify|Therefore, do not kill—release captive animals, and give rise to kindness aiding the poor and wretched. If we help all beings live in peace, our own lives shall be peaceful too.}
{align:justify|Told by Master Hue Tham, October 30, 1982 (translated from Tu Van Magazine, vol. 7)}
{align:justify|SNOW IN JUNE}
{align:justify|I always thought this tale a famous fictional script by Quan Hán Khanh—never imagining it was true.}
{align:justify|Seven or eight years ago, through a monastic at the City of Ten Thousand Buddhas, I visited Taiwan and heard Professor Truong recount in detail the strange events she lived through.}
{align:justify|Thirty years ago she did charity work in Taiwan, specializing in counseling—a pioneer lifting society's better trends—so Taiwan's charity workers all call her "Professor Truong." I know her real name, but here we shall call her Professor Truong.}
{align:justify|Today Professor Truong lives in America; I have spoken with her by phone. She is a lay practitioner, lifelong vegetarian, careful in speech, devoted to the Great Compassion Mantra and Buddha-recitation, traveling widely to spread the teachings. She is past seventy.}
{align:justify|Before telling her story, Professor Truong began:}
{align:justify|"What happened to me includes much that is uncanny—I think not everyone meets what I met. But just because you never met such things, do not hastily conclude they are wild fictions!}
{align:justify|Hearing my story, judge it as you will—I dare not object. But I tell it wholly truthfully, never uttering falsehood—on this point, trust me."}
{align:justify|This is a superb story of karmic retribution that I was fortunate to hear from Professor Truong's own lips; those holding high office, wielding power of life and death, should also read it—most beneficial. Now I record it to share with you:}
{align:justify|"Professor Truong entered university late (bearing children before sitting the exams), graduating at thirty-three (by then mother of three sons).}
{align:justify|At thirty-three, in May she found herself pregnant, expecting to deliver exactly on her thirty-fourth birthday. When her mother learned of it, she firmly demanded an abortion. Seeing her mother—long a Buddhist believer, vegetarian for years, strictly keeping the no-killing precept—now demanding her abortion, Mrs. Truong found it most strange; however she pressed, her mother would not explain why.}
{align:justify|During the pregnancy, most strange phenomena occurred. First: such discomfort inside that she must constantly bang her head against the wall. Second: when retching, she demanded her husband and three sons beat her, forcing them to strike with all their might (only then did she feel relief). If husband and sons refused, she turned on them. In former pregnancies she had never suffered anything like this.}
{align:justify|In the fourth month, her mother's friend—a monastic in Taichung—coming to Taipei on business, stopped by the house. Each meeting this monastic, Professor Truong would bow. This time, having bowed, she asked to bow once more.}
{align:justify|The master asked:}
{align:justify|"Why bow to me once more?"}
{align:justify|"I bow on behalf of the infant in my womb."}
{align:justify|The master said joyfully:}
{align:justify|"Very well—I accept the child. Since you wish the little one as my disciple, should anything happen hereafter, remember to tell me."}
{align:justify|In the sixth month, one day Professor Truong's mother craved a fine vegetarian dish sold only at a large Taipei restaurant. A most filial daughter, always wishing to please her mother, she exerted herself to go buy it. Leaving home, boarding the bus—strangely, though all aboard saw her great bulging belly, no one would yield a seat; some even feigned closed eyes to avoid seeing, and no one spared her a thought.}
{align:justify|So Professor Truong had to stand throughout. Suddenly the bus hit something heavy and braked hard, throwing her down and disturbing the pregnancy; she was rushed to emergency care.}
{align:justify|That night her belly ached violently; though the fetus was barely past six months, she delivered prematurely. Though still tiny, the babe was plainly a most beautiful, handsome boy.}
{align:justify|The doctors said the infant came far too early; they had done their utmost, but whether the babe could live they dared not promise—so Professor Truong should not hope too much…}
{align:justify|Professor Truong blamed herself bitterly, angry at not staying quietly home so the child might be born in peace. Now the babe could only lie in an incubator; she hoped the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas would bless and save him. Suddenly she remembered the master who had visited not long before, and telephoned to report:}
{align:justify|"Master, I have delivered—the baby is far too small. But I hope he may be saved."}
{align:justify|The master asked:}
{align:justify|"You wish to save him? Good! Then do as I require. From today, keep a hundred days' vegetarianism. What sutra do you usually recite?"}
{align:justify|"I specialize in the 'White-Robed Mantra.'" }
{align:justify|"Very well—recite this mantra, but ten thousand rounds, mind—if you wish to save the child, hang up and recite at once!"}
{align:justify|In the hospital, hearing her master's instruction, Professor Truong at once recited the White-Robed Mantra without pause. Except sleeping and resting, all remaining time she recited throughout.}
{align:justify|Before long the hospital discharged her. Home again, daily she recited the mantra before the Buddhas. Every day the same.}
{align:justify|When the babe turned one month old, that night she dreamed an evil dream: in dream the child kept dragging her, and she pulled back. Suddenly she let go; the child's face turned pitch black. She woke with a start, thinking the omen surely boded ill, and told her husband to telephone the hospital to ask how the child fared. It was three in the morning. Her husband hesitated to call—but as he wavered, the hospital called to report the babe had just died at three.}
{align:justify|Hearing it, Professor Truong burst into tears.}
{align:justify|Morning came, past six, and the master from Taichung hired a car to their house. Entering, she said at once:}
{align:justify|"Congratulations!"}
{align:justify|Bewildered, Professor Truong thought: "My little boy just died—why congratulations?"…}
{align:justify|But the master asked:}
{align:justify|"Did the infant pass at three this morning?"}
{align:justify|Amazed, Professor Truong said:}
{align:justify|"How do you know?"}
{align:justify|The master told the whole cause:}
{align:justify|"Last night (near midnight), the child came to bid me farewell. He said: I meant to take her away, but now I cannot seize her life. For toward her mother she is wholly filial—her mother craved a dish, and though great with child she strove to buy it for her mother, meeting the bus accident—leaving me too weak to live in her womb. I meant, exactly on her thirty-fourth birthday, to take her.}
{align:justify|The master asked:}
{align:justify|"Between you and her, what enmity is this?"}
{align:justify|The babe answered:}
{align:justify|"An age-old feud. Many lives ago she was a corrupt prefect who wrongly judged my case, and I died unjustly. So from her conception I began tormenting her—repaying the torture I suffered in prison. Making her butt her head against walls, having husband and sons… beat her, was washing away the hatred of the cruel torments I endured in jail."}
{align:justify|But now, since bearing me, she never ceases reciting the Buddha's name; through her recitation, Buddhas and Bodhisattvas ever shelter and bless her—so I cannot approach, with no chance to take her. Yet unreconciled, I ever wait. But her house holds a Buddha hall—I cannot enter at all, for the Buddha images ever guard and shield her—so now I lack the strength; wanting to drag her away, I cannot—so I must leave."}
{align:justify|The Dharma master told Professor Truong not to grieve for this child—he came for revenge, to seize her life and take her away…}
{align:justify|Hearing this, Professor Truong's mother at last revealed the family's hidden matter:}
{align:justify|Professor Truong's lineage held something most strange. Her grandfather, great-grandfather, and father all died exactly on their thirty-sixth birthdays. This ran through five straight generations: the line's eldest sons could never live past thirty-six—their fate, exactly on the thirty-sixth birthday, to leave the world. As for daughters, each eldest daughter died on her thirty-fourth birthday—none escaping…}
{align:justify|Though her mother knew this full well, she dared not tell Professor Truong, fearing to agitate her child—for Professor Truong too is an eldest daughter.}
{align:justify|This is why Professor Truong's mother firmly demanded the abortion.}
{align:justify|Professor Truong had three sons, each outstanding—above all the eldest: graduating college he went to America to study, then married and built a career. Two years ago, Professor Truong traveled from America to visit her second and youngest sons in Taiwan, when suddenly a call reported the eldest choking while bathing, now in emergency care. By the time she reached America, the eldest had died.}
{align:justify|To her it was a bolt from the blue; she collapsed, fainting. Through her mother's counsel she forced back the pain to complete his forty-nine days. By now she wept night and day—no comfort availed.}
{align:justify|Her youngest son came to Los Angeles, even leaving work to soothe his mother's grief. Professor Truong too hoped to climb out of the pain—so she thought of visiting a temple never once entered, hoping the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas might steady her mind.}
{align:justify|Ten years earlier Professor Truong had known of this temple, yet never visited. That day she went with her husband and youngest son; entering, she saw two people in the Buddha hall: one a Hong Kong Dharma master, one a lay resident. The Hong Kong monastic, seeing Mrs. Truong, stared fixedly—while Professor Truong then wept at the sight of anyone. The master asked why; learning the matter, he kept telling her:}
{align:justify|"Rest easy—I shall help you."}
{align:justify|Though first meeting, the master asked her telephone number and set tomorrow to meet. Thereafter weekly he met and talked with her, often saying: "The world is impermanent, ever parting in birth and death…" aiming to console and wake her—but she could never climb out of misery's abyss.}
{align:justify|Truly, all her life Professor Truong had done nothing to shame her conscience; in Taiwan she devoted herself to social charity, aiding countless youths and adults to study well, opening their wisdom. Why should this befall her?}
{align:justify|One day the master telephoned, inviting her over, saying:}
{align:justify|"I dearly wish to help you, but the cure must fit the disease. Yet I cannot trace your hidden cause and effect, so I cannot help. I have invited an Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) statue from the Mainland, now kept in my Buddha hall—are you fond of Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva?"}
{align:justify|"Since childhood I have deeply admired Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva!"}
{align:justify|The master said:}
{align:justify|"Arrange thus: to invite Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) Bodhisattva home, first buy sixty-six centimeters of yellow cloth, two bunches of chrysanthemums, with fruit, candles, incense… then tomorrow morning come to invite Her…"}
{align:justify|That day she went with her son to choose cloth. Yellow cloth abounded; wavering, not knowing which to buy, suddenly she saw a most unusual bolt—picked it up, ready to pay. The shopkeeper said:}
{align:justify|"This piece is not for sale—it is too short. Bought home, it could serve nothing." Measuring, Professor Truong found exactly sixty-six centimeters—so she bought it at once. Passing the flower stalls, exactly two bunches of chrysanthemums remained—she bought them too, and next day invited the Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) statue home.}
{align:justify|From then, for more than two months, Professor Truong still felt great misery, washing her face daily with tears. Once at the master's place, he said:}
{align:justify|"I must trace your cause and effect clearly—your case differs from ordinary people's; I must use special means to help you."}
{align:justify|Then he handed her a red paper and an envelope, saying:}
{align:justify|"On this paper write: metal, wood, water, fire, earth."}
{align:justify|Seeing her bewildered, he explained:}
{align:justify|"Metal: write of family background, birth origins. Wood: write of friends and work. Water: write the names of your whole family. Fire: what injustices have you in this life? Earth: what wishes have you? But heed one point: absolutely let no one see it—including husband, children, even myself."}
{align:justify|Written, then on that day, hour, month, year… facing that direction… read it five times, three days running, fifteen readings in all. Done, burn it, place the ashes in this envelope beneath your pillow.}
{align:justify|Professor Truong did exactly as told, writing more than sixteen hundred characters, reading fifteen rounds complete, burning it, placing it in the envelope beneath her pillow.}
{align:justify|A week passed; the master telephoned, asking:}
{align:justify|"In this time has any phenomenon occurred?"}
{align:justify|Professor Truong answered:}
{align:justify|"No."}
{align:justify|On the tenth day, the master called to say:}
{align:justify|"Congratulations—I have traced your cause and effect clearly; come quickly to my place."}
{align:justify|Her youngest son accompanied her.}
{align:justify|At the master's, she saw a very large yellow paper on his desk, densely covered with writing.}
{align:justify|The master said:}
{align:justify|"Last night I sat in meditation till dawn, at last tracing everything from the water altar. Meaning: whatever characters appeared in the water, I copied out—hours to finish copying—all recorded on this paper. Read it and you shall understand at once."}
{align:justify|Then the master told her:}
{align:justify|"Two more names for you to see." She glanced and said:}
{align:justify|"These two I surely know—they have come to collect their debts from me."}
{align:justify|The master said:}
{align:justify|"Do you hate these two deeply?"}
{align:justify|"Yes."}
{align:justify|The master said:}
{align:justify|"You should not hate them—in a former life you owed them; hate no more. Everything bears its own cause and effect."}
{align:justify|"Next the master handed her the yellow paper, saying:}
{align:justify|Reading the first passage, your heart shall fill with fear; reading the second, you shall marvel greatly; reading the third, you shall feel boundless comfort.}
{align:justify|So she began reading the document—very long: the whole large page detailing her former life. Astonishingly, this story of karmic retribution matched what the Taichung master told nineteen years before—only here recorded more fully, with names and surnames complete:}
{align:justify|It is history's famed "Dou E case," known to all—a wholly true story recorded thus:}
{align:justify|"Professor Truong in a former life was Prefect Tao Wu, a corrupt official, wicked in every way: he threw Dou E into prison, wrongly sentencing her to death. Dou E thereby bound a blood-deep feud with the corrupt official, vowing lifetime after lifetime to hunt him for vengeance, bringing his clan ever to 'house wrecked, line destroyed' (reading this passage, she trembled with fear).}
{align:justify|Briefly, the "Dou E case":}
{align:justify|"Dou E, motherless from childhood, had a father—a poor scholar named Dou Tianzhang—who, wishing to sit the capital exams yet lacking travel money, borrowed twenty taels of silver from the widowed mother-in-law surnamed Cai.}
{align:justify|Unexpectedly, the next year the debt (at high interest) became forty taels; Mr. Zhang unable to repay, had to give his daughter Dou E both as debt payment and as a child-bride raised by the Cai household.}
{align:justify|Ten years in the Cai house, Dou E was wed into the Cai line. But within two years, her husband died. Widowed, Dou E stayed to keep her mother-in-law company.}
{align:justify|At the time, in Chuzhou lived Zhang Lu'er, a ruffian: conspiring with old Zhang his father, together they bullied the two widowed Cais, forcing Mrs. Cai to marry old Zhang. To save her life, the mother-in-law had no choice but to accept old Zhang. But Zhang Lu'er's ambition stopped not there—he pressed Dou E to marry him. Dou E firmly refused, and Zhang Lu'er plotted to ruin her.}
{align:justify|One day her mother-in-law fell ill, and Dou E cooked a nourishing soup to serve her. But Zhang Lu'er secretly poisoned the soup, scheming first to kill Mrs. Cai, then force Dou E to wed him.}
{align:justify|Unexpectedly, Mrs. Cai felt nauseous and did not take the soup; old Zhang, seeing fine fare, seized and finished it. Moments later the poison struck—he fell and died.}
{align:justify|Events fell wholly outside his plan: meaning to kill his stepmother, Zhang Lu'er killed his own father. Deceitfully, he pinned the murder on Dou E and sued before the officials.}
{align:justify|Chuzhou's prefect was corrupt; bribed heavily by Zhang Lu, he tortured Dou E to confess. But Dou E would rather be beaten to death than confess—so the prefect turned to torturing and beating Mrs. Cai. Pitying her mother-in-law, to save her from the lash and torture, Dou E swallowed tears and confessed.}
{align:justify|Thus sentenced to death, she was bound and led to the execution ground. Then Dou E, heart brimming with grief and rage, pointed at heaven and earth, crying:}
{align:justify|"Heaven—letting the worthy suffer oppression and wrongful judgment—and you call yourself heaven? Earth—knowing neither good from bad, straight from crooked—and you call yourself earth?"}
{align:justify|As the headsman raised his great blade, lightning flashing, Dou E looked up at heaven and loudly read three vows, swearing:}
{align:justify|"If my sentence is unjust, then when the blade passes my neck, my blood shall not fall but fly straight to the white banner—not one drop of fresh blood staining my corpse upon the ground."}
{align:justify|"Throughout the sixth month (high summer), heaven must send snow three feet deep covering my corpse."}
{align:justify|"Dead, all Chuzhou prefecture must suffer three straight years of drought, proving my wrongful death."}
{align:justify|Her vows sworn, the headsman swung—the blood indeed flew straight to the white banner; next, all June heaven sent great snow covering her corpse. And after her death, Chuzhou suffered three years' drought.}
{align:justify|Dou E's wishes were fulfilled, proving plainly her wrongful death. This sounded an earth-shaking summons: all Chuzhou's people hated the corrupt Tao Wu utterly, sympathizing deeply with young Dou E wrongfully slain"…}
{align:justify|This historical tale later inspired many scripts, one titled "Snow in June"—applauded through a hundred thousand years; its author "Quan Hán Khanh" grew famed, regarded as a master, a world-class playwright.}
{align:justify|But in the performed script a conclusion was added:}
{align:justify|"After Dou E's father Dou Tianzhang passed the exams to high office, he came by imperial order to Chuzhou to survey the people's plight, finding the region drought-stricken, three years without a drop of rain. Thinking surely an injustice lay behind it, all that night he kept lamplight re-examining Dou E's case. Suddenly light flashed—Dou E appeared, telling her injustice in full, begging her father to uphold justice.}
{align:justify|Dou Tianzhang investigated the case minutely, retried it, declared Dou E innocent, sentenced Zhang Lu'er to death, executed at Chuzhou. The sentence pronounced, heaven rained in torrents"…}
{align:justify|Reading the second passage, Mrs. Zhang marveled indeed—for on that day… she had written this "Heavenly Memorial" (exactly as the master taught, then burned)—revealing it to none—yet the master had received its whole contents and copied them out (exactly the 1633 characters she wrote), like a photocopy.}
{align:justify|Reading the third passage, the master had traced and concluded: "In this very life, Professor Truong has never done anything evil. In Taiwan she aided countless young men and women to study well, opening their wisdom. She is a filial daughter, serving her mother wholeheartedly, daring disobey her in nothing"…}
{align:justify|So the master advised her now to strive in repentance. He said:}
{align:justify|"You should sincerely repent, diligently learn Buddhism, cultivate vigorously."}
{align:justify|Reading the final passage, she felt extraordinary comfort—bowing to the master, thanking him for all he had done for her.}
{align:justify|The master hastily said:}
{align:justify|"No need to thank me—rightly I must thank you. Do you know why I came here? For had I not traced your cause and effect, I might never return to Hong Kong!"}
{align:justify|Seeing Mrs. Zhang bewildered, the monastic explained::}
{align:justify|"My master in Hong Kong has entered retreat—rightly 'asking nothing of worldly affairs,' no longer minding the world. But long ago he received your kindness (once you saved him), and in retreat he discovered you in calamity, greatly suffering… So he sent me to bring this Avalokiteshvara (Guanyin) statue to Los Angeles and find you—failing, never dream of returning to Hong Kong…"}
{align:justify|My first day here, I knew roughly your age (though you lived nearby)—but Los Angeles is so vast, where could I search?… so I was certain return looked hopeless.}
{align:justify|I entered this temple, asking hopefully whether any woman like you… happened by—never dreaming you would appear the next day. The moment I met you I knew: this is the one I seek, the very one my master sent me to meet… But then I could not say so plainly, only eagerly promising to help…}
{align:justify|Now your cause and effect is traced—I shall carry these papers to Hong Kong, for my master wishes to help you; in Hong Kong now a Platform is raised, a most solemn, grand 'Ten Thousand Buddhas Repentance' assembly.}
{align:justify|For this is a former-life life-debt, an age-old injustice most hard to deliver—only an immense assembly may hope to dissolve it.}
{align:justify|Now you need worry no more; hereafter you shall have the chance to visit Hong Kong and bow to my master. What you must do now is repent daily.}
{align:justify|Professor Truong, in that moment moved, feeling her sins so deep and heavy, at once quit her work, vowing to spend her remaining days single-mindedly in repentance"…}
{align:justify|Finishing her story, Professor Truong concluded:}
{align:justify|"None of us must pass through such uncanny events; but from others' stories we too may draw fine lessons!}
{align:justify|I only regret repenting too late—if possible, I hope you all first hasten to repent, and whatever karma exists shall surely dissolve."}
{align:justify|Learning Professor Truong's story, this writer was profoundly moved and spared no ink recording it fully, hoping the destined may read it and wake, reforming body and mind…}
{align:justify|I take full responsibility for this article's truthfulness; Professor Truong now lives in Los Angeles, and my senior in America has checked and confirmed it entirely true.}
{align:justify|Recorded by Phuc Duc Hai}

{align:justify|Should readers wonder: Mrs. Truong created offenses and bore suffering—that is settled—but why did her kin also invite shared calamity? If you have read the retribution books, you will understand "collective karma drawing together"! The corrupt Tao Wu of old did not create evil alone—he had his whole faction. Superiors' and subordinates' ties can become kinship in later lives. So by the workings of cause and effect: "With hatred, they come seeking revenge; with debts, they must be repaid." Thus her whole clan had to taste Dou E's revenge. I sought out and translated the extra story "Thai Minh Thong" from another book, placing it here to help readers see the principle of karmic retribution more clearly—understanding that one person may be reborn back and forth within a lineage, bearing retribution in succession…}
{align:justify|Finishing this story, I was most astonished: Professor Truong in this life is a virtuous woman, yet in a former life a wicked corrupt official? (Comparing past and present, they seem two different people—utterly opposite.) But it is easily understood: after judging Dou E's case, merely witnessing blood flying to the white banner, snow falling in June, then three years' drought… added to the people's laments, blame, and curses… sufficed to drench him in sweating fear, giving rise to a fiercely repentant, practicing mind… And precisely through that remorseful, earnest reforming heart, Tao Wu passed from a wicked criminal of a past life into a virtuous person of this life—every trace of evil faults erased, so that examining this life, not a shred of past wicked habit remains—only the victim's hatred follows lifetime after lifetime seeking revenge.}
{align:justify|As for Dou E, she is a special girl: usually daughters-in-law clash with mothers-in-law, but Dou E differed—she treated her mother-in-law most well; unable to bear seeing her beaten, she accepted sacrifice, confessed, and bore a wrongful death sentence. One may say hers was a virtuous person—so every vow she uttered came true.}
{align:justify|I recall reading a Western story (though I know not whether it is true) of a death-row inmate wrongly sentenced, who pointed at the courtroom authorities, swearing his grave would never grow grass—to prove his innocence.}
{align:justify|Afterward in the cemetery, all other graves greened with grass—only the convict's grave never grew grass at all, unsettling the powerful figures; they had grass brought and planted upon it, but the grass died—the wrongfully executed man's grave forever grassless… (it seems the curses of the virtuous wrongfully sentenced are always fulfilled)…}
{align:justify|Usually in common folk status, where possessions are modest, people have less chance to err—compared with holding high office and rank. I recall Princess Thang Hoa in "Karmic Retribution in Present Life" vols. 1–2: as a princess she seized commoners' husbands and killed without blinking—cross her and she killed; arrogant, overbearing, powerful, cruel… But reborn a commoner, she is most gentle—daring not kill even a crab, even earnestly reciting the Buddha's name praying her husband would not kill it… (the same person—past cruel and wicked, present gentle and good)… All such changes generally spring from remorseful repentance, a fierce vow to end evil and practice good… Perhaps thereby, reborn, those wicked ones erased all base habits, carrying only good ways and wholesome conduct… as provisions into the next life… a joy for them. But not all know how to wake and reform so strongly…}
{align:justify|How true the Buddha's words: creating deluded blessings (creating without wisdom) is third-life karma! For blessings created in abundance, if enjoyed in heaven till exhausted, one shall fall. If enjoyed on earth in fortune, one is born powerful, rich… and precisely through that wealth, power, beauty… people gain more chances to sin! So the Buddha ever reminds us to practice the true teachings, so lifetime after lifetime wisdom lights our road, saving us from falling.}
{align:justify|CONFESSIONS OF A WOMAN ENGINEER}
{align:justify|I live in Bao Thanh County, Heilongjiang Province—a woman with an incurable cancer.}
{align:justify|At thirty-seven, I developed end-stage gallbladder cancer. My gallbladder has since been removed, but removing it at the terminal stage helped little, unlike catching it early. For at the final stage the cancer cells had spread throughout my body, easily flaring everywhere. After the gallbladder's removal, my uterus grew three more tumors, then my left ovary one, and within half a year my right ovary another. Next my throat grew a tumor—all who hear me speak notice my hoarse voice.}
{align:justify|My health utterly failed; I could undergo no major surgeries—so uterus, ovaries, and the rest could not be operated on… Only the throat tumor, plainly visible with a mirror down an open mouth, could be cut. My voice remains hoarse. Then my stomach grew a tumor too, my rectum diseased as well—people call it hemorrhoids, but wrongly: that spot too grew cancer.}
{align:justify|My torments were not yet done—the disease spread to my lungs, most viciously: the upper half of my left lung held five tumors. Sickness racked and tormented me… till I fainted clean away.}
{align:justify|I often coughed blood, blood thick with clotted impurities, coughing up mouthful after mouthful.}
{align:justify|All my illnesses came from the evil karma of killing. I sold fishing gear for catching seafood and supplied all kinds of aquatic creatures for people's tables.}
{align:justify|I worked from 1981; though my registration was in Bao Thanh County, my trade spread everywhere, from countryside to city…}
{align:justify|The cancers tormenting my whole body all came from killing karma (only after learning Buddhism did I understand).}
{align:justify|Before knowing the path, I had two abortions—great evil karma. When illness felled me, I collapsed onto bed, lying gasping… I tasted misery to the full: above, racking coughs; below, urine spurting to soak my trousers—so I wore pads, for each cough leaked urine at once—besides other diseases racking and tormenting me, misery past telling.}
{align:justify|Then the doctors declared I had one month to live.}
{align:justify|My hair turned wholly white from drugs and chemotherapy. Then chemo could continue no more—I was too weak to bear it. For chemo kills cancer cells by half, healthy cells by half. And in my condition, further chemicals were impossible. Too weak to walk or stand, a few sentences left me dizzy, near fainting. I could neither speak nor muster any strength.}
{align:justify|Then my child told me Master The Hue was holding a Buddha-recitation retreat, rumored powerfully blessed… so I wished to try attending… But my husband, mother-in-law, and birth mother all forbade it. Above all my birth mother, who dotes on me, firmly declared she would rather I lie home doctored till death than walk so superstitious a road!… She said if I recklessly went, in such failing health I might… lay my bones upon the train before arriving!}
{align:justify|Then my son pleaded hard, telling my mother:}
{align:justify|"Grandma, stop barring Mother—you love your daughter, and I love my mother too?! If Mother must lie home awaiting death… better grant her wish, let her come to the Buddha's teachings and test it once?…" }
{align:justify|Then the doctors told my whole family:}
{align:justify|"Her life has one month left—let her eat whatever she likes…"}
{align:justify|Mother barred my going, wanting in this final time to pour out all her love for her daughter… When Mother asked to wash my feet, I truly could not bear it—but to please her I let her have her way… (not wanting her haunted after my death)—so I stretched my feet out for her washing… As Mother washed my feet, my tears streamed…}
{align:justify|When Mother agreed to let me go, my son (then a graduate student at a research institute) asked leave from school to escort me to the retreat.}
{align:justify|The retreat was held at Bao Lam Zen Monastery in Cam Chau (Liaoning); then I could not walk steadily—my son and a woman friend from Heilongjiang half-carried me there.}
{align:justify|At the retreat, daily I must bow to the Buddha a thousand times, practicing wholly Buddha-recitation, Buddha-bowing… And miracles appeared.}
{align:justify|At the retreat the master taught me: "Reciting the Buddha's name, think of nothing—mind un scattered—only mindfully recite the Buddha's name"…}
{align:justify|Then the master taught me to bow; I received the Eight Precepts, practicing with the assembly. Then within three to five days, joyfully I coughed no more. Seeing my cough cease, the master set me bowing—a thousand bows daily—yet in my condition I could hardly stand, let alone bow? I thought "Why does the master still make me bow?"… so I wept, for unable to bow, I burst into tears…}
{align:justify|But the master never softened—he firmly forced me to bow! Unable or not, bow I must! Meaning bow I could or not, bow I must!}
{align:justify|A week of bowing, and the master began circling the practice hall swiftly—I must "run" after him. With lung disease, my lungs choked with impurities, unventilated. Breathless, voiceless—yet I must "run," easily suffocated dead… I dared not run, but the master encouraged me; I tried, stepping swiftly—oh, gradually I felt ease—so confidently I strode after the master; up and down, my heart glad.}
{align:justify|Then the retreat's second week: climb the mountain. The master guided me. Seeing him climb, I dared not follow, fearing death. For I knew my strength could hardly bear it—but the master came, took my arm, and said:}
{align:justify|"Trieu Le Binh, strive up the mountain!"}
{align:justify|I thought "Obey the master"—whatever he teaches, I follow—so I strained up. The mountain high, slopes steep, all path incline… I crawled step by step; reaching the summit, tears of joy covered my face. Standing atop, I thought: "Truly beyond imagining—never dreaming I could climb and stand upon the summit"…}
{align:justify|Gazing at the boundless blue sky, green trees, I was infinitely moved—for the master's retreat had saved and granted me a second life…}
{align:justify|Afterward I attended retreat after retreat the master held, down to today. Then, two parts of my hair had whitened, one part ashen gray. But single-mindedly I followed the master in retreat. Now when fellow practitioners meet me again, they exclaim:}
{align:justify|"Oh! Le Binh's hair is black again—wonderful!"}
{align:justify|They stand before me admiring, praising on and on. I am truly glad.}
{align:justify|Before, at home each meal I must eat porridge. So coming to the retreat I worried—here all eat rice. I thought "my stomach so gravely ill—how eat rice?"… But after attending, now my habits differ nothing from others'—I eat easily, and my face glows rosy, truly!}
{align:justify|Now, circumambulating with all, reciting the Buddha's name, I long to recite loudly. Before, newly attending, I recited breathlessly, voiceless—now I can voice it, loudly…}
{align:justify|As for other ills—uterine tumors, ovarian, and the rest… now I never seek exams or checks—I know myself they have settled. Why?}
{align:justify|Past thirty I developed cancer; menstruation ceased three years… Before, doctors warned me: "Should bleeding recur, count yourself done for!" I asked:}
{align:justify|"Why?"}
{align:justify|He answered:}
{align:justify|"Your uterus is full of tumors, crowded and crushing—rupture means hemorrhage, then death! Both ovaries tumorous besides—most grave…"}
{align:justify|After attending the master's retreats, then further ones, suddenly in this time my belly ached fiercely, violently…}
{align:justify|The retreat done, the master returned to Taiwan. I returned home to Bao Thanh County, thinking: "If the master holds another retreat, I shall attend"…}
{align:justify|Home again, suddenly my belly burned extremely hot. As if electric currents roasted and spread heat through my lower belly, with some pain. I wondered: "Why so strange?" Thinking my plight might match the doctors' warning—"life… done for, great hemorrhage likely"… I rushed to the toilet—suddenly below a loud "splash," something gushing from my belly… then ease and comfort… Rising, I looked into the pan: all red, with a round tumor the size of a small bowl, flat-surfaced, ringed like a six-petaled flower.}
{align:justify|Afterward, not knowing whether well or ill, I went for ultrasound: "Whatever has happened?"…}
{align:justify|Examined, the doctor said:}
{align:justify|"Your illness is healed—nothing wrong."}
{align:justify|Since then, I recovered like a normal person. Only lung disease remains—but I firmly seek no exams or cures, thinking: "My hair is black again; though not wholly healed, my strength is excellent—healthy as this suffices."}
{align:justify|Now I can work and live normally like everyone.}
{align:justify|Through my fierce illness, father-in-law, mother-in-law, birth mother, youngest uncle and aunt, younger brother and sister… every relative witnessing my passage from gasping sickness to hale health—now able to do anything—all exclaim: "The Buddha's teachings are truly wondrous, inconceivable!"}
{align:justify|Nor merely praise—all together now vegetarianism and Buddha-recitation. My father-in-law, eighty-one this year—before 1949 an old revolutionary, meritorious, medaled—believed only in Chairman Mao, nothing else. But now all alike: turned to vegetarianism and Buddha-recitation, cultivating vigorously.}
{align:justify|Second Uncle (my husband's brother) once owned Bao Thanh's famed eatery. A grand renowned restaurant, known to all. Daily killing vast quantities—someone always booking at least ten tables—so the shop must kill every fresh seafood: fish, shrimp, crabs, sea cucumbers… meaning countless killings beyond count… and great profits.}
{align:justify|But through my violent illness, Second Uncle and wife firmly quit the trade. They moved to Thong Hoa Street, laboring for others, utterly abandoning killing.}
{align:justify|Witnessing my plight, not only my family turned to vegetarianism and Buddha-recitation—friends throughout Bao Thanh County all deeply believe in Buddhism.}
{align:justify|True practice is such. I am especially grateful to the master; since attending his retreats, I have gained immense benefit. Not only great bodily gain, but my mind ascended higher. I learned many fine things from my kind teacher. His retreats not only saved my life, but helped me follow his lofty virtue (I speak truly—for I am no good person!). At first, hearing people say:}
{align:justify|"Old Master The Hue has come from Taiwan to the Mainland to hold retreats"…}
{align:justify|Then, unbelieving, ignorant of Buddhism, I was most arrogant. Knowing nothing of him, still I slandered and gossiped… With atheist prejudice I measured and imagined ill, freely criticizing and sneering… I jeered:}
{align:justify|"This monk is bad in every way… nothing good about him!…"}
{align:justify|Writing these lines now, I repent deeply, longing to cry aloud: "Master! Your disciple sincerely repents to you!"…}
{align:justify|Not till gravely ill at death's door did I wish to attend a retreat—having heard its blessings strong and efficacious! (I went as a gamble).}
{align:justify|My evil deeds—killing, abortion, slander, backbiting him…—naturally the master knew all, knew full well, yet ever tended with kindness a great evil, great killer, great slanderer… like me! Wherever going, whatever eating, he saved portions for me, explaining: Trieu Le Binh is sick and weak—bring her some…}
{align:justify|At the retreat he saved my life teaching stern yet all-embracing… and an arrogant atheist like me was moved and humbled by his supreme virtue… The kinder the master, the more favored, the more remorseful and ashamed I felt, the more admiring and grateful…}
{align:justify|Since then, sincerely attending his retreats continuously for nearly three years. The master has held more than fifty retreats; throughout, he was good not to me alone but to all… So I deeply revere him. Healed, still I wish to attend his retreats—to learn his virtue.}
{align:justify|At Mount Wutai, I met a girl of sixteen from Tay An, afflicted so that eating meant vomiting. Once most beautiful, lovely, refined… Since illness, her weight ballooned—from forty-five kilos to eighty-five—puffy and bloated. She sought masters everywhere; an orphan without parents, most pitiable.}
{align:justify|The master allowed her to follow him. Then I thought: "Master, why do this—this child bears a grave illness; keeping her beside you, should she not heal, your good name suffers!" Then, caring for the master, I thought so—but the master cared nothing for this.}
{align:justify|He told me: Le Binh! The child is pitiable! Treat her as your own, tend and care for her!}
{align:justify|The child single-mindedly followed the master's retreats all the way to Mount Wutai. Afterward she followed no more—her illness gone. She had to go home to work.}
{align:justify|All along following the master, I learned many precious lessons from his virtue—lofty qualities never once witnessed before. So healed, still I attend the master's retreats, longing to learn from him. In my heart he is a supremely virtuous high monk, a most great spiritual teacher in this world.}
{align:justify|He won my admiration, moving me to tears. The more retreats with the master, the more his virtue shone—for in this mundane life I never met anyone of such style; meeting a true exemplary cultivator like him, I learned what the Buddha Jewel, Dharma Jewel, and Sangha Jewel are…}
{align:justify|My father was a postgraduate lecturer, my mother a university professor. Not only did I fully absorb my parents' learning, I graduated with high degrees—so all I met were highest intellectuals. My youngest son studies at the Wuhan Agricultural and Forestry University's research institute… One might say all my life I mixed only with high intellectual circles—those I esteemed. For they say: "Of all trades the lowest, only learning ranks highest." So the ancients declared. So in my heart I ever aspired to this creed, watching the "highest learned circles" to see whether they truly bore the fine graceful character the ancient sages ever praised? But frankly, after meeting the master, I found: the master towers luminous, far surpassing the highest intellectuals!}
{align:justify|Sometimes, circumambulating the Buddha, I silently confessed:}
{align:justify|– O World-Honored One! Now I understand and thoroughly feel the line: "Thập phương thế giới ngã tận kiến, nhất thiết vô hữu như Phật giả!" (Surveying all worlds of the ten directions, I see none as wondrous as the Buddha!). The Buddhas are truly compassionate and most great!}
{align:justify|Trieu Le Binh}
{align:justify|THE EARLY-ARRIVING GUEST}
{align:justify|Tomorrow is Tomb-Sweeping Day; since early spring I have bustled without rest, strength utterly spent. This afternoon, teaching the Dharma to Buddhist friends, I grew so weak my voice failed—truly I crave rest, but the dream seems out of reach. Tonight, sleepless, I decided to rise and recite the early-morning liturgy.}
{align:justify|As always, I drew the curtain, opened the window… one look outside and my limbs went weak with fright: though still deep night, the northeastern sky glows brighter than cities'. Unprepared, I suddenly beheld towering before me a female ghost, long hair loose, feet off the ground (her face unseen, wholly veiled by hair). Ordinarily I would not fear—but today, so sudden, I gasped, mouth agape in shock.}
{align:justify|Composing myself, I asked the precept-protecting spirits what this was; they explained:}
{align:justify|"This girl often comes here, quite long now—never discovered till today; perhaps affinities link you."}
{align:justify|Since daily we recite sutras dedicating to all beings, perhaps the ghost came to hear the early liturgy?}
{align:justify|The spirits told me: to commune with this ghost, please settle your mind and speak heart to heart with her.}
{align:justify|So I settled, closed my eyes, communing heart to heart… I asked the ghost in thought:}
{align:justify|"Why appear and startle me?"}
{align:justify|Scarcely asked, the ghost burst into tears; understanding nothing, not knowing how to stop her weeping, I reached to touch and soothe her. Never having touched a ghost-realm being, the touch met hollow emptiness—I marveled, dazed, not knowing how to comfort her.}
{align:justify|Seeing my discomfiture, the ghost had to laugh, saying: I know your name, and know you ever recite sutras praying for all beings. I have listened a month now; today I meant to arrive a little early—never dreaming you would rise earlier still and discover me! Exhausted as you are, daily laboring to recite sutras praying for us—deepest thanks!…}
{align:justify|Pausing, the ghost told me:}
{align:justify|"Please recite 'Nam Mô Diệu Sắc Thân Như Lai' and at once my form shall turn fair, frightening you no more. For fearing my ghostly shape would sow terror, I meant my long hair to veil my face… please also keep your distance from me, for our kind bears heavy yin energy that would harm you—what I wholly wish not…"}
{align:justify|I did as she asked, palms joined, sincerely reciting "Nam Mô Diệu sắc Thân Như Lai" ten times. A miracle appeared: the ghost turned beautiful, hair neatly pinned—I could see her face clearly: most lovely.}
{align:justify|The ghost begged me hereafter to recite many Buddhas' names—it greatly benefits beings. Then she told me she was born locally, dying at nineteen leaping from a building. She said she is now a lonely wronged soul—suicide barring rebirth—abiding in the yin realm suffering, a ghost drifting vagrant through the human world.}
{align:justify|I asked: Why did you not fall into hell?}
{align:justify|She answered:}
{align:justify|"My evil karma deserved not hell. Yet though I went not to hell, the torments I taste differ little from hell. For daily, exactly at the hour I killed myself, I must replay it identically—meaning I must keep standing atop the building, leaping to suicide, tasting death's utmost agony. Ten years now I have performed this leap—every day replaying suicide so, misery past telling—besides a wretched ghostly existence contrary to all wishes, vagrant hunger and thirst, a hundred million times worse than being human. Had I known, I would never have killed myself!"}
{align:justify|The ghost told how ten years before, after her parents divorced, the court placed her with her mother. But her mother remarried—so she gained a stepfather. Then the depraved stepfather raped her; she told her birth mother, who not only disbelieved but drove her from the house. So in fury she leapt from her own building to suicide… She even gave her home's exact address, saying if I disbelieved I might investigate and verify!}
{align:justify|She said:}
{align:justify|"Is not your disciple's house near mine? Only eleven doors from my mother's."}
{align:justify|(That afternoon I sent a disciple to investigate—indeed her mother's house was found; neighbors confirmed it all true).}
{align:justify|The ghost further told how she avenged herself on mother and stepfather—giving her stepfather a malignant cancer, forcing her mother's divorce. Weeping as she told it, she lamented: why, appearing in her mother's dreams begging sutra recitation, did her mother disbelieve?}
{align:justify|She told me she set wild ghosts to torment her mother—body uneasy, day and night wretched… she wants her mother to believe in cause and effect, repent her own sins, and hereafter escape misery…}
{align:justify|I was moved by her trust, thanking her for the truth. My heart ached to see her so pitiable. Barely nineteen—why so miserable…}
{align:justify|I urged her to let go of resentment and promised to send a disciple to find her mother. Explaining my poor health, I wished to rest at home. I would have a disciple contact her mother—for strangers to her, telling plainly her plight and wishes, surely her mother would understand, believe, and see cause and effect clearly.}
{align:justify|Hearing it, she rejoiced, adding that she has listened to my Ksitigarbha Sutra recitation a month now—in six months more she shall be born into heaven, to a most exquisite place…}
{align:justify|She added: people of this world are as foolish now as I once was! Granted human bodies, most never cherish them. So you must write it out, picturing my misery plainly for all to know—urging them never to suicide, to understand suicide's retribution, dreadful and agonizing past imagining! A human body is most rare, yet unknown—if deludedly abandoning it and sinking into ghosthood, misery multiplies ten thousandfold! This torment is past telling. Luckily, through your and others' Ksitigarbha Sutra recitations dedicated to lonely wronged souls, I have the blessed fortune to listen—and future heaven…}
{align:justify|Finished, she told me:}
{align:justify|"The cock crows—I must go at once, for I cannot face the sun!"}
{align:justify|The ghost gone, my heart grew heavy, thinking: "People today believe only what eyes see—wholly unaware that bodily ills come from karmic creditors seeking revenge… Daily they enter hospitals, never knowing at home to recite sutras, release captive animals, repent, sincerely regret… purifying body, speech, mind, turning good, redeeming committed sins… And even practicing, after a few rounds with no visible effect, they hastily conclude: the Buddha deceives—then abandon all practice! Why will they not consider their own countless lifetimes' sins? People habitually grasp in, never give out!"…}
{align:justify|Now I am most weary of communing. For plain-spoken, I easily offend many. But do you know? Few today speak true words!}
{align:justify|I write with no other intent—only saying: constructive words perfecting your character deserve patient hearing and heed. Tomorrow is Tomb-Sweeping Day; people will burn paper money along roads in plenty—but I feel no joy! Do you know why? I think: instead of burning paper, recite many Ksitigarbha Sutras dedicated to the departed—then ancestors and kin gain great benefit! If you can reform yourselves—vegetarianism, doing good, releasing captive animals, giving, reciting sutras… dedicating the merit to kin of many lives, dissolving karma, delivering all departed kin—this is true grave unfilial transgression!}
{align:justify|Writing thus, suddenly I heard the old man downstairs had died—his children showing "filial piety" by hiring a whole band: drums and instruments blaring, old songs and new…}
{align:justify|My eyes suddenly moistened: 'Heavens, when shall people understand and practice true filial piety?" It seems tonight's blaring band shall harass me and the whole block all night!}
{align:justify|Chan Ngu}
{align:justify|Translator's note: Once, hearing Master Hsuan Hua teach that suicides must daily replay their suicide, I doubted, disbelieving—but now this story sets me pondering…. Moreover, the sutras say: "The Buddhas' names bear wondrous supreme power, drawn by Their perfect practice-merit and great vows." We in the human realm, wishing to verify, each knows personally—unwidespread, little understood.}
{align:justify|Translating to where the ghost asked to recite "Nam Mô Diệu Sắc Thân Như Lai"—one of seven Buddhas named in the food-bestowal liturgy for the departed—her face at once turned fair—proving the ancient worthies compiling this liturgy understood the holy names' mystery: in the human realm we see no transformation so plain and swift as in the ghost realm—meaning when repentance assemblies are held, invisible beings come hearing sutras, giving rise to joy, wholesomeness, thereby transforming fair in form; hungry ghosts are fed, even reborn at once into good destinies, turning evil minds to good. So ancient sages often raised repentance platforms praying for the living above, the dead below, on shores and waters…}
{align:justify|But others praying for you never equals practicing yourself! The sutras explain: if others pray or create blessings for you, the practitioner enjoys six shares of blessing, the dedicatee but one—and scriptures ever remind: each should practice themselves to reap practice's full fruit.}
{align:justify|The "Ksitigarbha Sutra" is confirmed as a sutra swiftly dissolving sins and karma, swiftly growing merit—through Ksitigarbha's great vows' might. Yet some gravely ill, releasing captive animals, doing good, reciting up to 108 rounds, still saw no result. Asked, Quả Khanh explained: "The reciter still eats meat. So meat-eaters' recitation shall not work! Abstain from the five pungents, keep vegetarianism and precepts, guard body, speech, mind pure, and recite with settled single-minded mind—results come swiftly." Readers may test and verify this themselves.}
{align:justify|WHERE SUICIDES GO}
{align:justify|I am a lifelong-vegetarian layperson keeping the Buddha's five precepts. I deeply admire Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's vow: "Until the hells are empty, I vow not to become a Buddha!" In the "Ksitigarbha Sutra" the first chapter tells how the Saintly Maiden, reciting the Buddha's name day and night, saw herself descend into hell, learning where her mother was reborn…}
{align:justify|I love and find true comfort reading this passage:}
{align:justify|"If one can, for twenty-one days, single-mindedly gaze in worship at Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's image and recite His name ten thousand times complete, the Bodhisattva shall manifest His boundless body, telling that person which realm their kin were born into. Or in dreams, the Bodhisattva manifests great spiritual power, personally leading that person to worlds to meet their kin."}
{align:justify|Whenever papers and television report suicides thick as flies, I grieve boundlessly. So I thirst to know: the dead by suicide—where do they go?}
{align:justify|In the Ksitigarbha Sutra, chapter twelve, it is recorded:}
{align:justify|"Those who hear Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's name, behold His image, and sincerely take refuge—able daily to recite the Bodhisattva's name a thousand times, for a thousand days running—their good men and women's prayers shall swiftly succeed, forever unhindered."}
{align:justify|So I practiced abundantly—more than the sutra teaches: long-term recitation, reforming each thought, earnestly bowing to Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva over a long time—wishing but one thing: "Grant me wisdom opened, to see and thoroughly understand where suicides go?"}
{align:justify|At last the wish came true. In dreams, I truly visited the realm where suicides dwell, understanding all questions. (The sutras speak of six realms: gods, humans, asuras, hells, hungry ghosts, animals. So describing the Ghost realm, I hope you are not startled.) A few directly see, but most do not. (Yet the near-death, their energies failing, minds weak, easily see those of the yin realm.)}
{align:justify|Now I invite Dharma friends to view and share this precious experience with me:}
{align:justify|"The yin realm's energy is most cold—so contacting or visiting it needs strong meditative power, great vows, a clear spirit. The Ghost realm too stretches boundless, divided into many zones. Never think suicide ends misery—it opens the curtain upon sky-high torment. Whether for love, for debts, for whatever reasons… you would leap from buildings, drown in rivers, drink poison, slit veins, cut throats, hang… thoughts of suicide… in every way… but once suicide, after death the torments received grow more dreadful still. Suicides dead, their conscious spirits are at once imprisoned, sunk in endless misery.}
{align:justify|Know that suicide bears three grave offenses:}
{align:justify|Unfilial to both parents.}
{align:justify|Destroying one's own form, betraying the rare blessing of human birth, not cherishing life.}
{align:justify|Foolish and witless.}
{align:justify|Our bodies come from our parents; living, never repaying them with utmost filial piety, never working to benefit life and people—yet foolishly destroying, killing one's own body. This is a most grave offense. Know: a human body is hard to gain, the Buddha's teachings hard to hear. This life granted human form—why despise and destroy it? For abandoning the human body, regaining one shall be most hard. Suicide is utter folly.}
{align:justify|Suppose debts drive you to suicide—understand: this life's debts unpaid, hereafter you repay still more—now shouldering besides suicide's great offense—misery heaped on misery! Know: suicides dead suffer piteous karmic retribution. Suicides after death shall endlessly replay the suicidal act.}
{align:justify|Suppose Mr. A leaps to suicide at two in the afternoon—dead, his conscious spirit is imprisoned suffering, lingering about the suicide's ground. Daily, exactly at the suicide's hour, he returns to that very place, that building… performing suicide once. Every day performing so, performing the self-destroying act until his lifespan ends. Suppose his fated age was seventy, but at forty he suicided—then dead, daily he must replay this suicide scene—meaning he must… leap from the building for thirty years, till his worldly lifespan exhausts.}
{align:justify|Moreover daily, that suicidal moment's excruciating thoughts, added to bodily pain, replay all together—exactly the same. Meaning daily he must re-enact and re-experience the suicidal tragedy once—outside which hours he is locked in the "Suicide Offenders' Zone" enduring punishment. This "suicide dead" dwelling is extraordinarily gloomy, most damp and foul. Here every heart brims with resentment—moans, weeping, laments unceasing… Here are no houses or beds, no television, feasts, no food… nothing at all! Only torment and weeping.}
{align:justify|I approached a cell holding suicides packed full, resentment soaring sky-high, most oppressive—every face most pitiful. I neared a suicide ghost, seeing his whole body soaked, eyes sunken deep. I greeted:}
{align:justify|"Are you well?"}
{align:justify|He tilted his head eyeing me, asking:}
{align:justify|"Who are you?"}
{align:justify|"I am an ordinary person, vowing to visit the yin realm to learn how suicides fare after death?"}
{align:justify|He said:}
{align:justify|"Why ask—one look tells you my utter misery! I know not how to voice my present heart—only saying I am… MISERABLE! SO MISERABLE!… and that is all!"}
{align:justify|"Why is your whole body soaked?"}
{align:justify|"I drowned myself in a river…"}
{align:justify|"But why did you suicide?"}
{align:justify|"I will not tell—ask no more!"}
{align:justify|"Very well! Farewell!"}
{align:justify|I walked elsewhere. Before me a woman—I asked:}
{align:justify|"Why are you in this place?"}
{align:justify|She answered:}
{align:justify|"Can you help me find a small child? I beg you."}
{align:justify|I asked:}
{align:justify|"Your child?"}
{align:justify|"Yes. I took my daughter suiciding with me—but now I know not where she is? For after suiciding I was at once escorted here, while my child vanished without trace. I suffer greatly…"}
{align:justify|"Your daughter now dwells in another zone, awaiting rebirth. For you willfully ended the young child's life wholly without her consent—so your offense is most grave. Since your child was small, innocent, knowing nothing, hers counts not as suicide—so she was taken elsewhere."}
{align:justify|"Truly? None told me this—I beg you, lead me to meet her, will you?"}
{align:justify|I cannot lead you there—for this is the yin realm, with the yin realm's own laws. You must bear suicide's retribution here—that is the law!}
{align:justify|She burst into bitter weeping…}
{align:justify|I walked on a stretch, crossing a mountain, reaching another place. Here another block confining other suicide dead. I met a man with a long deep gash across his belly, blood streaming… I asked:}
{align:justify|"Sir, what happened to your belly?"}
{align:justify|He answered:}
{align:justify|"This wound is from my own knife slicing my belly—for my wife cheated, following another man… so I hate 'her'!"}
{align:justify|"Even so, you should not have killed yourself!"}
{align:justify|"First I killed her, then suicided! Dead, I am locked here. Daily I must return home replaying the suicide scene. I ache and suffer past telling…"}
{align:justify|I sighed:}
{align:justify|"Ah! For love again! Sir, suicide is a most grave offense—can you recite the Buddha's name?"}
{align:justify|Then I taught him to recite: "Nam mô Địa Tạng Vương Bồ tát!" adding:}
{align:justify|"You must recite—it shall lighten karmic retribution! Moreover, repent—for killing was utterly wrong; having killed and suicided besides, your offenses grow heavier! Hear my urging—strive to recite the Buddha's name!"}
{align:justify|He only nodded, saying nothing.}
{align:justify|Before my eyes appeared ghost after ghost, hearts full of hateful resentment, most dreadful—some drenched head to toe in blood flowing unceasing. Witnessing these scenes with my own eyes, I was utterly terrified.}
{align:justify|Worldly people mostly think suicide ends all torment—never knowing "suicide opens the curtain upon torment past imagining." Utter folly! No wonder places of suicide often tell of "hauntings" though none are seen.}
{align:justify|For if a house or room… once held a suicide, the suicide ghost must daily return performing the suicidal act—so if that house or room knew no "ghostly disturbance," that would be strange! So we often hear "suicide spots often echo weeping… though none are seen." It is the suicide dead returning to perform suicide, sunk in the self-destroying moment's grieving thoughts…}
{align:justify|For my part, though by Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva's vow-power I witnessed the yin realm—frankly, were I to lodge in a suicide's house or room, surely I would suffer deeply. Why? For daily my eyes would plainly see ghosts returning to replay suicide, plainly seeing how piteously they died. How then find peace and ease? The teachings hold most fine, wondrous deliverance sutras. But you cannot rely on another's deliverance while refusing to practice. If your heart brims still with evil, venomous thoughts… none can save you. For scenes accord with minds—good minds draw good scenes! Deliverance helps you give rise to goodness, repent and correct faults. Perfect yourself while living, relying on none—that is best.}
{align:justify|In human life, who lives without cares, without pain? Those guarding body, speech, mind wholesome keep hearts ever glad. Misery comes… from evil seeds we once sowed. Repaying evil fruits, settling old debts—who feels light and easy? Ten thousand times I beg you: never raise suicidal thoughts. Understand: suicide solves nothing—dead, torment grows worse! If earthly life seems misery, know that suicides dead are locked in the suicide-dead zone. Fools think death ends all—wipes cares, ends every sorrow… wholly unaware that after suicide, torment but begins descending—ten thousand times fiercer than living pain. And why not pity the kin who shall grieve so at your foolish act? Drive yourself not onto the dead-end road.}
{align:justify|Suicides dead, having borne suicide's retribution complete, must proceed to greater and lesser hells enduring further torment, as the Ksitigarbha Sutra describes… till karma exhausts they may escape and be reborn—but where, depends still on their created karma.}
{align:justify|Buddhist Thieu Hoa}
{align:justify|Translator's note: I weighed and pondered long—at last deciding to translate this piece, hoping to wake those bent on suicide.}
{align:justify|In life, however utter the misery, however hopeless, never wish for suicide. For present pain unbearable means post-death pain fiercer still! Remember: suicide does not end pain—it opens the curtain upon endless torment… For in this life all bear pain, not you alone—some bear pains still more towering, yet never flee but face them, striding bravely through to grow and strengthen… (for the abyss is the ladder of heroes). Though our bodies die, minds still work… Killing oneself too bears killing's sin: violating the ten evils, barring rebirth. The body dead, feelings of cold, heat, hunger, thirst, resentment… still cling, tormenting consciousness unceasing. Minds full of evil thoughts must fall, sinking… Minds full of loving kindness shall rise high. As when you give, release captive animals, or help someone—night brings deep sleep, hearts floating glad… that is the wholesome mind's realm. So robbers and murderers, doers of utter evil, surely never sleep peacefully, ever meeting nightmares, though law has not yet punished…}
{align:justify|When suicidal wishes arise, let the thought rise no further (nurse not vain grief—it will goad you to folly)—strive steering the mind to wholesome brightness. Truly no dead end or cul-de-sac exists—only your mind imagines so. "Sweet seeds sown reap sweet fruits." So the escape lies in your own mind—not in others or outward scenes. Open your own way out, drown not in deadlock. Joy and misery self-made—seeking joy, end evil, do good—ever guarding body, speech, mind wholesome: the good your mind holds, the peace you shall enjoy—here no debate argues it; to know true from false, only practice, experience, and draw your own answer.}
{align:justify|THE DRAGON-TIGER TAVERN}
{align:justify|Graduating high school, introduced by someone, I took work serving tables at a restaurant called the "Dragon-Tiger Tavern." The tavern stands in deep mountains thick with trees, teeming with wild beasts. The owner, Mr. Vi, counted a business king—shrewd, calculating investments—reaping much money; he hung a sign selling wild game, hawking "rustic delicacies"—diners flocked thick.}
{align:justify|The tavern kept expanding. Old Vi's shop grew fiercer, famed far and near. The house specialty, "Dragon-Tiger Duel"—great python flesh cooked with wildcat—became the tavern's namesake.}
{align:justify|So wildcats and pythons throughout neighboring lands met calamity. As Boss Vi always paid above others, many money-hungry villagers risked their lives braving peril, creeping into desolate unpeopled mountains seeking goods to supply the boss. The back courtyard daily reeked of blood, piteous wails echoing unceasing in our ears.}
{align:justify|One day old Vi bought a monstrous six-meter python, some inch-and-a-half thick, scales shimmering gold tinged silver. They say many died hunting this python; from its massive build one could guess the python king.}
{align:justify|The python lay senseless a day before waking. Old Vi neared the iron cage saying:}
{align:justify|"Ha! You look stately and majestic—so I shall not kill you yet, granting you a few more days' life."}
{align:justify|The python seemed to understand—rearing its head high, suddenly gaping wide, it spat a mouthful of reeking foul saliva into the boss's face; unprepared, he could only shriek piteously, then turn and flee. Hearing his roar we rushed over, washing his face with clean water. Python saliva corrodes and destroys strongly—rescue too late, and scars and sores surely remain.}
{align:justify|Old Vi blazed with rage, seizing a pot of boiling water to dash into the python's pen; vented, he snarled:}
{align:justify|"Were it not for my high purchase price, I would hack you to pieces for satisfaction! Humph! Now I shall make you live worse than death—half a month hence I shall butcher you, soaking you in wine to toast my fortieth birthday!"}
{align:justify|From that day the python was locked solitary in a great cage in the garden corner—no food or drink; sometimes the boss abused, tormented, and loudly cursed it—daily slaughtering its kind before it, flaying and carving animals in its sight to cow it.}
{align:justify|Seven, eight days passed—the great python never submitted; seeing the boss, eyes blazing wrathful, mouth gaping fangs bared. Nearing its cage, all felt dreadful unease.}
{align:justify|More days passed—suddenly something happened: the great caged python vanished wingless, traceless, its whereabouts… unknown. Hearing the news, the boss paled, eyes rolling back; he came to the cage staring, observing… The cage stood wholly intact, not a scratch of damage, no gaps around. He guessed: "Unless someone deliberately freed it? Else this monstrous python surely could never escape"…}
{align:justify|The boss stormed off, snarling:}
{align:justify|"WHO? WHO FREED IT, HUH? Confess sensibly and nothing happens—but let me find out and I shall flay you alive… just watch!"}
{align:justify|We all trembled with fear, swearing together we had done nothing. The boss, wretched-faced, returned to the iron cage observing on—at last discovering the great python's escape: two iron bars gapped rather wide—the python plainly escaped that way.}
{align:justify|We discussed all morning—at last admitting the fact before our eyes: this python's body stretches and shifts most elastically, supreme at slithering; so tiny a gap, yet it slipped through. For freedom, it could endure torment past imagining…}
{align:justify|The boss, having "vented his spleen"—his fury discharged—seemed calmed, saying nothing more. Past two in the morning, all sleeping soundly, terrified screams woke us. I started up, dashing to the courtyard to see.}
{align:justify|One look, and my soul fled. In bright moonlight, I saw dozens of pythons filling the courtyard, some slithering in through windows. Never had I witnessed pythons acting in concert. Usually the tavern's pythons seemed dull-witted—but now before my eyes, these great pythons looked most cunning, blazing with unquenchable wrath, their might fiercely menacing…}
{align:justify|I hurriedly shouted the alarm all around:}
{align:justify|"Wake up, wake up and run—the pythons are crawling into the house!"}
{align:justify|My terrified cries roused the whole area. In a blink, several snakes spread all around, many slithering to besiege the boss's room—sealed without a crack.}
{align:justify|In the tavern, chefs and kitchen hands, adept hands—having killed countless pythons, skilled and practiced—felt little horror; a flicker of fear passing, they quickly seized implements hunting pythons to kill. After a fierce battle, several people left the house, running to the courtyard. The pythons attacked at leisure—several charging straight for the boss's room, as if reinforcing.}
{align:justify|Plainly the pythons' attack centered on the boss's room. Windows open, several had entered within. I easily recognized the escaped python king—golden, largest of the pack, easiest to spot—and understood at once: it led and commanded this pack in revenge!}
{align:justify|Strangely, nowhere did I see the boss, nor hear clashing combat…}
{align:justify|I called loudly, warning those still standing in the courtyard:}
{align:justify|"Hurry upstairs—safer there!"}
{align:justify|As if waking, they dashed for the stairs—all running for their lives, rushing into rooms like whirlwinds. The pythons began climbing—we joined hearts and strength, holding the "high beats low" advantage—at last driving them back.}
{align:justify|As the pack withdrew from the boss's room, still the great golden king led proudly ahead—then swiftly they vanished into the dense woods behind the mountain.}
{align:justify|Certain danger had passed, we descended. I saw the two butchers Nhan Trung Vi and Ly Truong Huu had still not left their room. These two daily slaughtered snakes—countless pythons killed. Mouths agape in horror, we found both men's sinews severed, entrails torn through, blood streaming from all seven orifices—dead long since.}
{align:justify|The waitresses' room windows, shut tight, the pythons could not enter—so none were hurt—only scared "souls fled"!}
{align:justify|But where was Boss Vi? Wondering, we moved together to his door. Outside, chaos—yet no sign of him. Anxious, we pressed within—greater chaos, everywhere the pythons' trampling traces. Besides, a reeking stench lingered.}
{align:justify|Strangely, still no old Vi. "Could the pythons have kidnapped him, swallowed him whole? Dreadful"… Thinking it, my head numbed, spine chilled, gooseflesh rising…}
{align:justify|Then, as one, we all noticed a great jar in the corner—about one and a half meters high, wooden lid with handle—but now the lid closed upside down, handle inward; the jar's sides printed with snakes' clinging, wrestling traces… Surely old Vi was inside?}
{align:justify|We called softly:}
{align:justify|"Boss, the pythons have withdrawn—come out quickly!"}
{align:justify|Calling on with no stir within, foreboding, we neared, trembling lifting the lid. We saw Mr. Vi's whole body curled, face pallid, eyes bulging, tongue protruding, ten fingers clawing the lid till bleeding—a death too piteous to behold.}
{align:justify|Likely old Vi, seeing the pack enter, knew he could not rush out—so dove into the jar, pulling the lid shut. The python king, hating him to the bone, would hardly release him? Brimming with utmost fury, it joined the pack ringing the jar, attacking with full force… The snakes pressed the lid tight, letting no air seep in—old Vi suffocated within.}
{align:justify|This unheard-of horror swiftly spread—throughout the region, the villages… And locals henceforth dared kill no snakes.}
{align:justify|Bao Quang}
{align:justify|THE SCIENTISTS' STORY}
{align:justify|This article's author is layman Tu Quang—his whole family outstanding scientists. His parents are two scientists, both working at the Chinese Academy of Sciences. After studying Buddhism, the author admired it and took refuge in the Three Jewels. His filial piety and sincere Buddhist practice saved his mother even as one foot stepped through the ghost gate. Thus his parents turned their hearts, together stepping onto the Buddhist path. This story is entirely true and most trustworthy. Here is his self-account:}
{align:justify|In February 1989 I took refuge at Nga Long Temple in Tay An under Great Master Tri Chan. In 1991 I took a post teaching at a Beijing graduate school. More than twenty years now, I have taught and trained people, researching science while practicing—though not diligent enough, in life and work I have enjoyed profound benefits.}
{align:justify|Before, tales of boundless responses to learning Buddhism all came from fellow practitioners' telling. But later I lived through one myself. This true story arose in my own house—strange and marvelous past measure. To repay the Buddhas' and Bodhisattvas' kindness, I recount it here, hoping Buddhists may grow in right, steadfast faith.}
{align:justify|Early 2008, my mother's heart disease and high blood pressure grew grave—within half a year, four times her racing heart suddenly stopped and she fainted unconscious.}
{align:justify|Once my father went marketing—leaving my mother barely half an hour—returning to find her fallen on the ground, head gashed and bleeding… afterward though she woke, she lacked strength to crawl up. Taking Mother to great hospitals, she had no strength to walk. Examining, doctors saw her heart racing, oft palpitating, panicked—pulse blocked, beating erratically.}
{align:justify|The doctors said: operating at eighty-five endangers greatly—unless utterly failing, avoid surgery—so only drugs could soothe the illness.}
{align:justify|By 2009, my mother fell ill every day or two—nearly whole nights unsleeping. Doctors gave heart and blood-pressure drugs—all vain. So grieved I watching Mother's treatment fail. When weather shifted, her illness worsened. Even as the forecast merely announced cold reaching Tan Cuong, my mother already felt unwell. Her body failed to the utmost. Nights her heart often stopped or beat wildly—leaving her palpitating, panicked…}
{align:justify|The last Saturday of May 2009, I visited my parents at Trung Quan Village. My father said:}
{align:justify|"This summer heat, scorching so—likely your mother shall not survive."}
{align:justify|Unable to sit watching Mother await death, in the crisis I thought of the Buddha's teachings, wishing secretly to create merit for her. Then I had just memorized the "Diamond Sutra." Knowing its merit wondrous past thought, I resolved to recite the "Diamond Sutra," dedicating its merit to her, praying the Buddhas and Bodhisattvas bless Mother's turn for the better.}
{align:justify|So Sunday I began reciting—daily at five in the morning I rose to recite one "Diamond Sutra" round—recited, then dedicating two vows:}

{align:justify|"May my mother's karmic obstructions dissolve, her blessings and wisdom grow, may she enjoy health and long life, give rise to faith and vow to recite the Buddha's name, and after this body is done, be reborn in the Western Pure Land of Ultimate Bliss."}
{align:justify|I recited for one week, completing seven copies of the "Diamond Sutra." Since I recited at around five in the morning, fearing to disturb my family's sleep I only recited silently; the dedication verses too I intoned silently, each round clear in my mind, only never spoken aloud. But of this reciting of the "Diamond Sutra" dedicated to my mother, I breathed not a word to my mother or anyone.}
{align:justify|After a week I went to Zhongguancun to visit my parents. When I asked about my mother's illness, my father said excitedly:}
{align:justify|"How fortunate—all this week your mother's heart and blood pressure have been stable, so every day she sleeps wonderfully. Seven straight days without an attack—something that hasn't happened in nearly a year."}
{align:justify|Hearing this, I rejoiced inwardly, guessing quietly that my sutra recitation for my mother had taken effect… So I told everyone what had happened; all who heard were amazed, agreeing it was marvelous beyond measure!}
{align:justify|Only then did my mother reveal what she had hidden so long, and I was more shaken still. My mother confessed that before, fearing no one in the family would believe her, she had kept silent.}
{align:justify|As it happened, for nearly a year, every night her blood pressure soared, her heart labored terribly, she could not sleep, and worse, she kept seeing people walking back and forth inside the house. My mother stressed that she was not dreaming at all—fully awake, she still saw that crowd! Alarmed, I quickly asked:}
{align:justify|"What did they look like?"}
{align:justify|Mother answered:}
{align:justify|"Men and women, fairly old…"}
{align:justify|"And their appearance?"}
{align:justify|"Mostly I couldn't see their faces clearly. Not only that—at midnight I would also hear a woman weeping. Once, a big, brawny man with a ferocious face lunged at me and grabbed my arm; I wrestled free and escaped…"}
{align:justify|Mother explained:}
{align:justify|"Because I feared seeing those people, every night I dared not sleep facing outward. But even lying facing the wall, once—less than thirty centimeters before my face—there suddenly appeared a creature, old, ugly, unspeakably filthy and foul… I vomited violently, retching over and over, and only then did the monster vanish."}
{align:justify|Before 1949 my mother graduated from university; before retiring she worked at China's "Academy of Advanced Sciences" in Zhongguancun. As a senior intellectual with decades at the Academy of Sciences, she had never touched anything classed as superstition—naturally she could never accept phenomena (persons or things) of that realm; she never believed ghosts or spirits existed! Yet this time, perversely, she met ghosts face to face while still alive—and not by hallucination. Whether she liked it or not, my mother had to believe!}
{align:justify|I asked:}
{align:justify|"Why didn't you tell me sooner? Don't you know how dangerous and frightening this is? The frightening thing is, by then one of your feet had already stepped through the gates of ghosts!"}
{align:justify|I explained to my mother:}
{align:justify|"Among the six realms, the Human realm, the Animal realm, and the Ghost realm… all three share one space. We can see the animal realm, but the ghost realm is yin in nature, while ordinary people, their yang energy prevailing, cannot be approached by ghost-realm beings—hence we do not see them.}
{align:justify|But karmic creditors who died and now dwell in the Ghost realm lie ever in wait; when a person falls gravely ill or nears death, yin waxes and yang wanes, and they immediately draw close to collect their debts, tormenting and avenging themselves… So the very moment you saw ghost-realm beings, one foot had already stepped through the ghost gate. Had you told me everything sooner, I could have performed Buddhist rites to rescue you earlier…"}
{align:justify|Mother said:}
{align:justify|"But for about a week now I no longer see ghosts (it was from the very day I began reciting sutras and dedicating the merit to my mother and her karmic creditors that the ghosts vanished that very night and never came to trouble her for the whole week)."}
{align:justify|For the whole half year after, I single-mindedly recited the "Diamond Sutra" dedicated to my parents—about one hundred copies. From the summer Sunday of 2009 onward, my mother's heart condition and high blood pressure improved and changed for the better: each day she takes medicine once, the dose halved, less than usual.}
{align:justify|My parents are intellectuals, university graduates before 1949, both outstanding scientists at the Academy of Advanced Sciences—persuading them to believe in Buddhism was very hard.}
{align:justify|I have learned Buddhism for more than twenty years, tirelessly proclaiming the Buddha's teachings for my parents to understand, yet my father remained half-believing, half-doubting, while my mother flatly refused to believe.}
{align:justify|But ever since my recitation of the "Diamond Sutra" for my mother drew this marvelous response, my two headstrong scientists were shaken and moved—this time both were utterly convinced, and began to believe in Buddhism, resolving to recite the Buddha's name seeking rebirth in the West.}
{align:justify|My mother is eighty-eight this year. Every day, morning and evening, she spends an hour walking, reciting the Buddha's name as she walks, her spirit and health robust—as if a different person from half a year ago.}
{align:justify|My father is also eighty-eight; daily he practices walking meditation reciting the Buddha's name for over two hours. I even bought a Buddha-recitation machine, playing around the clock at home to help my parents gather their minds more strongly.}
{align:justify|My own miraculous response, judged even by science, still meets the conditions for proof:}
{align:justify|Clearly, throughout the week I recited the "Diamond Sutra" praying for my mother, I revealed this to neither my parents nor anyone, and while reciting I knew nothing at all of my mother's secret. So when everything was smoothly resolved, it cannot be concluded that it all came from my own "autosuggestion" or "hallucination." Hence I am fully convinced that the merit of the "Diamond Sutra" is truly inconceivably wondrous!}
{align:justify|A READER'S LETTER}
{align:justify|Dear Bhikkhuni, I wish to report a happy news so you may rejoice with me—and be cheered to know how immensely your translation work has served life and people.}
{align:justify|You surely remember that more than two years ago my left eye turned red and streamed tears, burning terribly. The eye doctor in America did his utmost yet could not cure it; only twenty percent of my vision remained—I was nearly totally blind. After reading of the Green and White Snakes in "The Flooding of Jinshan Temple" in your translated "Karmic Retribution in Present Life – Volume 3," a gleam of hope flashed… So I recited the Ksitigarbha Sutra and prostrated through about ten sets of the Liang Huang Repentance, and the eye disease gradually eased, then healed completely. It is now nearly three months. My late report comes from waiting to see whether I was truly healed—now it is certain the illness is fully cured; I see very clearly, even driving at night.}
{align:justify|The eye doctor who treated me is also a devout Buddhist; hearing my story, she hugged me in joy. Doctor and patient both shed tears.}
{align:justify|Once more, my deep gratitude to you, Bhikkhuni. I wish you good health.}
{align:justify|N.T – October 25, 2014}
{align:justify|Translator's note: Above is a letter from a female reader in the U.S.A., who once taught American college students and is now retired. I take the liberty of publishing it so readers may have verified proof and deepen their faith. (My apologies to Ms. N. T. for publishing without asking your permission. For the benefit of so many, I hope you will forgive me.)}
{align:justify|The translator – December 25, 2014}
